Let $\pi:E\to B$ be a principal $S^1$-bundle.  Analysis of the associated Serre spectral sequence gives the following Gysin long exact sequence:
\begin{equation}\label{gysinserregeneral}
\cdots\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-1}(B)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}H_\bullet(E)\xrightarrow{\pi_\ast}H_\bullet(B)\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-2}(B)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}\cdots
\end{equation}
The $\cap e$ map is cap product with the Euler class $e(E)\in H^2(B)$.  To see this, observe that the sequence \eqref{gysinserregeneral} coincides with the long exact sequence of the pair for (the mapping cone of) $\pi:E\to B$, and that there is a natural isomorphism $H_{\bullet+2}(B,E)\xrightarrow{\cap\tau}H_\bullet(B)$ where $\tau=\tau(E)\in H^2(B,E)$ is the Thom class (e.g.\ as argued in \cite[p444]{hatcher} for the corresponding sequence in cohomology).

For any $S^1$-space $X$. there is a principal $S^1$-bundle $\pi:X\times ES^1\to(X\times ES^1)/S^1$, and thus a long exact sequence:
\begin{equation}\label{gysinserre}
\cdots\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(X)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}H_\bullet(X)\xrightarrow{\pi_\ast}H_\bullet^{S^1}(X)\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-2}^{S^1}(X)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}\cdots
\end{equation}
for $e(X)\in H^2_{S^1}(X)$.  The same reasoning applies for pairs of spaces, so the same exact sequence exists for relative homology as well.

\label{SSimplicitatlasdef}
Let $X$ be an $S^1$-space.  An \emph{$S^1$-equivariant implicit atlas} $A$ on $X$ is an implicit atlas $A$ along with an action of $S^1$ on each thickening $X_I$ (commuting with the $\Gamma_I$-action) such that each map $\psi_{IJ}$ is $S^1$-equivariant, each function $s_\alpha$ is $S^1$-invariant, and each subset $X_I^\mathrm{reg}$ is $S^1$-invariant.

Similarly, we define an $S^1$-equivariant implicit atlas with boundary and/or stratification by in addition requiring that the boundary loci $\partial X_I$ and/or stratifications $X_I\to\mathcal S$ be $S^1$-invariant.

Let $X$ be an $S^1$-space with finite $S^1$-equivariant implicit atlas with boundary $A$.  For any $S^1$-invariant compact $K\subseteq X$, we define:
\begin{align}
\label{bigSSpartial}C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}\quad&C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}\\
\label{bigSS}C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)\phantom{_{IJ}}\quad&C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)
\end{align}
as in Definitions \ref{CSTcomplexdef}--\ref{Ccomplexdef}, except using $C_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}$ in place of $C_\bullet$ in \eqref{CcomplexcriticaldefI}--\eqref{CcomplexcriticaldefII}.  It is clear that \eqref{bigSSpartial}--\eqref{bigSS} are complexes of $\mathcal K$-presheaves on $X/S^1$ (replace $K$ with $\pi^{-1}(K)$, where $\pi:X\to X/S^1$).  The Gysin map \eqref{pishriek} induces ``comparison maps'':
\begin{equation}\label{CSStimesSS}
C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{(IJ)}\to C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)_{(IJ)}
\end{equation}
for all flavors \eqref{bigSSpartial}--\eqref{bigSS}

Analogously with \eqref{Erelpartialpullback}--\eqref{Epushforward1} and \eqref{Frelpartialpullback}--\eqref{Fpushforward}, there are natural maps (compatible with \eqref{CSStimesSS}):
\begin{align}
C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}\\
C^{d+\bullet}_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&\xrightarrow{s_\ast}C_{-\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;A)\\
C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{I',J'}\\
\label{CSSrelpartialpullback}C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)\\
\label{CSSpushforward}C^{d+\bullet}_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)&\xrightarrow{s_\ast}C_{-\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;A)
\end{align}
Analogously with \eqref{Epushforward1more}--\eqref{Fpushforward1}, there are natural maps (compatible with \eqref{CSStimesSS}):
\begin{align}
C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}\otimes C_{-\bullet}(E;A'\setminus A)&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A')_{I',J'}\\
C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)\otimes C_{-\bullet}(E;A'\setminus A)&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A')
\end{align}

An \emph{MF-set} is a set $\mathcal Y$ along with:
\begin{rlist}
\item For every $i\in\mathcal Y$, a PL manifold with cells $(M_i,\mathcal S_i)$.
\item(Face identifications). For every $i\in\mathcal Y$ and every $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_i$, an index $j\in\mathcal Y$ and an isomorphism $(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s})\xrightarrow\sim(M_j,\mathcal S_j)$.  These indices and isomorphisms must be (strictly) transitive in the following obvious sense.  If $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S$ is maximal, then $j=i$ and $(M_i,\mathcal S_i)=(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s})\xrightarrow\sim(M_j,\mathcal S_j)=(M_i,\mathcal S_i)$ is the identity map.  If $\mathfrak s'\preceq\mathfrak s$ is a nested pair of faces with identifications $(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s})\xrightarrow\sim(M_j,\mathcal S_j)$ and $(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s'},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s'})\xrightarrow\sim(M_{j'},\mathcal S_{j'})$, and the $\mathfrak s'$ face of $(M_j,\mathcal S_j)$ is identified $(M_j^{\leq\mathfrak s'},\mathcal S_j^{\leq\mathfrak s'})\xrightarrow\sim(M_k,\mathcal S_k)$, then $k=j'$ and the two identifications of $(M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s'},\mathcal S_i^{\leq\mathfrak s'})$ with $(M_k,\mathcal S_k)=(M_{j'},\mathcal S_{j'})$ are the same.
\end{rlist}
A morphism of MF-sets $f:\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Y'$ is a map of sets covered by isomorphisms $(M_i,\mathcal S_i)\to(M_{f(i)},\mathcal S_{f(i)})$, compatible with the face identifications for $\mathcal Y$ and $\mathcal Y'$ in the obvious way.

The category of MF-sets has a natural symmetric monoidal structure: given MF-sets $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ and $\{M_j'\}_{j\in\mathcal Y'}$, their product is defined to be $\{M_i\times M_j'\}_{(i,j)\in\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}$, which is again an MF-set.

For an MF-set $\mathcal Y$, let $C^\mathcal Y_\bullet(X)$ denote the complex freely generated\footnote{A given map $M_i\to X$ contributes a copy of the orientation module of $M_i$, which is isomorphic to $\mathbb Z$ but not canonically so.} by maps $M_i\to X$ (for $i\in\mathcal Y$), with differential given by the obvious sum over all codimension one faces.  Note that there is a natural map:
\begin{equation}
C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\otimes\mathcal C_\bullet^{\mathcal Y'}(X')\to C_\bullet^{\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}(X\times X')
\end{equation}

\label{SSMFdef}
An \emph{$S^1$-MF-set} is an MF-set $\mathcal Y$ along with a principal $S^1$-bundle $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$ where $(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$ (and hence also $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$) is an increasing union of compact polyhedra (we do not require $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$ to be contractible), and for every $i\in\mathcal Y$, a pullback diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{SSMFstructuremaps}
\begin{tikzcd}
M_i\ar{r}\ar{d}&(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{d}\\
N_i\ar{r}&(BS^1)_\mathcal Y
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
with PL maps where $N_i$ (and hence also $M_i$) is a PL manifold, and where the stratification on $M_i$ is pulled back from $N_i$, such that these diagrams are compatible with the face identifications.  A map of $S^1$-MF-sets $\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Y'$ is said to be injective iff it is injective as a map of sets and the map $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y'}$ is injective.

There is a forgetful functor from $S^1$-MF-sets to MF-sets, where we remember $M_i$ (of course, there is another natural forgetful functor remembering $N_i$, though we will never use it).  When speaking of a morphism $\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Y'$ where $\mathcal Y$ is an $S^1$-MF-set and $\mathcal Y'$ is an MF-set, we implicitly apply the forgetful functor to $\mathcal Y$.

The category of $S^1$-MF-sets has a natural symmetric monoidal structure: given $S^1$-MF-sets $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ and $\{M_j'\}_{j\in\mathcal Y'}$, we may define their product $\{M_i\times M_j'\}_{(i,j)\in\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}$, which is again an $S^1$-MF-set, via the diagonal $S^1$-action on $M_i\times M_j'$ and $(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}:=(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\times(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y'}$ with the diagonal action.  The forgetful functor from $S^1$-MF-sets to MF-sets is clearly a symmetric monoidal functor.  It also makes sense to take the product of an $S^1$-MF-set and an MF-set.

For an $S^1$-MF-set $\mathcal Y$, let $C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)$ (for $X$ an $S^1$-space) denote the complex generated by commuting diagrams of $S^1$-equivariant maps:
\begin{equation}\label{SSchaindiagram}
\begin{tikzcd}
M_i\ar{r}\ar{d}&X\times(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{r}\ar{d}&(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{d}\\
N_i\ar{r}&(X\times(ES^1)_\mathcal Y)/S^1\ar{r}&(BS^1)_\mathcal Y
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
($i\in\mathcal Y$), where the outer square coincides with \eqref{SSMFstructuremaps}, with differential given by the obvious sum over all codimension one faces.  A generator \eqref{SSchaindiagram} resides in degree $\dim N_i$.  Note that there are natural compatible maps:
\begin{align}
\mathcal C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y}(X')&\to C_{\bullet+1}^\mathcal Y(X)\\
C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\otimes\mathcal C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y'}(X')&\to C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}(X\times X')\\
C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y}(X)\otimes\mathcal C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y'}(X')&\to C_{\bullet+1}^{S^1,\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}(X\times X')
\end{align}

An $S^1$-MF-set $\mathcal Y$ is called \emph{saturated} iff $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$ is contractible and every pullback diagram:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
M\ar{r}\ar{d}&(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{d}\\
N\ar{r}&(BS^1)_\mathcal Y
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $N$ (and thus $M$) is a PL manifold with cells, along with strictly transitive identifications of the faces of positive codimension with elements of $\mathcal Y$ (as in Definition \ref{SSMFdef}), is isomorphic to some $i\in\mathcal Y$.

Note that the notions of saturation for MF-sets and $S^1$-MF-sets are different: an $S^1$-MF-set is \emph{never} saturated as an MF-set.

\label{thickendefs}
Let $I\subseteq A^\mathrm{GW}$ be a finite subset.  An \emph{$I$-thickened $J$-holomorphic map of type $(g,n)$} is a quadruple $(C,u,\{\phi_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I},\{e_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I})$ where:
\begin{rlist}
\item$C$ is a nodal curve of type $(g,n)$.
\item$u:C\to X$ is a smooth map such that $u\pitchfork D_\alpha$ with exactly $r_\alpha$ intersections for all $\alpha\in I$.
\item$\phi_\alpha:C\to\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha$ an isomorphism between $C$ (with $r_\alpha$ extra marked points $u^{-1}(D_\alpha)$) and some fiber of $\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha\to\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha$.
\item$e_\alpha\in E_\alpha$.
\item The following \emph{$I$-thickened $\bar\partial$-equation} is satisfied:
\begin{equation}\label{complementeddelbar}
\bar\partial u+\sum_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha(e_\alpha)(\phi_\alpha,u)=0
\end{equation}
This equation takes place in $C^\infty(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C u^\ast TX)$.  Certainly $\bar\partial u$ is a smooth section of $\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C u^\ast TX$ over $\tilde C$.  To interpret $\lambda_\alpha(e_\alpha)(\phi_\alpha,u)$ as such a section, consider the following composition:
\begin{equation}
C\xrightarrow{(\phi_\alpha,u)}\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha\times X\xrightarrow{\lambda(e_\alpha)}\Omega^{0,1}_{\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha/\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha}\otimes_\mathbb C TX
\end{equation}
This sends a point $p\in C$ to an element of $(\Omega^{0,1}_{\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha/\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha})_{\phi_\alpha(p)}\otimes T_{u(p)}X$, which we identify via $\phi_\alpha$ with $(\Omega^{0,1}_C\otimes_\mathbb C u^\ast TX)_p$.  This is what we mean by $\lambda_\alpha(e_\alpha)(\phi_\alpha,u)$.
\end{rlist}
An \emph{isomorphism} $\iota:(C,u,\{\phi_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I},\{e_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in I})\to(C',u',\{\phi_\alpha'\}_{\alpha\in I},\{e_\alpha'\}_{\alpha\in I})$ between two $I$-thickened $J$-holomorphic maps of type $(g,n)$ is an isomorphism $\iota:C\to C'$ of curves of $(g,n)$-type such that $u=u'\circ\iota$, $\phi_\alpha=\phi_\alpha'\circ\iota$, and $e_\alpha=e_\alpha'$.  We say an $I$-thickened $J$-holomorphic map is \emph{stable} iff its automorphism group (i.e.\ group of self-isomorphisms) is finite.

Fix nonnegative integers $g$ and $n$ with $2g+n>2$, and fix $\beta\in H_2(X;\mathbb Z)$.  By Proposition \ref{GWgluingneeded}, the virtual orientation sheaf induced by $A^\mathrm{GW}$ is canonically trivialized.  Thus the implicit atlas $A^\mathrm{GW}$ induces a virtual fundamental class $[\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)]^\mathrm{vir}\in\check H^\bullet(\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X);\mathbb Q)^\vee$.  We define the \emph{Gromov--Witten invariant}:
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{GW}_{g,n}^\beta(X)\in H_\bullet(\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}\times X^n;\mathbb Q)
\end{equation}
as the pushforward of $[\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)]^\mathrm{vir}$ under the tautological map $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)\to\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}\times X^n$.  This is well-defined by the next lemma.

\label{simplexflows}
For this definition, let us view the $n$-simplex $\Delta^n$ as:
\begin{equation}
\Delta^n=\{\underline x\in[0,1]^{n+1}:0=x_0\leq\cdots\leq x_n\leq 1\}
\end{equation}
The $i$th vertex of $\Delta^n$ is given by $x_{n-i}=0$ and $x_{n-i+1}=1$.  We now consider the Morse function on $\Delta^n$ given by $f(x):=\sum_{i=1}^n\cos\pi x_i$.  Its gradient:
\begin{equation}\label{simplexmorsefunctiongradient}
\nabla f(x)=\sum_{i=1}^n\pi\sin(\pi x_i)\frac\partial{\partial x_i}
\end{equation}
is tangent to the boundary of $\Delta^n$, and its critical points are precisely the vertices of $\Delta^n$, the index at vertex $i$ being $n-i$.  Note also that for any facet inclusion $\Delta^k\hookrightarrow\Delta^n$, the pushforward of $\nabla f$ is again $\nabla f$.

Let us consider the space $\mathcal F(\Delta^n)$ of broken Morse flow lines from vertex $0$ (index $n$) to vertex $n$ (index $0$).  This space is homeomorphic to a cube $[0,1]^{n-1}$, the factors $[0,1]$ being naturally indexed by the ``middle vertices'' $1,\ldots,n-1$ of $\Delta^n$.  A flow line is broken at a vertex $i\in\{1,\ldots,n-1\}$ iff the corresponding coordinate in $[0,1]^{n-1}$ equals $1$.  Note that for any simplex $\sigma$ there are canonical compatible product/face maps:
\begin{align}
\label{Fproduct}\mathcal F(\sigma|[0\ldots k])\times\mathcal F(\sigma|[k\ldots n])&\to\mathcal F(\sigma)\\
\label{Fface}\mathcal F(\sigma|[0\ldots\hat k\ldots n])&\to\mathcal F(\sigma)
\end{align}
See also \S\ref{simplexflowsorsec} for more details.  Adams \cite{adamscobar} considered spaces of paths on $\Delta^n$ with the same key properties (though it is important that our flow lines are smooth, whereas Adams' are not).

\label{floertrajdef}
Let $\sigma\in J_n(M)\times H_n(M)$ be an $n$-simplex; we denote by $H^\sigma:\Delta^n\times M\times S^1\to\mathbb R$ and $J^\sigma:\Delta^n\to J(M)$ the corresponding smooth families.  Let $p\in\mathcal P_{H_0}$ and $q\in\mathcal P_{H_n}$ be periodic orbits, where $H_i=H^\sigma(i\in\Delta^n,\cdot,\cdot)$ is the Hamiltonian associated to the $i$th vertex of $\Delta^n$.  A \emph{Floer trajectory of type $(\sigma,p,q)$} is a triple $(C,\ell,u)$ where:
\begin{rlist}
\item$C$ is a nodal curve of type $(0,2)$.  Let us call the two marked points $x^-,x^+\in C$, and let $k=k(C)$ be the number of vertices (irreducible components of $C$) on the unique path from $x^-$ to $x^+$ in the dual graph of $C$.
\item$\ell:\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R\to\Delta^n$ is a broken Morse flow line from vertex $0$ to vertex $n$ (for the Morse function in Definition \ref{simplexflows}).  Let $0=v_0\leq\cdots\leq v_k=n$ be the corresponding sequence of vertices.  We allow $\ell$ to contain constant flow lines, i.e.\ we allow $v_i=v_{i+1}$.
\item\label{buildingdef}$u:C\to M\times S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R$ is a smooth \emph{building} of type $(\sigma,p,q)$, by which we mean the following.  Let $C^\circ$ be $C$ punctured at $\{x^-,x^+\}$ and at the nodes corresponding to the edges in the unique path in the dual graph of $C$ from $x^-$ to $x^+$.  The connected components $\{C^\circ_1,\ldots,C^\circ_k\}$ are naturally ordered ($x^-$ on the $1$st component and $x^+$ on the $k$th component).  There must be periodic orbits $\{\gamma_0,\ldots,\gamma_k\}$ where $\gamma_i\in\mathcal P_{H_{v_i}}$ with $\gamma_0=p$ and $\gamma_k=q$.  Then the negative (resp.\ positive) end of $C^\circ_i$ must be asymptotic to $(\gamma_{i-1}(t),t)$ (resp.\ $(\gamma_i(t),t)$) (with multiplicity one).  We also require that $u$ have ``finite energy''.  In addition, $u|C_i^\circ$ must be in the correct homology class: the element of $\pi_2(M)$ obtained by gluing together $u|C_i^\circ$ (resolving any nodes of $C_i^\circ$) with the given disks bounding $\gamma_i$ and $\gamma_{i-1}$ must vanish in homology.
\item$u$ is pseudo-holomorphic with respect to the almost complex structure on $M\times S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R$ defined as follows.  Use coordinates $(t,s)\in S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R$.  Fix the standard almost complex structure on $S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R$, namely $J_{S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R}(\frac\partial{\partial s})=\frac\partial{\partial t}$.  Also fix the ($s$-dependent) almost complex structure $J^\sigma(\ell(s))$ on $M$.  We let $A:T[S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R]\to TM$ be defined by $A(\frac\partial{\partial s})=X_{H^\sigma(\ell(s),t,\cdot)}$ and extended anti-holomorphically.  Now we use the following almost complex structure on $M\times S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R$:
\begin{equation}\label{compositeJoncylinder}
J=\left(\begin{matrix}
J^\sigma(\ell(s))&A\cr
0&J_{S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R}
\end{matrix}\right)
\end{equation}
Note that the projection $M\times S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R\to S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R$ is holomorphic.
\end{rlist}
An \emph{isomorphism} $\iota:(C,\ell,u)\to(C',\ell',u')$ of Floer trajectories is an isomorphism $\iota_1:C\to C'$ of curves of type $(0,2)$ and an isomorphism $\iota_2:\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R\to\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R$ (acting by translation on each factor and respecting the ordering of the terms; note that the existence of $\iota_1$ implies that $k(C)=k(C')$) such that $u=(\operatorname{id}_{M\times S^1}\times\iota_2^{-1})\circ u'\circ\iota_1$ and $\ell=\ell'\circ\iota_2$.  We say a Floer trajectory is \emph{stable} iff its automorphism group (i.e.\ group of self-isomorphisms) is finite.

\label{implicitatlasonFloer}
A \emph{(Hamiltonian Floer) thickening datum} $\alpha$ on the simplex $\Delta^n$ is a quadruple $(D_\alpha,r_\alpha,E_\alpha,\lambda_\alpha)$ where:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{implicitatlasFloerD}$D_\alpha\subseteq M\times S^1\times\Delta^n$ is a compact smooth submanifold with corners locally modeled on $\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^N\times\mathbb R^{N'}\subseteq\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^N\times\mathbb R^{N'+2}$ or $\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^{N+1}\times\mathbb R^{N'}\subseteq\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^N\times\mathbb R^{N'+3}$.  Let us denote by $\partial^\mathrm{ess} D_\alpha\subseteq\partial D_\alpha$ the closure of $\partial D_\alpha\setminus[M\times S^1\times\partial\Delta^n]$ (which is precisely the set of points with local model of the second type).
\item$r_\alpha\geq 1$ is an integer; let $\Gamma_\alpha=S_{r_\alpha}$.
\item$E_\alpha$ is a finitely generated $\mathbb R[S_{r_\alpha}]$-module.
\item$\lambda_\alpha:E_\alpha\to C^\infty(\overline{\mathcal C}_{0,2+r_\alpha}\times M,\Omega^{0,1}_{\overline{\mathcal C}_{0,2+r_\alpha}/\overline{\mathcal M}_{0,2+r_\alpha}}\otimes_\mathbb R TM)$ is an $S_{r_\alpha}$-equivariant linear map supported away from the nodes and marked points of the fibers of $\overline{\mathcal C}_{0,2+r_\alpha}\to\overline{\mathcal M}_{0,2+r_\alpha}$ (the universal family).
\end{rlist}
Let $A^\mathrm{HF}(\Delta^n)$ denote the set of all thickening datums on $\Delta^n$.

\label{morseTdef}
Let $\sigma\in\ast$ be the $n$-simplex, and let $p,q\in\mathcal P=\operatorname{crit}(H)\times\pi$.  A \emph{Morse trajectory of type $(\sigma,p,q)$} is a triple $(k,\ell,u)$ where:
\begin{rlist}
\item$k\geq 1$ is a positive integer.
\item$\ell:\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R\to\Delta^n$ is a broken Morse flow line from vertex $0$ to vertex $n$ (for the Morse function in Definition \ref{simplexflows}).  Let $0=v_0\leq\cdots\leq v_k=n$ be the corresponding sequence of vertices.  We allow $\ell$ to contain constant flow lines, i.e.\ we allow $v_i=v_{i+1}$.
\item$u:\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R\to M$ is a broken Morse flow line from $p$ to $q$ for the function $H$.  We allow $u$ to contain constant flow lines.
\item The $\pi$-components of $p$ and $q$ agree.
\end{rlist}
An \emph{isomorphism} $\iota:(k,\ell,u)\to(k',\ell',u')$ of Morse trajectories is an isomorphism $\iota:\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R\to\coprod_{i=1}^{k'}\mathbb R$ (acting by translation on each factor and respecting the ordering of the terms; we require $k=k'$) such that $u=u'\circ\iota$ and $\ell=\ell'\circ\iota$.  We say a Morse trajectory is \emph{stable} iff its automorphism group (i.e.\ group of self-isomorphisms) is finite.

Let $\overline{\mathcal M}_\mathrm{morse}(\sigma,p,q)$ denote the space of stable Morse trajectories.

We will also need the following deeper fact (and henceforth we assume that $H$ is defined as in Lemma \ref{morseistransverse}):

\begin{lemma}                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   \end{lemma}

\begin{proof}                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         \end{lemma}

\begin{proof}                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            \end{proof}

\subsection{\texorpdfstring{$S^1$}{S{\textasciicircum}1}-equivariant implicit atlas}\label{SSequivariantFloeratlassec}

We constructed in \S\ref{HFIAsubsec} an implicit atlas $\mathcal A$ on $\overline{\mathcal M}/J\!H_\bullet(M)$, and thus in particular on $\overline{\mathcal M}/\ast$.

\label{modifiedatlasforMorse}
We define an implicit atlas $\mathcal B$ on $\overline{\mathcal M}/\ast$ as follows.  On the level of index sets, we define $\mathcal B:=\mathcal A$.  However, we modify the definition of an $I$-thickened Floer trajectory as follows.  We require that when $C_\alpha$ is considered with the $r_\alpha$ extra marked points $(u|C_\alpha)^{-1}(D_\alpha)$, the only unstable components are mapped by $u$ to Morse flow lines; let $C_\alpha\to C_\alpha^\mathrm{st}$ be the map contracting all such unstable components.  Now instead of $\phi_\alpha:C_\alpha\to\overline{\mathcal C}_{0,2+r_\alpha}$, we use $\phi_\alpha:C_\alpha^\mathrm{st}\to\overline{\mathcal C}_{0,2+r_\alpha}$.

The rest of the atlas data is defined analogously with that of $\mathcal A$ without any serious difference.  Note, however, that to verify that the locus $U_{IJ}\subseteq X_I$ is open, we must appeal to Lemma \ref{morseistransverse}.

A \emph{sheaf} is a presheaf $\mathcal F$ satisfying the following condition:
\begin{equation*}
\tag{Sh}\label{opensheafproperty}0\to\mathcal F\Bigl(\bigcup_{\alpha\in A}U_\alpha\Bigr)\to\prod_{\alpha\in A}\mathcal F(U_\alpha)\to\prod_{\alpha,\beta\in A}\mathcal F(U_\alpha\cap U_\beta)\quad\text{ is exact }\forall\text{ }\{U_\alpha\subseteq X\}_{\alpha\in A}
\end{equation*}
The category of sheaves on a space $X$ is denoted $\operatorname{Shv} X$ (a full subcategory of $\operatorname{Prshv} X$, meaning a morphism between sheaves is the same as a morphism of the corresponding presheaves).

A \emph{$\mathcal K$-sheaf} is a $\mathcal K$-presheaf $\mathcal F$ satisfying the following three conditions:\footnote{A similar set of axioms appears in Lurie \cite[Definition 7.3.4.1]{luriehtt}.}
\begin{align*}
\tag{Sh$_\mathcal K$1}\label{sheafvanishing}\mathcal F(\varnothing)&=0\\
\tag{Sh$_\mathcal K$2}\label{sheafproperty}0\to\mathcal F(K_1\cup K_2)\to\mathcal F(K_1)\oplus\mathcal F(K_2)\to\mathcal F(K_1\cap K_2)&\text{ is exact }\forall\text{ }K_1,K_2\subseteq X\\
\tag{Sh$_\mathcal K$3}\label{sheafcontinuity}\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}K\subseteq U\cr U\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F(\overline U)\to\mathcal F(K)&\text{ is an isomorphism }\forall\text{ }K\subseteq X
\end{align*}
The category of $\mathcal K$-sheaves on a space $X$ is denoted $\operatorname{Shv}_\mathcal K X$ (a full subcategory of $\operatorname{Prshv}_\mathcal K X$).

\label{opencptrelationfunctors}
We define functors:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}\operatorname{Prshv}_\mathcal K X\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{\alpha_\ast}&\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{\alpha^\ast}\operatorname{Prshv} X\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
by the formulas:
\begin{align}
\label{alphaast}(\alpha^\ast\mathcal F)(K)&:=\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}K\subseteq U\cr U\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F(U)\\
\label{alphaupperast}(\alpha_\ast\mathcal F)(U)&:=\varprojlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}K\subseteq U\cr K\textrm{ compact}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F(K)
\end{align}
It is easy to see that there is an adjunction $\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{Prshv}_\mathcal K X}(\alpha^\ast\mathcal F,\mathcal G)=\operatorname{Hom}_{\operatorname{Prshv} X}(\mathcal F,\alpha_\ast\mathcal G)$ (giving an element of either $\operatorname{Hom}$-set is the same as giving a compatible system of maps $\mathcal F(U)\to\mathcal G(K)$ for pairs $K\subseteq U$).

A \emph{homotopy sheaf} is a complex of presheaves $\mathcal F^\bullet$ satisfying the following condition:
\begin{equation*}
\tag{hSh}\label{openhomotopysheafproperty}\hspace{-20pt}\biggl[\mathcal F^\bullet\Bigl(\bigcup_{\alpha\in A}U_\alpha\Bigr)\to\prod_{\alpha\in A}\mathcal F^{\bullet-1}(U_\alpha)\to\prod_{\alpha,\beta\in A}\mathcal F^{\bullet-2}(U_\alpha\cap U_\beta)\to\cdots\biggr]\text{ is acyclic }\forall\text{ }\{U_\alpha\subseteq X\}_{\alpha\in A}
\end{equation*}
The category of homotopy sheaves on a space $X$ is denoted $\operatorname{hShv} X$ (morphisms are morphisms of complexes of presheaves).

\label{homotopyKsheaf}
A \emph{homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf} is a complex of $\mathcal K$-presheaves $\mathcal F^\bullet$ satisfying the following three conditions:
\begin{align*}
\tag{hSh$_\mathcal K$1}\label{homotopysheafacyclic}\mathcal F^\bullet(\varnothing)&\text{ is acyclic}\\
\tag{hSh$_\mathcal K$2}\label{homotopysheafproperty}\hspace{-10pt}\bigl[\mathcal F^\bullet(K_1\cup K_2)\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-1}(K_1)\oplus\mathcal F^{\bullet-1}(K_2)\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-2}(K_1\cap K_2)\bigr]&\text{ is acyclic }\forall\text{ }K_1,K_2\subseteq X\\
\tag{hSh$_\mathcal K$3}\label{homotopysheafcontinuity}\smash{\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}K\subseteq U\cr U\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}}\mathcal F^\bullet(\overline U)\to\mathcal F^\bullet(K)&\text{ is a quasi-isomorphism }\forall\text{ }K\subseteq X
\end{align*}
The category of homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves on a space $X$ is denoted $\operatorname{hShv}_\mathcal K X$ (morphisms are morphisms of complexes of $\mathcal K$-presheaves).

\label{presheafpushforward}
Let $f:X\to Y$ be a map of spaces.  We define functors:
\begin{rlist}
\item$f_\ast:\operatorname{Prshv} X\to\operatorname{Prshv} Y$ by $(f_\ast\mathcal F)(U):=\mathcal F(f^{-1}(U))$.
\item$f_\ast:\operatorname{Prshv}_\mathcal K X\to\operatorname{Prshv}_\mathcal K Y$ by $(f_\ast\mathcal F)(K):=\mathcal F(f^{-1}(K))$ (if $f$ is proper).
\end{rlist}
(the action of $f_\ast$ on morphism spaces is obvious).

Let $f:X\to Y$ be an injective map of spaces.  We define:
\begin{rlist}
\item$f^\ast:\operatorname{hShv}_\mathcal K Y\to\operatorname{hShv}_\mathcal K X$ by $(f^\ast\mathcal F^\bullet)(K):=\mathcal F^\bullet(f(K))$.
\end{rlist}
We check the properties: \eqref{homotopysheafacyclic} is clear, and \eqref{homotopysheafproperty} follows since $f$ is injective.  For \eqref{homotopysheafcontinuity}, use Remark \ref{nbhdintersectiongood}, the injectivity of $f$, and Lemma \ref{anycompactintersectionworks}.

\label{cechdefnI}
Let $\mathcal F$ be a sheaf on a space $X$.  We define the \v Cech cohomology:
\begin{equation}\label{cechcomplexI}
\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F):=\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}X=\bigcup_{\alpha\in A}U_\alpha\cr\text{open cover}\end{smallmatrix}}H^\bullet\biggl[\bigoplus_{p\geq 0}\prod_{\begin{smallmatrix}S\subseteq A\cr\left|S\right|=p+1\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F\Bigl(\bigcap_{\alpha\in S}U_\alpha\Bigr)[-p]\biggr]
\end{equation}
with the standard \v Cech differential.\footnote{Technically speaking, so that the signs in the differential can be defined canonically, we should really tensor each term of the direct product with $(\mathbb Zo_1\oplus\mathbb Zo_2)/(o_1+o_2)\cong\mathbb Z$ where $o_1,o_2$ denote the two orientations of the $p$-simplex on vertex set $S$.}  For any compact $K\subseteq X$, define $\check H^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)$ (\v Cech cohomology with supports in $K$) via \eqref{cechcomplexI} except replacing every instance of $\mathcal F(U)$ with $\ker[\mathcal F(U)\to\mathcal F(U\setminus K)]$.  We let $\check H^\bullet_c(X;\mathcal F):=\varinjlim_{K\subseteq X}\check H^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)$ (\v Cech cohomology with compact supports).

\label{cechdefnII}
Let $\mathcal F^\bullet$ be a complex of $\mathcal K$-presheaves on a compact space $X$.  We define:
\begin{equation}\label{cechcomplexII}
\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F^\bullet):=\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}X=\bigcup_{i=1}^nK_i\cr\text{finite compact cover}\end{smallmatrix}}H^\bullet\biggl[\bigoplus_{p\geq 0}\bigoplus_{1\leq i_0<\cdots<i_p\leq n}\mathcal F^{\bullet-p}\Bigl(\bigcap_{j=0}^pK_{i_j}\Bigr)\biggr]
\end{equation}
with the standard \v Cech differential (plus the internal differential of $\mathcal F^\bullet$).  We also define $\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F)$ for any $\mathcal K$-presheaf $\mathcal F$ by viewing it as a complex concentrated in degree zero.

\label{pullbackcHKprshv}
Let $f:X\to Y$ be a map of compact spaces.  A finite compact cover of $Y$ pulls back to give a finite compact cover of $X$, and this gives an identification of the \v Cech complex for the cover of $Y$ with coefficients in $f_\ast\mathcal F^\bullet$ with the \v Cech complex for the cover of $X$ with coefficients in $\mathcal F^\bullet$.  Hence we get a natural map:
\begin{rlist}
\item$f^\ast:\check H^\bullet(Y;f_\ast\mathcal F^\bullet)\to\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F^\bullet)$ for $\mathcal F^i\in\operatorname{Prshv}_\mathcal K X$.
\end{rlist}

\label{purehomotopyKsheaf}
We say that a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf $\mathcal F^\bullet$ on $X$ is \emph{pure} iff:
\begin{rlist}
\item(Stalk cohomology) $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet_p=0$ for $i\ne 0$ and all $p\in X$.
\item(Weak vanishing) $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet=0$ for $i<<0$ locally on $X$ (meaning that for all $p\in X$, there exists an open set $U\subseteq X$ containing $p$ and an integer $N>-\infty$ such that $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K)=0$ for all $K\subseteq U$ and $i\leq N$).
\end{rlist}

\label{manordef}
Let $M$ be a topological manifold.  We let $\mathfrak o_M$ denote the \emph{orientation sheaf}\footnote{Note that the fundamental class lies in homology twisted by $\mathfrak o_M^\vee$.} of $M$ (the corresponding $\mathcal K$-sheaf is defined by $\mathfrak o_M(K):=H_{\dim M}(M,M\setminus K)$); it is locally isomorphic to the constant sheaf $\underline\mathbb Z$ (and following Convention \ref{signconventions}, it has parity $\dim M\in\mathbb Z/2$).  Now let $M$ be a topological manifold with boundary, and define orientation sheaves on $M$:
\begin{align}
\mathfrak o_M&:=j_*\mathfrak o_{M\setminus\partial M}\\
\mathfrak o_{M\operatorname{rel}\partial}&:=j_!\mathfrak o_{M\setminus\partial M}
\end{align}
where $j:M\setminus\partial M\hookrightarrow M$.  Then $\mathfrak o_M$ is locally isomorphic to the constant sheaf $\underline\mathbb Z$, and there is a sequence of sheaves which is exact on stalks:
\begin{equation}
0\to\mathfrak o_{M\operatorname{rel}\partial}\to\mathfrak o_M\to i_\ast\mathfrak o_{\partial M}\to 0
\end{equation}
where $i:\partial M\hookrightarrow M$, and the second map comes from the boundary map in the long exact sequence of the pair $(M,\partial M)$.

\label{simplicialproduct}
Let $\mathcal S$ and $\mathcal T$ be two finite posets, and let $\{A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s'}\}$ and $\{B^\bullet_{\mathfrak t,\mathfrak t'}\}$ be homotopy diagrams over $\mathcal S$ and $\mathcal T$ respectively.  Their tensor product $\{A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s'}\otimes B^\bullet_{\mathfrak t,\mathfrak t'}\}$ is naturally a homotopy diagram over $\mathcal S\times\mathcal T$.  Now there is a natural morphism:
\begin{equation}\label{tensorproductdirectlimitmorphism}
\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{\mathfrak s\preceq\mathfrak s'\in\mathcal S}A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s'}\otimes\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{\mathfrak t\preceq\mathfrak t'\in\mathcal T}B^\bullet_{\mathfrak t,\mathfrak t'}\to\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{\mathfrak s\times\mathfrak t\preceq\mathfrak s'\times\mathfrak t'\in\mathcal S\times\mathcal T}A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s'}\otimes B^\bullet_{\mathfrak t,\mathfrak t'}
\end{equation}
To define this morphism, we simply observe that the nerve of $\mathcal S\times\mathcal T$ is the standard simplicial subdivision of the product of the nerves of $\mathcal S$ and $\mathcal T$, and this is covered by a morphism of coefficient systems.\footnote{In fact, from this perspective one easily sees that \eqref{tensorproductdirectlimitmorphism} is always a quasi-isomorphism.}

Let $\mathcal F$ be a sheaf.  We define the \v Cech cochains:
\begin{equation}\label{cechcomplexIderived}
\check C^\bullet(X;\mathcal F):=\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}\{U_x\subseteq X\}_{x\in X}\cr\text{rigid open cover}\end{smallmatrix}}\bigoplus_{p\geq 0}\prod_{\begin{smallmatrix}S\subseteq X\cr\left|S\right|=p+1\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F\Bigl(\bigcap_{x\in S}U_x\Bigr)[-p]
\end{equation}
with the standard \v Cech differential.  For any compact $K\subseteq X$, define $\check C^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)$ (\v Cech cochains with supports in $K$) via \eqref{cechcomplexIderived} except replacing every instance of $\mathcal F(U)$ with $\ker[\mathcal F(U)\to\mathcal F(U\setminus K)]$.  We let $\check C^\bullet_c(X;\mathcal F):=\varinjlim_{K\subseteq X}\check C^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)$ (\v Cech cochains with compact supports).

Clearly the homology of $\check C^\bullet(X;\mathcal F)$ (resp.\ $\check C^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)$, $\check C^\bullet_c(X;\mathcal F)$) is $\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F)$ (resp.\ $\check H^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)$, $\check H^\bullet_c(X;\mathcal F)$).

Let $\{C^\bullet_\lambda\}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}$ be an inverse system of complexes.  We define:
\begin{equation}
\mathop{R\varprojlim}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}C^\bullet_\lambda:=\prod_{q\geq 0}\prod_{\lambda_0\leq\ldots\leq\lambda_q}C^{\bullet-q}_{\lambda_0}
\end{equation}
with differential obtained by viewing this as cochains on the nerve of $\Lambda$ with a particular coefficient system.  See Marde\v si\'c \cite[\S 17]{mardesic} for more details on and basic properties of $\mathop{R\varprojlim}$.

Let $\{A_\lambda\}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}$ be an inverse system of abelian groups.  We define:
\begin{equation}\label{limidef}
\mathop{\textstyle\varprojlim^i}\limits_{\lambda\in\Lambda}A_\lambda:=H^i\Bigl[\mathop{R\varprojlim}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}A_\lambda\Bigr]
\end{equation}
(viewing $A_\lambda$ as an inverse system of complex concentrated in degree zero).  The inverse limit functor $\varprojlim$ from inverse systems of abelian groups indexed by $\Lambda$ to abelian groups is left exact, and $\varprojlim^i$ are its right derived functors (see Marde\v si\'c \cite[Corollary 11.47]{mardesic}).  See Marde\v si\'c \cite[\S\S 11--15]{mardesic} for more details on and basic properties of $\varprojlim^i$.

\label{steenrodhomdef}
Let $X$ be compact.  We define:
\begin{equation}\label{steenrodchainsRlimeqn}
\overline C_\bullet(X):=\mathop{R\varprojlim}_{X=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^nE_i}\bigoplus_{p\geq 0}\bigoplus_{\begin{smallmatrix}S\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}\cr\left|S\right|=p+1\cr\bigcap_{i\in S}\overline{E_i}\ne\varnothing\end{smallmatrix}}\mathbb Z[-p]
\end{equation}
For a map $f:X\to Y$, there is an induced pushforward map $f_\ast:\overline C_\bullet(X)\to\overline C_\bullet(Y)$.  We denote by $\overline H_\bullet(X;\mathcal F)$ the homology of $\overline C_\bullet(X;\mathcal F)$, and we define relative homology $\overline H_\bullet(X,Y;\mathcal F)$ as the homology of the relevant mapping cone.

More generally, let $\mathcal F$ be a locally constant sheaf of abelian groups on $X$.  Fix a partition $X=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^nE_i^0$ and trivializations of $\mathcal F|_{\overline{E_i^0}}$.  This gives rise to a local system $\mathcal F$ on $N(\{\overline{E_i}\}_{i=1}^n)$ for any partition $\{E_i\}$ refining $\{E_i^0\}$.  We now define $\overline C_\bullet(X;\mathcal F)$ as in \eqref{steenrodchainsRlimeqn}, restricted to partitions refining $\{E_i^0\}$ and using the coefficient system on the nerve induced by $\mathcal F$ and the fixed trivializations of $\mathcal F|_{\overline{E_i^0}}$.  Since any two choices of trivializations of $\mathcal F|_{\overline{E_i}}$ become isomorphic (in the sense that that their sets of ``constant sections'' coincide) after pulling back to some common refinement, it follows that $\overline C_\bullet(X;\mathcal F)$ is well-defined up to essentially unique quasi-isomorphism (by Lemma \ref{cofinalityforRlim}).  For a map $f:X\to Y$ and $\mathcal F\to f^\ast\mathcal G$, there is a pushforward map $\overline C_\bullet(X;\mathcal F)\to\overline C_\bullet(Y;\mathcal G)$.

We call the resulting curve $C_\alpha$, and it is equipped with cylindrical ends (corresponding to those $\alpha_i=0$) and necks (corresponding to those $\alpha_i\ne 0$):
\begin{align}
\label{Gend}[0,\infty)\times S^1\to C_\alpha\\
\label{Gneck}[0,6S_i]\times S^1\to C_\alpha
\end{align}
In each neck, we have coordinates $(s,t)\in[0,6S_i]\times S^1$ and coordinates $(s',t')\in[0,6S_i]\times S^1$, which satisfy $s+s'=6S_i$ and $t+t'=\theta_i$.

For $\alpha\in\mathbb C^d$, we define the ``flattened'' map $u_{0|\alpha}:C_0\to X$ as follows.  Away from the ends, $u_{0|\alpha}$ coincides with $u_0$.  Over an end $[0,\infty)\times S^1$, we define it as follows:
\begin{equation}
u_{0|\alpha}(s,t):=\begin{cases}
u_0(s,t)&s\leq S-1\cr
\exp_{u_0(n)}\left[\chi(S-s)\cdot\exp_{u_0(n)}^{-1}u_0(s,t)\right]&S-1\leq s\leq S\cr
u_0(n)&S\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $n\in C_0$ denotes the corresponding node.

For $\xi\in C^\infty(C_0,u_0^\ast TX)$, we define:
\begin{equation}
\xi_\alpha\in C^\infty(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)
\end{equation}
as follows.  Away from the necks, $\xi_\alpha$ coincides with $\xi$.  Over a neck $[0,6S]\times S^1$, we define it as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{kernelpregluingformula}
\xi_\alpha(s,t):=\begin{cases}
\xi(s,t)&s\leq S-1\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(s,t)\to u_\alpha(s,t)}\left[\xi(s,t)\right]&S-1\leq s\leq S\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(s,t)\to u_\alpha(s,t)}\left[\xi(s,t)\right]\cdot(1-\chi(s-S))+\chi(s-S)\cdot \xi(n)&S\leq s\leq S+1\cr
\xi(n)&S+1\leq s\leq 5S-1\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(s',t')\to u_\alpha(s',t')}\left[\xi(s',t')\right]\cdot(1-\chi(s'-S))+\chi(s'-S)\cdot \xi(n)&5S-1\leq s\leq 5S\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(s',t')\to u_\alpha(s',t')}\left[\xi(s',t')\right]&5S\leq s\leq 5S+1\cr
\xi(s',t')&5S+1\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

We define a map:
\begin{equation}
T_{\alpha,y}:W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TX)\to W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)_D\oplus E
\end{equation}
as the composition:
\begin{equation}
T_{\alpha,y}:={\operatorname{glue}}\circ\mathsf{PT}\circ\operatorname{id}\circ Q_{0,0}\circ I_y\circ\mathsf{PT}\circ\operatorname{break}
\end{equation}
of maps in the following diagram, to be defined below:
\begin{equation}\label{biginversediagram}
\begin{tikzcd}
W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)_D\oplus E\ar{r}{D_{\alpha,y}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TX)\ar{d}{\mathrm{break}}\\
W^{k,2,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TX)_D\oplus E\ar{u}{\mathrm{glue}}\ar{r}{D_{0|\alpha,y}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TX)\ar[leftrightarrow]{d}{\mathsf{PT}}\\
W^{k,2,\delta}(C_0,u_0^\ast TX)_D\oplus E\ar[leftrightarrow]{u}{\mathsf{PT}}\ar{r}{D_{0,y}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_0^\ast TX)\ar[leftrightarrow]{d}{I_y}\\
W^{k,2,\delta}(C_0,u_0^\ast TX)_D\oplus E\ar[equals]{u}{\operatorname{id}}\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{D_{0,0}}&\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{Q_{0,0}}W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_0^\ast TX)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
($D_{0|\alpha,y}$ denotes the linearized operator at $u_{0|\alpha}$).

We fix once and for all a smooth family of $(j_0,j_y)$-linear identifications $I_y:(T\tilde C_0,j_0)\to(T\tilde C_0,j_y)$ which are the identity over the ends/necks.  This gives rise to the bottom right vertical map in \eqref{biginversediagram}.

We define the middle vertical maps in \eqref{biginversediagram} using parallel transport (equivalently, using the local trivializations defined by \eqref{localPTcoords}).

We define the map:
\begin{equation}
W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TX)\xrightarrow{\mathrm{break}}W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TX)
\end{equation}
as follows.  Fix a smooth function $\bar\chi:\mathbb R\to[0,1]$ such that:
\begin{equation}
\bar\chi(x)=\begin{cases}1&x\leq-1\cr0&x\geq+1\end{cases}\qquad\bar\chi(x)+\bar\chi(-x)=1
\end{equation}
Let $\eta\in W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TX)$.  Away from the ends with $\alpha\ne 0$, $\operatorname{break}(\eta)$ is simply $\eta$.  In any particular end $[0,\infty)\times S^1\subseteq C_0$ with $\alpha\ne 0$, we define:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{break}(\eta)(s,t):=\begin{cases}
\eta(s,t)&s\leq 3S-1\cr
\bar\chi(s-3S)\cdot\eta(s,t)&3S-1\leq s\leq 3S+1\cr
0&3S+1\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
(noting the corresponding neck $[0,6S]\times S^1\subseteq C_\alpha$).

We define the map:
\begin{equation}
W^{k,2,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TX)_D\xrightarrow{\mathrm{glue}}W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)_D
\end{equation}
Let $\xi\in W^{k,2,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TX)_D$.  Away from the necks of $C_\alpha$, $\operatorname{glue}(\xi)$ is simply $\xi$.  In any particular neck $[0,6S]\times S^1\subseteq C_\alpha$, we define:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{glue}(\xi)(s,t):=\begin{cases}
\xi(s,t)&s\leq 2S\cr
\xi(n)+\chi(4S-s)\cdot[\xi(s,t)-\xi(n)]+\chi(4S-s')\cdot[\xi(s',t')-\xi(n)]&2S\leq s\leq 4S\cr
\xi(s',t')&4S\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
(noting the corresponding ends $(s,t)\in[0,\infty)\times S^1\subseteq C_0$ and $(s',t')\in[0,\infty)\times S^1\subseteq C_0$).  Note that the cutoff of $\xi(s,t)$ occurs around $4S\in[0,6S]$, where the weight $e^{2\delta\min(s,6S-s)}$ is much smaller than the weight $e^{2\delta s}$ at $4S\in[0,\infty)$.  We will see in the proof of Lemma \ref{approxtop} that this makes it easy to derive the desired estimates on $\bar\partial\mathrm{glue}(\xi)$.  This trick was explained to us by Abouzaid and attributed to Fukaya--Oh--Ohta--Ono \cite{foootechnicaldetails}.

We define a map:
\begin{equation}
Q_{\alpha,y}:W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TX)\to W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)_D\oplus E
\end{equation}
as the sum:
\begin{equation}
Q_{\alpha,y}:=T_{\alpha,y}\sum_{k=0}^\infty(\mathbf 1-D_{\alpha,y} T_{\alpha,y})^k
\end{equation}

We define:
\begin{align}
u_{\alpha,y,\kappa}&:=\exp_{u_\alpha}\kappa_{\alpha,y}\\
e_{\alpha,y,\kappa}&:=e_0+\operatorname{proj}_E\kappa_{\alpha,y}
\end{align}
where $\kappa_{\alpha,y}$ is as in Proposition \ref{newtoniteration}, and we consider the map:
\begin{align}\label{gluingmap}
\mathbb C^d\times\mathbb R^{\dim\overline{\mathcal M}^d}\times K&\to\overline{\mathcal M}(X)\\
(\alpha,y,\kappa)&\mapsto(\alpha,y,u_{\alpha,y,\kappa},e_{\alpha,y,\kappa})
\end{align}
(with $\{x_i\}$ understood).  It follows from the definition that \eqref{gluingmap} commutes with the projection from both sides to $\overline{\mathcal M}\times E/E'$.

For $\alpha\in\mathbb C^d\times\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^{k-1}$, we define the ``flattened'' map $u_{0|\alpha}:C_0\to M$ as follows.  Away from the ends, $u_{0|\alpha}$ coincides with $u_0$.  Over a bubble end $S^1\times[0,\infty)$, we define $u_{0|\alpha}$ as follows:
\begin{equation}
u_{0|\alpha}(t,s):=\begin{cases}
u_0(t,s)&s\leq S-1\cr
\exp_{u_0(n)}\left[\chi(S-s)\cdot\exp_{u_0(n)}^{-1}u_0(t,s)\right]&S-1\leq s\leq S\cr
u_0(n)&S\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $n\in C_0$ denotes the corresponding node.  Over a positive main end $S^1\times[N,\infty)$, we define $u_{0|\alpha}$ as:
\begin{equation}
u_{0|\alpha}(t,s):=\begin{cases}
u_0(t,s)&s\leq S-1\cr
\exp_{\gamma(t)}\left[\chi(S-s)\cdot\exp_{\gamma(t)}^{-1}u_0(t,s)\right]&S-1\leq s\leq S\cr
\gamma(t)&S\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
where $\gamma(t)=u_0(t,\infty)$ denotes the corresponding periodic orbit; an analogous definition applies over the negative main ends.

\label{kernelpregluingHF}
For $\kappa\in K\subseteq C^\infty(C_0,u_0^\ast TM)$, we define $\kappa_\alpha\in C^\infty(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)$ as follows.  Away from the necks, $\kappa_\alpha$ coincides with $\kappa$.  Over a bubble neck $S^1\times[0,6S]$, we define $\kappa_\alpha$ as:
\begin{equation}\label{kernelpregluingformulaHFbubble}
\kappa_\alpha(t,s):=\begin{cases}
\kappa(t,s)&s\leq S-1\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(t,s)\to u_\alpha(t,s)}\left[\kappa(t,s)\right]&S-1\leq s\leq S\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(t,s)\to u_\alpha(t,s)}\left[\kappa(t,s)\right]\cdot(1-\chi(s-S))+\chi(s-S)\cdot \kappa(n)&S\leq s\leq S+1\cr
\kappa(n)&S+1\leq s\leq 5S-1\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(t',s')\to u_\alpha(t',s')}\left[\kappa(t',s')\right]\cdot(1-\chi(s'-S))+\chi(s'-S)\cdot \kappa(n)&5S-1\leq s\leq 5S\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(t',s')\to u_\alpha(t',s')}\left[\kappa(t',s')\right]&5S\leq s\leq 5S+1\cr
\kappa(t',s')&5S+1\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
and over a main neck $S^1\times[N,6S-N]$, we define $\kappa_\alpha$ as:
\begin{equation}\label{kernelpregluingformulaHFmain}
\kappa_\alpha(t,s):=\begin{cases}
\kappa(t,s)&s\leq S-1\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(t,s)\to u_\alpha(t,s)}\left[\kappa(t,s)\right]&S-1\leq s\leq S\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(t,s)\to u_\alpha(t,s)}\left[\kappa(t,s)\right]\cdot(1-\chi(s-S))&S\leq s\leq S+1\cr
0&S+1\leq s\leq 5S-1\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(t',s')\to u_\alpha(t',s')}\left[\kappa(t',s')\right]\cdot(1-\chi(-s'-S))&5S-1\leq s\leq 5S\cr
\mathsf{PT}_{u_0(t',s')\to u_\alpha(t',s')}\left[\kappa(t',s')\right]&5S\leq s\leq 5S+1\cr
\kappa(t',s')&5S+1\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

We define a map:
\begin{equation}
T_{\alpha,w,y}:W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})\to W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E
\end{equation}
as the composition:
\begin{equation}
T_{\alpha,w,y}:={\operatorname{glue}}\circ\mathsf{PT}\circ Q_{0,0,0}\circ\mathsf{PT}\circ(\operatorname{id}^{1,0}_\ast\otimes\operatorname{id}^{1,0})^{-1}\circ\operatorname{break}
\end{equation}
of maps in the following diagram, to be defined below:
\begin{equation}\label{biginversediagramHF}
\begin{tikzcd}
W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E\ar{r}{D_{\alpha,w,y}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})\ar{d}{\mathrm{break}}\\
W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E\ar{u}{\mathrm{glue}}\ar{r}{D_{0|\alpha,0,y}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_y(\cdot)))})\\
W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E\ar[equals]{u}\ar{r}{D_{0|\alpha,0,0}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})\ar[leftrightarrow]{d}{\mathsf{PT}}\ar[swap]{u}{{\operatorname{id}^{1,0}_\ast}\otimes{\operatorname{id}^{1,0}}}\\
W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_0^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E\ar[leftrightarrow]{u}{\mathsf{PT}}\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{D_{0,0,0}}&\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{Q_{0,0,0}}W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_0^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
The top and bottom horizontal maps $D_{\alpha,w,y}$ and $D_{0,0,0}$ are the linearized operators defined earlier.  The third horizontal map $D_{0|\alpha,0,0}$ is the linearized operator at the flattened map $u_{0|\alpha}$ (its definition is identical to that of $D_{0,0,0}$ except for using $u_{0|\alpha}$ in place of $u_0$).  Similarly, the second horizontal map $D_{0|\alpha,0,y}$ is the linearized operator at the flattened map $u_{0|\alpha}$, using $(j_y,A_y)$ in place of $(j_0,A_0)$.

The vertical maps $\mathsf{PT}$ are simply parallel transport $\mathsf{PT}^{\ell_{0,0}(A_0(\cdot))}$; this is valid since $\mathsf{PT}$ is $J$-linear.

The vertical map $\operatorname{id}^{1,0}_\ast\otimes\operatorname{id}^{1,0}$ denotes the tensor product of $\operatorname{id}^{1,0}:TM_{J(a)}\to TM_{J(b)}$ (the $(1,0)$-component of the identity map) and $\operatorname{id}^{1,0}_\ast:\Omega^{0,1}_{C_0,j_0}\to\Omega^{0,1}_{C_0,j_y}$ (the map induced by $\operatorname{id}^{1,0}:(TC_0,j_0)\to(TC_0,j_y)$).

We define the map:
\begin{equation}
W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM)_{D,H}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{glue}}W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)_{D,H}
\end{equation}
Let $\xi\in W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM)_{D,H}$.  Away from the necks of $C_\alpha$, $\operatorname{glue}(\xi)$ is simply $\xi$.  In any particular bubble neck $S^1\times[0,6S]\subseteq C_\alpha$, we define:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{glue}(\xi)(s,t):=\begin{cases}
\xi(s,t)&s\leq 2S\cr
\xi(n)+\chi(4S-s)\cdot[\xi(s,t)-\xi(n)]+\chi(4S-s')\cdot[\xi(s',t')-\xi(n)]&2S\leq s\leq 4S\cr
\xi(s',t')&4S\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
(noting the corresponding ends $(t,s)\in S^1\times[0,\infty)\subseteq C_0$ and $(t',s')\in S^1\times[0,\infty)\subseteq C_0$); this definition also applies over the main necks with the obvious adjustment (and no $\xi(n)$ terms).

We define the map:
\begin{equation}
W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})\xrightarrow{\mathrm{break}}W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_y(\cdot)))})
\end{equation}
as follows.  Fix a smooth function $\bar\chi:\mathbb R\to[0,1]$ such that:
\begin{equation}
\bar\chi(x)=\begin{cases}1&x\leq-1\cr0&x\geq+1\end{cases}\qquad\bar\chi(x)+\bar\chi(-x)=1
\end{equation}
Let $\eta\in W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})$.  Away from the ends with $\alpha\ne 0$, $\operatorname{break}(\eta)$ is given by $(\operatorname{id}^{1,0})^{-1}(\eta)$, where $\operatorname{id}^{1,0}:TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_y(\cdot)))}\to TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))}$ denotes the $(1,0)$-component of the identity map.  In any particular bubble end $[0,\infty)\times S^1\subseteq C_0$ with $\alpha\ne 0$, we define:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{break}(\eta)(t,s):=\begin{cases}
(\operatorname{id}^{1,0})^{-1}(\eta(t,s))&s\leq 3S-1\cr
\bar\chi(s-3S)\cdot(\operatorname{id}^{1,0})^{-1}(\eta(t,s))&3S-1\leq s\leq 3S+1\cr
0&3S+1\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
(noting the corresponding neck $[0,6S]\times S^1\subseteq C_\alpha$); this definition also applies over the main ends, with the obvious adjustment for negative main ends.

We define a map:
\begin{equation}
Q_{\alpha,w,y}:W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})\to W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E
\end{equation}
as the sum:
\begin{equation}
Q_{\alpha,w,y}:=T_{\alpha,w,y}\sum_{k=0}^\infty(\mathbf 1-D_{\alpha,w,y}T_{\alpha,w,y})^k
\end{equation}

We define:
\begin{align}
u_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa}&:=\exp_{u_\alpha}\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}\\
e_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa}&:=e_0+\operatorname{proj}_E\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}
\end{align}
where $\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}$ is as in Proposition \ref{newtoniterationHF}, and we consider the following \emph{gluing map}:
\begin{align}\label{gluingmapHF}
\mathbb C^d\times(\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^{k-1})^{\leq\mathfrak s}\times\mathbb R^\ast\times\prod_{i=1}^{k\prime}(H^i)^{\leq\mathfrak s}\times K&\to\overline{\mathcal M}(M)\\
(\alpha,w,y,\kappa)&\mapsto(\alpha,w,y,u_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa},e_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa})
\end{align}
($\{x^i\}_{1\leq i\leq r}$ are determined uniquely by $\alpha,w,y,u_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa}$, so we omit them from the notation).  It follows from the definition that the gluing map commutes with the projection from both sides to $\mathbb C^d\times(\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^{k-1})^{\leq\mathfrak s}\times\mathbb R^\ast\times\prod_{i=1}^{k\prime}(H^i)^{\leq\mathfrak s}\times E/E'$.

theorem.raw:
\label{Fsheaffundamentaliso}
Let $X$ be a space with finite locally orientable implicit atlas $A$ with boundary.  Then there are canonical isomorphisms:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X;A)&=\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_X)\\
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)&=\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{align}

\label{Slocalfundamentaliso}
Let $X$ be an almost free $S^1$-space with finite locally $S^1$-orientable $S^1$-equivariant implicit atlas with boundary $A$.  Then there are canonical isomorphisms fitting (as the top horizontal maps) into commutative diagrams:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{CD}
H^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(X;A)@>\sim>>\check H^\bullet(X/S^1,\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_X)\cr
@V\eqref{CSStimesSS}VV@V\pi^\ast VV\cr
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X;A)@>\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}>>\check H^\bullet(X,\mathfrak o_X)
\end{CD}
\qquad
\begin{CD}
H^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)@>\sim>>\check H^\bullet(X/S^1,\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\cr
@V\eqref{CSStimesSS}VV@V\pi^\ast VV\cr
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)@>\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}>>\check H^\bullet(X,\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{CD}
\end{equation*}

\label{mainresult}
In the above setup (from the beginning of \S\ref{setupforgluing} until here), we have:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{openness}$\overline{\mathcal M}(X)^\mathrm{reg}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(X)$ is open.
\item\label{manifold}$\overline{\mathcal M}(X)^\mathrm{reg}$ is a topological manifold of dimension $\dim\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n+\ell}+(1-g)\dim X+2\langle c_1(X),\beta\rangle+\dim E-2\ell$.
\item\label{submersion}The projection $\overline{\mathcal M}(X)^\mathrm{reg}\to E/E'$ is a topological submersion, i.e.\ locally modeled on a projection $\mathbb R^n\times\mathbb R^m\to\mathbb R^n$.
\item\label{orientation}The orientation sheaf $\mathfrak o_{\overline{\mathcal M}(X)^\mathrm{reg}}$ is canonically isomorphic to $\mathfrak o_E\otimes\bigotimes_{i=1}^\ell\mathfrak o_{u(p_{n+i})^\ast N_{D/X}}^\vee$.
\end{rlist}

lemma.raw:
\label{CSTpure}
Let $X$ be a space with finite locally orientable implicit atlas $A$ with boundary.  Then $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$ are pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves on $X$.  Furthermore, there are canonical isomorphisms of sheaves on $X$:
\begin{align}
\label{EisoI}H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)_{IJ}&=j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_X\\
\label{EisoII}H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}
\end{align}
where $j:V_I\cap V_J\hookrightarrow X$ for $V_I:=\psi_{\varnothing I}((s_I|X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{-1}(0))\subseteq X$ (an open subset).

\label{localdescofISO}
Let $X$ be a space with finite locally orientable implicit atlas $A$ of dimension $d$ with boundary.  Consider the following maps of complexes of $\mathcal K$-presheaves on $X$:
\begin{align}
C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K;A)_{II}&\leftarrow\left[\begin{matrix}C_{d+\dim E_A-1-\bullet}(E_{A\setminus I}\times\partial X_I^\mathrm{reg},E_{A\setminus I}\times(\partial X_I^\mathrm{reg}\setminus\psi_{\varnothing I}^{-1}(K));\mathfrak o_{E_A}^\vee)^{\Gamma_A}\hfill\cr\downarrow\cr C_{d+\dim E_A-\bullet}(E_{A\setminus I}\times X_I^\mathrm{reg},E_{A\setminus I}\times(X_I^\mathrm{reg}\setminus\psi_{\varnothing I}^{-1}(K));\mathfrak o_{E_A}^\vee)^{\Gamma_A}\hfill\end{matrix}\right]\\
C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{II}&\leftarrow C_{d+\dim E_A-\bullet}(E_{A\setminus I}\times X_I^\mathrm{reg},E_{A\setminus I}\times(X_I^\mathrm{reg}\setminus\psi_{\varnothing I}^{-1}(K));\mathfrak o_{E_A}^\vee)^{\Gamma_A}
\end{align}
(induced by the corresponding maps on spaces).  These maps are quasi-isomorphisms, and the isomorphisms \eqref{EisoI}--\eqref{EisoII} from Lemma \ref{CSTpure} coincide with the Poincar\'e duality isomorphisms for the complexes on the right above.

\label{celllikelocalstructure}
Let $(M,\partial M)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ be cell-like.  Then:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{inbdry}If $\mathfrak s\prec\mathfrak t$ then $M^\mathfrak s\subseteq\partial M^{\leq\mathfrak t}$.
\item\label{toplessdim}If $M^\mathfrak s\ne\varnothing$, then $\dim\mathfrak s\leq\dim M$.
\item\label{topopen}If $\dim\mathfrak s=\dim M$, then $M^\mathfrak s\subseteq M$ is open.
\item\label{bdrystratumstruct}If $\dim\mathfrak s=\dim M-1$, $\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S$, and $M^\mathfrak s\ne\varnothing$, then $\#\mathcal S^{>\mathfrak s}=1$.
\item\label{intstratumstruct}If $\dim\mathfrak s=\dim M-1$, $\mathfrak s\notin\partial\mathcal S$, and $M^\mathfrak s\ne\varnothing$, then $\#\mathcal S^{>\mathfrak s}=2$, and these $M^{\leq\mathfrak t}$ give collars on either side of $M^\mathfrak s\subseteq M$ (so in particular $M^\mathfrak s\subseteq M$ is locally flat).
\end{rlist}

\label{cofibrantinjectivelemma}
Let $\mathcal W_\bullet$ be a cofibrant $\mathsf{F}$-module complex.  Then the map:
\begin{equation}\label{generalizedboundaryinjective}
\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\begin{smallmatrix}\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)\cr\mathfrak s\prec\mathfrak s_0\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)\rightarrowtail\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s_0)
\end{equation}
a cofibration for all $\mathfrak s_0\in\mathcal S(\sigma,p,q)$.

\label{cofibranthaslotsofcofibrations}
Let $\mathcal W_\bullet$ be a cofibrant $\mathsf{F}$-module complex.  Let $\mathcal S\subseteq\mathcal T\subseteq\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)$ be finite downward closed subsets.  Then the map:
\begin{equation}
\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)\rightarrowtail\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal T}\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)
\end{equation}
is a cofibration.  In particular, $\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal T}\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)$ is projective (take $\mathcal S=\varnothing$ above).

\label{Hzeroofcofibrantcolimit}
Let $\mathcal W_\bullet$ be a cofibrant $\mathsf{F}$-module complex over $\mathbb Z$ such that $H_i\mathcal W_\bullet=0$ for $i<0$.  Then for any finite downward closed subset $\mathcal S\subseteq\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)$, the map:
\begin{equation}\label{tocolimitsurjective}
H_i\bigoplus_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)\twoheadrightarrow H_i\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)
\end{equation}
is surjective for $i\leq 0$.  Moreover, both sides vanish for $i<0$.

\label{universalcoefficient}
Let $A_\bullet$ be a cofibrant complex over $\mathbb Z$.  Then there is a natural short exact sequence:
\begin{equation}
0\to\operatorname{Ext}^1(H_{i-1}A_\bullet,\mathbb Z)\to H^i\operatorname{Hom}(A_\bullet,\mathbb Z)\to\operatorname{Hom}(H_iA_\bullet,\mathbb Z)\to 0
\end{equation}

\label{cocycleextendinglemma}
Suppose we have the following commuting diagrams (solid arrows) of complexes over $\mathbb Z$ and their homology (where $\mathbb Z$ is concentrated in degree zero):
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
A_\bullet\ar[tail]{r}\ar{d}&\tilde A_\bullet\ar[dashed]{dl}\\
\mathbb Z
\end{tikzcd}
\overset{H_\bullet}\Longrightarrow
\begin{tikzcd}
H_\bullet A_\bullet\ar{r}\ar{d}&H_\bullet\tilde A_\bullet\ar{dl}\\
\mathbb Z
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $A_\bullet$ and $\tilde A_\bullet$ are cofibrant and $H_{-1}A_\bullet=0$.  Then there exists a dashed arrow compatible with the rest of the diagram.

\label{descentofdiagram}
Let $\pi:\tilde Z_\bullet\to Z_\bullet$ be a trivial Kan fibration of semisimplicial sets, and let $\widetilde\mathbb H:\tilde Z_\bullet\to\mathcal C$ be a diagram in some category $\mathcal C$.  A \emph{descent} of $\widetilde\mathbb H$ to $Z_\bullet$ is a diagram $\mathbb H:Z_\bullet\to\mathcal C$ along with an isomorphism $\widetilde\mathbb H\to\mathbb H\circ\pi$:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
\tilde Z_\bullet\arrow{r}{\widetilde\mathbb H}\arrow{d}[swap]{\pi}&\mathcal C\\
Z_\bullet\arrow[dashed]{ur}[swap]{\mathbb H}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Suppose that:
\begin{rlist}
\item$Z_\bullet$ is a simplicial set.
\item\label{degenerateIDindescent}For any edge $\sigma^1$ in $\tilde Z_\bullet$ whose endpoints coincide and which projects to a degenerate edge in $Z_\bullet$, the associated map in $\mathcal C$ is the identity map.
\end{rlist}
Then a descent exists and is unique up to unique isomorphism.

\label{trivialactiongysin}
Let $X$ be a trivial $S^1$-space.  Then the $\pi^!$ map in the Gysin sequence vanishes, turning it into a short exact sequence:
\begin{equation}
0\to H_\bullet(X)\xrightarrow{\pi_\ast}H_\bullet^{S^1}(X)\xrightarrow{\cap e}H_{\bullet-2}^{S^1}(X)\to 0
\end{equation}
The same statement applies to relative homology of trivial $S^1$-spaces.

\label{SSmanifoldPDbase}
Let $M$ be a topological $S^1$-manifold of dimension $d$ which is almost free near $p\in M$.  Suppose that $\#(S^1)_p$ is invertible in the ground ring $R$.  Then there is a canonical isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
H_\bullet^{S^1}(M,M\setminus S^1p)=\begin{cases}H^1(S^1p;\mathfrak o_M)&\bullet=d-1\cr 0&\bullet\ne d-1\end{cases}
\end{equation}
(note also that $H^1(S^1p;\mathfrak o_M)=H^0(S^1p;\mathfrak o_M\otimes\mathfrak o_{S^1p}^\vee)$).

\label{CSTpureSS}
Let $X$ be an almost free $S^1$-space with finite locally $S^1$-orientable $S^1$-equivariant implicit atlas with boundary $A$.  Then $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$ are pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves on $X/S^1$.  Furthermore, there are canonical isomorphisms of sheaves on $X/S^1$:
\begin{align}
H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}&=\pi_\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_X\\
H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\pi_\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}
\end{align}
where $j:V_I\cap V_J\hookrightarrow X$ for $V_I:=\psi_{\varnothing I}((s_I|X_I^\mathrm{reg})^{-1}(0))\subseteq X$ and $\pi:X\to X/S^1$.

\label{everythingcanbethickened}
We have:
\begin{equation}
\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)=\bigcup_{I\subseteq A^\mathrm{GW}}\psi_{\varnothing I}((s_I|\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_I^\mathrm{reg})^{-1}(0))
\end{equation}

\label{morseistransverse}
Fix a Morse function $H_0:M\to\mathbb R$ whose gradient flow is Morse--Smale and suppose $H=\epsilon\cdot H_0$ with $\epsilon>0$ sufficiently small.  Then $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{S^1}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)$ is open and cut out transversally (meaning $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{S^1}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^\mathrm{reg}$).  Hence we have a partition into disjoint closed subsets:
\begin{equation}
\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)=\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{S^1}\sqcup[\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)\setminus\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{S^1}]
\end{equation}

\label{opencptshvequivalence}
We have:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}\operatorname{Shv}_\mathcal K X\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{\alpha_\ast}&\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{\alpha^\ast}\operatorname{Shv} X\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
and this is an equivalence of categories.\footnote{A similar result appears in Lurie \cite[Corollary 7.3.4.10]{luriehtt}.}

\label{anycompactintersectionworksSH}
Let $\{K_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ be a filtered directed system of compact subsets of $X$ (i.e.\ for all $\alpha,\beta\in A$ there exists $\gamma\in A$ with $K_\gamma\subseteq K_\alpha\cap K_\beta$).  Then for any $\mathcal F$ satisfying \eqref{sheafcontinuity}, the following is an isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\varinjlim_{\alpha\in A}\mathcal F(K_\alpha)\to\mathcal F\Bigl(\bigcap_{\alpha\in A}K_\alpha\Bigr)
\end{equation}

\label{anycompactintersectionworks}
Let $\{K_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ be a filtered directed system of compact subsets of $X$ (i.e.\ for all $\alpha,\beta\in A$ there exists $\gamma\in A$ with $K_\gamma\subseteq K_\alpha\cap K_\beta$).  Then for any $\mathcal F^\bullet$ satisfying \eqref{homotopysheafcontinuity}, the following is a quasi-isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\varinjlim_{\alpha\in A}\mathcal F^\bullet(K_\alpha)\to\mathcal F^\bullet\Bigl(\bigcap_{\alpha\in A}K_\alpha\Bigr)
\end{equation}

\label{cechcohomologyfinitequotient}
Let $X$ be a space and let $\pi:X\to X/\Gamma$ be the quotient map under a finite group action.  Let $\mathcal F$ be any sheaf of $\mathbb Z[\frac 1{\#\Gamma}]$-modules on $X/\Gamma$.  Then the following maps are all isomorphisms:
\begin{align*}
\check H^\bullet(X/\Gamma;\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet(X/\Gamma;(\pi_\ast\pi^\ast\mathcal F)^\Gamma)\to\check H^\bullet(X/\Gamma;\pi_\ast\pi^\ast\mathcal F)^\Gamma\to\check H^\bullet(X;\pi^\ast\mathcal F)^\Gamma\\
\check H^\bullet_c(X/\Gamma;\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet_c(X/\Gamma;(\pi_\ast\pi^\ast\mathcal F)^\Gamma)\to\check H^\bullet_c(X/\Gamma;\pi_\ast\pi^\ast\mathcal F)^\Gamma\to\check H^\bullet_c(X;\pi^\ast\mathcal F)^\Gamma
\end{align*}
(here we note that by functoriality, $\pi^\ast\mathcal F$ is $\Gamma$-equivariant, and thus $\Gamma$ acts on $\pi_\ast\pi^\ast\mathcal F$).

\label{cechsimplebasic}
Let $\mathcal F^\bullet$ be a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.  Then:
\begin{rlist}
\item(Strong vanishing) $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet=0$ for $i<0$.
\item$H^0\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a $\mathcal K$-sheaf.
\end{rlist}

\label{singularchainshomotopyKsheaf}
Let $X$ be a topological space.  Then we have:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{scacyc}$C_\bullet(X,X)$ is acyclic.
\item\label{scpatch}Let $A,B\subseteq X$ be closed.  Then the complex:
\begin{equation*}
\bigl[C_\bullet(X,X\setminus(A\cup B))\to C_{\bullet+1}(X,X\setminus A)\oplus C_{\bullet+1}(X,X\setminus B)\to C_{\bullet+2}(X,X\setminus(A\cap B))\bigr]
\end{equation*}
is acyclic.
\item\label{sccont}Let $K=\bigcap_{\alpha\in A}K_\alpha$ where $\{K_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ is a family of closed subsets of $X$ which is \emph{filtered} in the sense that for all $\alpha_1,\alpha_2\in A$, there exists $\beta\in A$ with $K_\beta\subseteq K_{\alpha_1}\cap K_{\alpha_2}$.  Then $\varinjlim_{\alpha\in A}C_\bullet(X,X\setminus K_\alpha)\to C_\bullet(X,X\setminus K)$ is a quasi-isomorphism.
\end{rlist}

\label{poincareduality}
Let $M$ be a topological manifold of dimension $n$ with boundary.  Let $i:X\hookrightarrow M$ be a closed subset.  Let $N\subseteq\partial M$ (closed) be a tamely embedded codimension zero submanifold with boundary.\footnote{In other words, $N\subseteq\partial M$ is a closed subset which locally looks like either $\varnothing\subseteq\mathbb R^{n-1}$, $\mathbb R_{\geq 0}\times\mathbb R^{n-2}\subseteq\mathbb R^{n-1}$, or $\mathbb R^{n-1}\subseteq\mathbb R^{n-1}$.}  Then there is a canonical isomorphism:
\begin{equation}\label{poincaredualitygoal}
H^\bullet[C_{n-1-\bullet}(N,N\setminus X)\to C_{n-\bullet}(M,M\setminus X)]=\check H^\bullet_c(X;i^\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_M)
\end{equation}
where $j:M^\circ\cup N^\circ\hookrightarrow M$.  In particular, specializing to $N=\varnothing$, there is a canonical isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
H_{n-\bullet}(M,M\setminus X)=\check H^\bullet_c(X;i^\ast\mathfrak o_{M\operatorname{rel}\partial M})
\end{equation}

\label{gluingcechsimple}
Let $A$ be a finite set.  Let $\{U_I\}_{I\subseteq A}$ be an open cover of a space $X$ satisfying:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{nestedintersectionOK}$U_I\cap U_K\subseteq U_J$ for $I\subseteq J\subseteq K$.
\item\label{nonnestedintersectionbig}$U_I\cap U_{I'}\subseteq U_{I\cup I'}$ for $I,I'\subseteq A$.
\end{rlist}
Let $\mathcal G$ be a sheaf on $X$ and let $\mathcal G_{IJ}:=(j_{IJ})_!(j_{IJ})^\ast\mathcal G$ where $j_{IJ}:U_I\cap U_J\hookrightarrow X$.  By property (\ref{nestedintersectionOK}), this gives rise to a homotopy diagram $\{\mathcal G_{IJ}\}_{I\subseteq A}$ over $2^A$ of sheaves on $X$.

Let $\{\mathcal F_{IJ}^\bullet\}_{I\subseteq J\subseteq A}$ be a homotopy diagram over $2^A$ of pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves on $X$, and suppose we give a compatible system of isomorphisms $\mathcal G_{IJ}\xrightarrow\sim H^0\mathcal F^\bullet_{IJ}$.  Then $\mathcal F^\bullet:=\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{I\subseteq J\subseteq A}\mathcal F^\bullet_{IJ}$ is a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf and there is a canonical induced isomorphism $\mathcal G\xrightarrow\sim H^0\mathcal F^\bullet$.

\label{cofinalityforRlim}
Let $f:\Lambda'\to\Lambda$ be weakly increasing ($\lambda_1\leq\lambda_2\implies f(\lambda_1)\leq f(\lambda_2)$) and cofinal ($f(\Lambda')\subseteq\Lambda$ cofinal).  Then the following natural map is an isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\mathop{\textstyle\varprojlim^i}\limits_{\lambda\in\Lambda}A_\lambda\xrightarrow\sim\mathop{\textstyle\varprojlim^i}\limits_{\lambda'\in\Lambda'}A_{f(\lambda')}
\end{equation}
More generally, the following natural map is a quasi-isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\mathop{R\varprojlim}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}C^\bullet_\lambda\xrightarrow\sim\mathop{R\varprojlim}_{\lambda'\in\Lambda'}C^\bullet_{f(\lambda')}
\end{equation}

\label{quasiisomorphismforRlim}
If $C_\lambda^\bullet\xrightarrow\sim D_\lambda^\bullet$ is a morphism of inverse systems which is a level-wise quasi-isomorphism (i.e.\ for every $\lambda$), then the natural map:
\begin{equation}
\mathop{R\varprojlim}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}C^\bullet_\lambda\xrightarrow\sim\mathop{R\varprojlim}_{\lambda\in\Lambda}D^\bullet_\lambda
\end{equation}
is a quasi-isomorphism.

\label{inverselimithassamecech}
Let $X$ be compact.  There is a natural map:
\begin{equation}\label{chogoshvilinervecomparison}
\varprojlim_{X=\bigsqcup_{i=1}^nE_i}N(X;\{\overline{E_i}\})\to X
\end{equation}
which induces an isomorphism on \v Cech cohomology.

\label{steenrodisderiveddualofcech}
Let $\mathcal F$ be a locally constant sheaf whose stalks are finitely generated free $R$-modules, and let $M$ be an $R$-module.  Then there is a natural isomorphism:
\begin{equation}\label{strongcechdualiso}
\overline C_\bullet(X;\operatorname{Hom}(\mathcal F,M))=R\operatorname{Hom}_{D(R)}(\check C^\bullet(X;\mathcal F),M)
\end{equation}
in the derived category $D(R)$.  The same holds for relative (co)chains of a closed subspace $Y\subseteq X$, and the isomorphisms are compatible with the relevant exact triangles relating $Y$, $X$ and $(X,Y)$.

\label{linearizedformula}
The linearized operator $D_{\alpha,y}$ is given by:
\begin{align}\label{formulaforlinearizedoperator}
D_{\alpha,y}\xi&=\Bigl(\nabla\xi+T_\nabla(\xi,du_\alpha)\Bigr)^{0,1}_y\cr
&\quad+\sum_{i=1}^r\frac{d[\lambda(e_0)]}{dx_i}(\alpha,y,x_1,\ldots,x_r,\cdot,u_\alpha(\cdot))(-\operatorname{proj}_{TC_\alpha}\xi(x_i))\cr
&\quad+\nabla_\xi[\lambda(e_0)](\alpha,y,x_1,\ldots,x_r,\cdot,u_\alpha(\cdot))\cr
&\quad+\lambda(\operatorname{proj}_E\xi)(\alpha,y,x_1,\ldots,x_r,\cdot,u_\alpha(\cdot))
\end{align}
where $\operatorname{proj}:u_\alpha^\ast TX\to TC_\alpha$ denotes the projection associated to the splitting $T_{u_\alpha(x_i)}X=T_{u_\alpha(x_i)}D_i\oplus T_{x_i}C_\alpha$, and $\nabla_\xi[\lambda(e_0)]$ means covariant derivative in the direction of $\xi$ along the $X$ factor.

\label{fredholmandsamekernelcokernel}
Consider the following commutative square:
\begin{equation}\label{fredholmsquare}
\begin{tikzcd}
W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)_D\oplus E\ar{r}{D_{\alpha,y}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TX)\\
W^{k,2}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)_D\oplus E\ar[hook]{u}\ar{r}{D_{\alpha,y}}&W^{k-1,2}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TX)\ar[hook]{u}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Both horizontal operators are Fredholm, and the induced map from the kernel (resp.\ cokernel) of the bottom horizontal map to the kernel (resp.\ cokernel) of the top horizontal map is an isomorphism.

\label{mappregluingissmall}
We have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|\bar\partial_yu_\alpha+\lambda(e_0)(\alpha,y,x_1^0,\ldots,x_r^0,\cdot,u_\alpha(\cdot))\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}\leq c\cdot\left[\left|y\right|+\sum_{i=1}^de^{-(1-\delta)S_i}\right]
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{pregluingKestimate}
For all $\kappa\in K$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|D_{\alpha,y}\kappa_\alpha\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}\leq c\cdot\left[\left|y\right|+\sum_{i=1}^de^{-(1-\delta)S_i}\right]\left\|\kappa\right\|
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{approxbottom}
Let:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
X@>D>>Y\cr
@AGAA@VVBV\cr
X'@>D'>>Y'
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
denote the bottom square in \eqref{biginversediagram}.  Then for $\xi\in X'$ and $\eta\in Y$ with $D'\xi=B\eta$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|DG\xi-\eta\right\|\leq c\cdot\left\|\xi\right\|\left|y\right|
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{approxmiddle}
Let:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
X@>D>>Y\cr
@AGAA@VVBV\cr
X'@>D'>>Y'
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
denote the middle square in \eqref{biginversediagram}.  Then for $\xi\in X'$ and $\eta\in Y$ with $D'\xi=B\eta$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|DG\xi-\eta\right\|\leq c\cdot\left\|\xi\right\|\sum_{i=1}^de^{-S_i}
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{approxtop}
Let:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
X@>D>>Y\cr
@AGAA@VVBV\cr
X'@>D'>>Y'
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
denote the top square in \eqref{biginversediagram}.  Then for $\xi\in X'$ and $\eta\in Y$ with $D'\xi=B\eta$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|DG\xi-\eta\right\|\leq c\cdot\left\|\xi\right\|\sum_{i=1}^de^{-2\delta S_i}
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{bootstrapinverse}
Let $X$ and $Y$ be Banach spaces, let $D:X\to Y$ and $T:Y\to X$ be bounded, and suppose $\epsilon:=\left\|DT-\mathbf 1_Y\right\|<1$.  Then:
\begin{equation}
Q:=T\cdot\sum_{n\geq 0}(\mathbf 1_Y-DT)^n
\end{equation}
converges and satisfies $DQ=\mathbf 1_Y$.  In addition, we have the following estimates:
\begin{equation}
\left\|Q\right\|\leq\frac 1{1-\epsilon}\left\|T\right\|\qquad
\left\|Q-T\right\|\leq\frac\epsilon{1-\epsilon}\left\|T\right\|
\end{equation}

\label{bootstrappingapprox}
Suppose we have a diagram of Banach spaces as follows:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
X_1\ar{r}{D_1}&Y_1\ar{d}{B_1}\\
\vdots\ar{u}{G_1}&\vdots\ar{d}{B_{n-2}}\\
X_{n-1}\ar{u}{G_{n-2}}\ar{r}{D_{n-1}}&Y_{n-1}\ar{d}{B_{n-1}}\\
X_n\ar{u}{G_{n-1}}\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{D_n}&Y_n\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{Q_n}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $\left\|D_i\right\|,\left\|G_i\right\|,\left\|B_i\right\|,\left\|Q_n\right\|\leq c$ and $D_nQ_n=\mathbf 1$.  Then for all $\delta>0$ there exists $\epsilon=\epsilon(n,c,\delta)>0$ such that if for all $1<i\leq n$:
\begin{equation}\label{keytocheckforARI}
D_i\xi=B_{i-1}\eta\implies\left\|D_{i-1}G_{i-1}\xi-\eta\right\|\leq\epsilon\cdot\left\|\xi\right\|
\end{equation}
then we have:
\begin{equation}\label{approxbootstrappingworks}
\left\|D_1G_1\cdots G_{n-1}Q_nB_{n-1}\cdots B_1-\mathbf 1_{Y_1}\right\|\leq\delta
\end{equation}

\label{contractionprop}
There exists $c'>0$ (depending on data which has been previously fixed) such that for sufficiently small $(\alpha,y)$:
\begin{rlist}
\item The map $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}$ is defined for $\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$.
\item For $\xi_1-\xi_2\in\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}$ and $\left\|\xi_1\right\|_{k,2,\delta},\left\|\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|(\xi_1-\xi_2)-(Q_{\alpha,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi_1-Q_{\alpha,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi_2)\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq\frac 12\left\|\xi_1-\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta}
\end{equation}
\end{rlist}

\label{energydecayexponential}
For all $\mu<1$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ with the following property.  Let $u:[-R,R]\times S^1\to B^{2n}(1)$ be $J$-holomorphic for an almost complex structure $J$ on $B^{2n}(1)\subseteq\mathbb R^{2n}$ satisfying $\left\|J-J_\mathrm{std}\right\|\leq\epsilon$ (measured with respect to $g_\mathrm{std}$).  Then:
\begin{equation*}
\left(\int_{[-r,r]\times S^1}\biggl[\Bigl|\frac{du}{ds}\Bigr|^2+\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\biggr]\,ds\,dt\right)^{1/2}\leq c\cdot e^{\mu\cdot(r-R)}\left(\int_{\partial[-R,R]\times S^1}\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,dt\right)^{1/2}
\end{equation*}
for $0\leq r\leq R$.

\label{energycontrolsWoneinfty}
Let $J$ be an almost complex structure on $B^{2n}(1)\subseteq\mathbb R^{2n}$; there exist $\epsilon>0$ and $c<\infty$, depending only on $\left\|J\right\|_{C^2}$ (measured with respect to $g_\mathrm{std}$) with the following property.  Let $u:[0,1]\times[0,1]\to B^{2n}(1)$ be $J$-holomorphic, and suppose that:
\begin{equation}
\int_{[0,1]^2}\biggl[\Bigl|\frac{du}{dx}\Bigr|^2+\Bigl|\frac{du}{dy}\Bigr|^2\biggr]\,dx\,dy<\epsilon
\end{equation}
Then we have:
\begin{equation}
\Bigl|\frac{du}{dx}({\textstyle\frac 12},{\textstyle\frac 12})\Bigr|+\Bigl|\frac{du}{dy}({\textstyle\frac 12},{\textstyle\frac 12})\Bigr|\leq c\cdot\left(\int_{[0,1]^2}\biggl[\Bigl|\frac{du}{dx}\Bigr|^2+\Bigl|\frac{du}{dy}\Bigr|^2\biggr]\,dx\,dy\right)^{1/2}
\end{equation}

\label{Woneinftycontrolsall}
Let $u:[0,1]\times[0,1]\to B^{2n}(1)$ be $J$-holomorphic for an almost complex structure $J$ on $B^{2n}(1)\subseteq\mathbb R^{2n}$.  For all $k\geq 1$, there exists $c_k<\infty$ depending only on $\left\|J\right\|_{C^k}$ such that if:
\begin{equation}
\sup_{(x,y)\in[0,1]^2}\biggl[\Bigl|\frac{du}{dx}\Bigr|+\Bigl|\frac{du}{dy}\Bigr|\biggr]\leq 1
\end{equation}
then:
\begin{equation}
\left|(D^ku)({\textstyle\frac 12},{\textstyle\frac 12})\right|\leq c_k\cdot\sup_{(x,y)\in[0,1]^2}\biggl[\Bigl|\frac{du}{dx}\Bigr|+\Bigl|\frac{du}{dy}\Bigr|\biggr]
\end{equation}

\label{injectiveHdorffhomeo}
Let $X$ be a Hausdorff topological space and let $f:\mathbb R^n\to X$ be continuous.  Suppose that:
\begin{rlist}
\item$f$ is injective.
\item The restriction of $f$ to any neighborhood of zero is surjective onto a neighborhood of $f(0)$.
\end{rlist}
Then there is an open set $0\in U\subseteq\mathbb R^n$ such that $f(U)\subseteq X$ is open and $f:U\xrightarrow\sim f(U)$ is a homeomorphism.

\label{linearizedformulaHF}
The linearized operator $D_{\alpha,w,y}$ is given by:
\begin{align}\label{formulaforlinearizedoperatorHF}
D_{\alpha,w,y}\xi&=\biggl(\nabla^{\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot))}\xi+T_\nabla^{\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot))}(\xi,du_\alpha)\\
&\quad\quad+2d(\operatorname{proj}_{S^1}A_y)\otimes\nabla_\xi^{\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot))}X_{H((\ell_{\alpha,w}\times\operatorname{id}_{S^1})(A_y(\cdot)))}\\
\label{firstLterm}&\quad\quad+\sum_{i=1}^r\frac{d[\lambda(e_0)]}{dx^i}(\alpha,y,x^1,\ldots,x^r,\cdot,u_\alpha(\cdot))(-\operatorname{proj}_{TC_\alpha}\xi(x^i))\\
&\quad\quad+\nabla_\xi^{\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot))}[\lambda(e_0)](\alpha,y,x^1,\ldots,x^r,\cdot,u_\alpha(\cdot))\\
\label{lastLterm}&\quad\quad+\lambda(\operatorname{proj}_E\xi)(\alpha,y,x^1,\ldots,x^r,\cdot,u_\alpha(\cdot))\biggr)^{0,1}_{j_y,J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))}
\end{align}
where $\operatorname{proj}_{TC_\alpha}:T(M\times S^1\times\Delta^n)\to TC_\alpha$ denotes the projection associated to the splitting $T(M\times S^1\times\Delta^n)=TD_i\oplus TC_\alpha$ at the point $x^i\in C_\alpha$ and $(\operatorname{id}_{M\times S^1}\times\ell_{\alpha,w})((u_\alpha\times A_y)(x^i))\in M\times S^1\times\Delta^n$, and $\nabla_\xi[\lambda(e_0)]$ denotes the derivative in the direction of $\xi$ along the $M$ factor with respect to the connection $\nabla$.

\label{mappregluingissmallHF}
We have the following estimate on $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}(0)$:
\begin{multline}\label{mappregluingdelbar}
\biggl\|\Bigl(du_\alpha+2d(\operatorname{proj}_{S^1}A_y)\otimes X_{H((\ell_{\alpha,w}\times\operatorname{id}_{S^1})(A_y(\cdot)))}(u_\alpha)\\
{}+\lambda(e_0)(\alpha,y,x^1,\ldots,x^r,\cdot,u_\alpha(\cdot))\Bigr)^{0,1}_{j_y,J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))}\biggr\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}
\leq c\cdot\bigl(\left|\alpha\right|^\epsilon+\left|w\right|+\left|y\right|\bigr)
\end{multline}
uniformly over $(\alpha,w,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ and $\epsilon>0$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{pregluingKestimateHF}
For all $\kappa\in K$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|D_{\alpha,w,y}\kappa_\alpha\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}\leq c\cdot\bigl(\left|\alpha\right|^\epsilon+\left|w\right|+\left|y\right|\bigr)\left\|\kappa\right\|
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,w,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ and $\epsilon>0$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{approxbottomHF}
Let:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
X@>D>>Y\cr
@AGAA@VVBV\cr
X'@>D'>>Y'
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
denote the bottom square in \eqref{biginversediagramHF}.  Then for $\xi\in X'$ and $\eta\in Y$ with $D'\xi=B\eta$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|DG\xi-\eta\right\|\leq c\cdot\left|\alpha\right|^\epsilon\left\|\xi\right\|
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,w,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ and $\epsilon>0$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{approxmiddleHF}
Let:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
X@>D>>Y\cr
@AGAA@VVBV\cr
X'@>D'>>Y'
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
denote the middle square in \eqref{biginversediagramHF}.  Then for $\xi\in X'$ and $\eta\in Y$ with $D'\xi=B\eta$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|DG\xi-\eta\right\|\leq c\cdot\left|y\right|\left\|\xi\right\|
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,w,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ and $\epsilon>0$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{approxtopHF}
Let:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
X@>D>>Y\cr
@AGAA@VVBV\cr
X'@>D'>>Y'
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
denote the top square in \eqref{biginversediagramHF}.  Then for $\xi\in X'$ and $\eta\in Y$ with $D'\xi=B\eta$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|DG\xi-\eta\right\|\leq c\cdot\bigl(\left|\alpha\right|^\epsilon+\left|w\right|\bigr)\left\|\xi\right\|
\end{equation}
uniformly over $(\alpha,w,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ and $\epsilon>0$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{contractionpropHF}
There exists $c'>0$ (depending on data which has been previously fixed) such that for sufficiently small $(\alpha,w,y)$:
\begin{rlist}
\item The map $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}$ is defined for $\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}\leq c'$.
\item For $\xi_1-\xi_2\in\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}$ and $\left\|\xi_1\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta},\left\|\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}\leq c'$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\left\|(\xi_1-\xi_2)-(Q_{\alpha,w,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi_1-Q_{\alpha,w,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi_2)\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}\leq\frac 12\left\|\xi_1-\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}
\end{equation}
\end{rlist}

\label{Ltwocontrolsinnbhd}
Let $u:[0,1]\times[0,1]\to B^{2n}(1)$ be $J$-holomorphic with respect to some smooth almost complex structure $J$ on the unit ball $B^{2n}(1)\subseteq\mathbb R^{2n}$.  There exists $\epsilon>0$ (depending only on upper bounds on $\left\|J\right\|_{C^\ell}$ and $\left\|u\right\|_{C^\ell}$ for some absolute $\ell<\infty$) with the following property.  Suppose that $\xi:[0,1]\times[0,1]\to\mathbb R^{2n}$ with $|\xi|<\epsilon$ pointwise is such that $u+\xi$ has image contained in $B^{2n}(1)$ and is $J$-holomorphic.  Then:
\begin{equation}
\left|D^k\xi(0,0)\right|\leq c_k\biggl(\int_{[0,1]\times[0,1]}\left|\xi(x,y)\right|^2\,dx\,dy\biggr)^{1/2}
\end{equation}
for all $k<\infty$ for constants $c_k<\infty$ depending only on upper bounds on $\left\|J\right\|_{C^\ell}$ and $\left\|u\right\|_{C^\ell}$ for some $\ell=\ell(k)<\infty$.

proposition.raw:
\label{Cpure}
Let $X$ be a space with finite locally orientable implicit atlas $A$ with boundary.  Then $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)$ and $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)$ are pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves on $X$.  Furthermore, there are canonical isomorphisms of sheaves on $X$:
\begin{align}
\label{FisoI}H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)&=\mathfrak o_X\\
\label{FisoII}H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)&=\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}
\end{align}

\label{compatibilityoflongexactsequence}
The following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation}\label{compatibilityoflesdiagram}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofF}}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X;A)\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofF}}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\partial X;A)\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofF}}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&H^{\bullet+1}_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}\\
\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofO}}&\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_X)\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofO}}&\check H^\bullet(\partial X;\mathfrak o_{\partial X})\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofO}}&\check H^{\bullet+1}(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}

\label{stratifiedispurehomotopysheaf}
Let $(X,\partial X)\to(\mathcal S,\partial\mathcal S)$ be equipped with a finite locally orientable implicit atlas with boundary and cell-like stratification $A$.  Then \eqref{stratifiedtounstratifiedpushforward} is a quasi-isomorphism, and the following diagram of sheaves on $X$ commutes:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-,\mathcal S;A)\ar{d}\ar{r}{\eqref{stratifiedtounstratifiedpushforward}}&H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)\ar{r}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}&\mathfrak o_X\ar{d}\\
H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\cap X^{\leq\mathfrak s},\mathcal S^{\leq\mathfrak s};A)\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\ar{r}{\eqref{stratifiedtounstratifiedpushforward}}&H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\cap X^{\leq\mathfrak s};A)\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\ar{r}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}&\mathfrak o_{X^{\leq\mathfrak s}}\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\\
H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\cap X^{\leq\mathfrak s}\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\ar{u}\ar{rr}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}&&\mathfrak o_{X^{\leq\mathfrak s}\operatorname{rel}\partial}\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathfrak s\ar{u}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}

\label{trivialKanfibration}
The map $\pi:\tilde Z_\bullet\to Z_\bullet$ has the right lifting property with respect to the boundary inclusions $\partial\Delta^n\hookrightarrow\Delta^n$ for all $n\geq 0$ (where $\Delta^n$ is the semisimplicial $n$-simplex).  In other words, given any commuting diagram of solid arrows below:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
\partial\Delta^n\arrow{r}\arrow{d}&\tilde Z_\bullet\arrow{d}\\
\Delta^n\arrow{r}\arrow[dashrightarrow]{ur}&Z_\bullet
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
there exists a dashed arrow making the diagram commute.

\label{degenerateID}
Let $Z_\bullet=\ast$ be the semisimplicial set with a single simplex $\sigma^i$ in dimension $i$ for all $i\geq 0$ (i.e.\ the simplicial $0$-simplex).  Let $\mathcal X/Z_\bullet$ be an $H$-equivariant flow category diagram with implicit atlas and coherent orientations.  Suppose that:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{identitytransverse}$\mathcal X(\sigma^1,p,p)=\mathcal X(\sigma^1,p,p)^\mathrm{reg}$ is a single point and $\omega(\sigma^1,p,p)=1$, for all $p$.
\item\label{highernonemptyissameaszero}$\mathcal X(\sigma^0,p,q)=\varnothing\implies\mathcal X(\sigma^i,p,q)=\varnothing$ for all $i$ and all $p\ne q$.
\end{rlist}
Then for any $\sigma\in\tilde Z_1$ whose two vertices coincide, the associated map in $\widetilde\mathbb H(\mathcal X)$ is homotopic to the identity map.

\label{iftransversejustcountzero}
Let $Z_\bullet$, $\mathcal X/Z_\bullet$, $\mathcal A$, and $\omega$ be as in Definition \ref{defofhomologygroups}.  Suppose that $\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)=\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)^\mathrm{reg}$ for all $(\sigma,p,q)$ (so, in particular, $\mathcal X/Z_\bullet$ is Morse--Smale in the sense of Remark \ref{MSflowcatediag}).

Define a diagram $\mathbb H'(\mathcal X):Z_\bullet\to\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{dg}}(\mathsf{Ch}_{R[[H]]})$ as follows.  The graded $R[[H]]$-module associated to a vertex $z$ of $\tilde Z_\bullet$ is $R[[\mathcal P_z]]$.  For a simplex $\sigma\in\tilde Z_n$, we define the map $f_\sigma:R[[\mathcal P_{\sigma(0)}]]\to R[[\mathcal P_{\sigma(n)}]]$ by the formula:
\begin{equation}\label{diagramndgformulaMS}
f_\sigma(p):=\sum_{\begin{smallmatrix}q\in\mathcal P_{\sigma(n)}\cr\operatorname{gr}(q)-\operatorname{gr}(p)=1-\dim\sigma\end{smallmatrix}}\langle\omega(\sigma,p,q),[\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)]\rangle\cdot q
\end{equation}
It is easy to verify that the maps $f_\sigma$ are well-defined and satisfy \eqref{Ndgmaster}.

Now there is a canonical isomorphism $\mathbb H(\mathcal X)=\mathbb H'(\mathcal X)$ (where on the right hand side we implicitly compose with the forgetful functor $\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{dg}}\to H^0$).

\label{CpureSS}
Let $X$ be an almost free $S^1$-space with finite locally $S^1$-orientable $S^1$-equivariant implicit atlas with boundary $A$.  Then $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)$ and $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)$ are pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves on $X/S^1$.  Furthermore, there are canonical isomorphisms of sheaves on $X/S^1$:
\begin{align}
H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)&=\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_X\\
H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)&=\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}
\end{align}
where $\pi:X\to X/S^1$.

\label{inductionformodels}
Let $\mathcal X/Z_\bullet$ be a flow category diagram.  There exists an $\mathsf{F}$-module of MF-sets $\mathcal Y$ and an $\mathsf{F}$-module of $S^1$-MF-sets $\mathcal Y_{S^1}$ (both supported inside $\operatorname{supp}\mathcal X$) along with a morphism $\mathcal Y_{S^1}\to\mathcal Y$, satisfying the following property.  For all $(\sigma,p,q)\in\operatorname{supp}\mathcal X$, both $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$ and $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)$ are saturated, and the tautologous maps:
\begin{align}
\label{colimitYone}\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)&\hookrightarrow\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)\\
\label{colimitYtwo}\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)}\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)&\hookrightarrow\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)
\end{align}
are injective (and the colimits on the left exist).  Moreover, such $\mathcal Y_{S^1}\to\mathcal Y$ may be constructed by induction on $(\sigma,p,q)$, partially ordered by $\preceq_\mathcal X$ ($\preceq_\mathcal X$ is well-founded, see Definition \ref{cofibrantreplacements} or the proof of Proposition \ref{trivialKanfibration}).

\label{GWgluingneeded}
For all $I\subseteq J\subseteq A^\mathrm{GW}$, we have:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{GWgluingopen}$\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)^\mathrm{reg}_I\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_I$ is an open subset.
\item\label{GWgluingsubmersion}The map $s_{J\setminus I}:\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_J\to E_{J\setminus I}$ is locally modeled on the projection:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb R^{\operatorname{vdim}\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)+\dim E_I}\times\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}\to\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}
\end{equation}
over $\psi_{IJ}^{-1}(\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_I^\mathrm{reg}))\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_J$.
\item\label{GWgluingor}There is a canonical identification of the orientation local system $\mathfrak o_{\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_I^\mathrm{reg}}$ with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}$ (by the standard reduction to the canonical orientation of a complex linear Fredholm operator as in McDuff--Salamon \cite{mcduffsalamonJholquant,mcduffsalamonJholsymp}).
\end{rlist}

\label{HFgluingneeded}
For all $I\subseteq J\subseteq\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s}$, we have:
\begin{rlist}
\item$(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I$ is an open subset.
\item The map $s_{J\setminus I}:\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_J\to E_{J\setminus I}$ over the locus $\psi_{IJ}^{-1}((\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\leq\mathfrak s})^\mathrm{reg})\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_J$ is locally modeled on the projection:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb R^{\operatorname{vdim}\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}+\dim E_I}\times\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}\to\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}
\end{equation}
over the top stratum $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_{\overline{\mathcal M}}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}$.  More generally, the local model (compatible with stratifications) is given by replacing the first factor on the left by $\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^n\times\mathbb R^{n'}$ stratified appropriately by $\mathcal S_{\overline{\mathcal M}}(\sigma,p,q)$.
\item There exist $\pi$-invariant coherent trivializations of the local systems $\mathfrak o_{(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_I}^\vee$ (in the sense of Definition \ref{cohordefabstract}).
\end{rlist}

\label{HFgluingSS}
For all $I\subseteq J\subseteq\mathcal B(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s}$, we have:
\begin{rlist}
\item$(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I$ is an open subset.
\item The map $s_{J\setminus I}:\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_J\to E_{J\setminus I}$ over the locus $\psi_{IJ}^{-1}((\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\leq\mathfrak s})^\mathrm{reg})\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_J$ is locally modeled on the projection:
\begin{equation}
\mathbb R^{\operatorname{vdim}\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}+\dim E_I}\times\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}\to\mathbb R^{\dim E_{J\setminus I}}
\end{equation}
over the top stratum $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_{\overline{\mathcal M}}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}$.  More generally, the local model (compatible with stratifications) is given by replacing the first factor on the left by $\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^n\times\mathbb R^{n'}$ stratified appropriately by $\mathcal S_{\overline{\mathcal M}}(\sigma,p,q)$.
\item There exist $\pi$-invariant coherent trivializations of the local systems $\mathfrak o_{(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_I}^\vee$ (in the sense of Definition \ref{cohordefabstract}), agreeing by restriction with those for $\mathcal A$, and coinciding with the usual orientations from Morse theory on $\overline{\mathcal M}_\mathrm{morse}(\sigma,p,q)=\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{S^1}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^\mathrm{reg}$.
\end{rlist}

\label{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}
Let $\mathcal F^\bullet$ be a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.  Then there is a canonical isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet(X)=\check H^\bullet(X;H^0\mathcal F^\bullet)
\end{equation}
More generally, let $[\mathcal F^\bullet_0\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-1}_1\to\cdots\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-n}_n]$ be a complex of $\mathcal K$-presheaves where each $\mathcal F^\bullet_i$ is a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.  Then there is a canonical isomorphism:
\begin{equation}\label{cechsimplecanonicaliso}
H^\bullet[\mathcal F^\bullet_0(X)\to\cdots\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-n}_n(X)]=\check H^\bullet(X;[H^0\mathcal F^\bullet_0\to\cdots\to(H^0\mathcal F^\bullet_n)[-n]])
\end{equation}

\label{approxworks}
We have:
\begin{align}
\left\|T_{\alpha,y}\right\|&\leq c\\
\label{Tapproxeqn}\left\|D_{\alpha,y} T_{\alpha,y}-\mathbf 1\right\|&\to 0\\
\operatorname{im} T_{\alpha,y}&\subseteq\ker L_\alpha
\end{align}
as $(\alpha,y)\to 0$, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{Qestimates}
We have:
\begin{align}
\left\|Q_{\alpha,y}\right\|&\leq c\\
D_{\alpha,y}Q_{\alpha,y}&=\mathbf 1\\
\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}&\subseteq\ker L_\alpha
\end{align}
uniformly over $(\alpha,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{quadestimate}
There exist $c'>0$ and $c<\infty$ (depending on data which has been previously fixed) such that for $\left\|\xi_1\right\|_{k,2,\delta},\left\|\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$, we have:
\begin{equation}\label{quadestgoal}
\bigl\|D_{\alpha,y}(\xi_1-\xi_2)-(\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi_1-\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi_2)\bigr\|_{k-1,2,\delta}\leq c\cdot\left\|\xi_1-\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta}(\left\|\xi_1\right\|_{k,2,\delta}+\left\|\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta})
\end{equation}
(and $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi_1$ and $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi_2$ are both defined), uniformly over $(\alpha,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero.

\label{newtoniteration}
There exists $c'>0$ (depending on data which has been previously fixed) so that for $(\alpha,y,\kappa\in K)$ sufficiently small, there exists a unique $\kappa_{\alpha,y}\in W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)_D\oplus E$ satisfying:
\begin{align}
\kappa_{\alpha,y}&\in\kappa_\alpha+\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}\\
\left\|\kappa_{\alpha,y}\right\|_{k,2,\delta}&\leq c'\\
\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\kappa_{\alpha,y}&=0
\end{align}

\label{neckestimate}
For all $\mu<1$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ with the following property.  Let $J$ be a smooth almost complex structure on $B^{2n}(1)\subseteq\mathbb R^{2n}$ satisfying $\left\|J-J_\mathrm{std}\right\|_{C^2}\leq\epsilon$ (measured with respect to $g_\mathrm{std}$).  There exists $c_k<\infty$ depending only on $\left\|J\right\|_{C^k}$ with the following property.  Let $u:[-R,R]\times S^1\to B^{2n}(1)$ be $J$-holomorphic.  If:
\begin{equation}
\int_{\partial[-R,R]\times S^1}\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,dt<\epsilon
\end{equation}
Then:
\begin{equation}
\left|(D^ku)(s,t)\right|\leq c_k\cdot e^{\mu\cdot(\left|s\right|-R)}\left(\int_{\partial[-R,R]\times S^1}\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,dt\right)^{1/2}
\end{equation}
for $\left|s\right|\leq R-1$.

\label{approxworksHF}
We have:
\begin{align}
\left\|T_{\alpha,w,y}\right\|&\leq c\\
\label{TapproxeqnHF}\left\|D_{\alpha,w,y} T_{\alpha,w,y}-\mathbf 1\right\|&\to 0\\
\operatorname{im} T_{\alpha,w,y}&\subseteq\ker L_\alpha
\end{align}
as $(\alpha,w,y)\to 0$, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{QestimatesHF}
We have:
\begin{align}
\left\|Q_{\alpha,w,y}\right\|&\leq c\\
D_{\alpha,w,y}Q_{\alpha,w,y}&=\mathbf 1\\
\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}&\subseteq\ker L_\alpha
\end{align}
uniformly over $(\alpha,w,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero, for $c<\infty$ depending on data which has been previously fixed.

\label{quadestimateHF}
There exist $c'>0$ and $c<\infty$ (depending on data which has been previously fixed) such that for $\left\|\xi_1\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta},\left\|\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}\leq c'$, we have:
\begin{equation}\label{quadestgoalHF}
\bigl\|D_{\alpha,w,y}(\xi_1-\xi_2)-(\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi_1-\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi_2)\bigr\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}\leq c\cdot\left\|\xi_1-\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}(\left\|\xi_1\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}+\left\|\xi_2\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta})
\end{equation}
(and $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi_1$ and $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi_2$ are both defined), uniformly over $(\alpha,w,y)$ in a neighborhood of zero.

\label{newtoniterationHF}
There exists $c'>0$ (depending on data which has been previously fixed) so that for $(\alpha,w,y,\kappa\in K)$ sufficiently small, there exists a unique $\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}\in W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E$ satisfying:
\begin{align}
\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}&\in\kappa_\alpha+\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}\\
\left\|\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}&\leq c'\\
\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}&=0
\end{align}

\label{neckestimateHF}
Let $(M,\omega)$ be a symplectic manifold, $J$ an $\omega$-compatible almost complex structure on $M$, $H:M\times S^1\to\mathbb R$ a Hamiltonian, and $\gamma:S^1\to M$ a non-degenerate periodic orbit of $H$.  We consider the (unbounded) asymptotic linearized operator:
\begin{align}
L^2(S^1,\gamma^\ast TM)&\to L^2(S^1,\gamma^\ast TM)\\
\xi&\mapsto J\mathcal L_{X_H}\xi
\end{align}
where $\mathcal L_{X_H}$ denotes the symplectic connection on $\gamma^\ast TM$ induced by $X_H$, and we use the standard inner product $g(\cdot,\cdot):=\omega(\cdot,J\cdot)$ on $\gamma^\ast TM$ for the inner product on $L^2(S^1,\gamma^\ast TM)$.  This operator is self-adjoint; we denote by $\delta>0$ the smallest magnitude of any of its eigenvalues ($\delta$ is positive since the orbit is non-degenerate).

We consider (partially defined) sections $\xi:S^1\times\mathbb R\to\gamma^\ast TM$ with $\left|\xi(t,s)\right|<\epsilon$ such that $u:=\exp_\gamma\xi:S^1\times\mathbb R\to M$ satisfies $(du+2dt\otimes X_H(u))^{0,1}_J=0$.  Now for all $\mu<1$, there exists $\epsilon>0$ such that we have the following estimates.

Suppose that $\xi$ as above is defined on $S^1\times[0,\infty)$.  Then:
\begin{equation}
\left|D^k\xi\right|\leq c_k\cdot e^{-\mu\delta s}\left(\int_{S^1}\bigl|\xi(t,0)\bigr|^2\,dt\right)^{1/2}\qquad s\geq 1
\end{equation}
for all $k\geq 0$.  A symmetric statement holds for $u$ and $\xi$ defined over $S^1\times(-\infty,0]$.

Suppose that $\xi$ as above is defined on $S^1\times[0,N]$.  Then:
\begin{equation*}
\left|D^k\xi\right|\leq c_k\cdot\left[e^{-\mu\delta s}\left(\int_{S^1}\bigl|\xi(t,0)\bigr|^2\,dt\right)^{1/2}+e^{-\mu\delta(N-s)}\left(\int_{S^1}\bigl|\xi(t,N)\bigr|^2\,dt\right)^{1/2}\right]\qquad 1\leq s\leq N-1
\end{equation*}
for all $k\geq 0$.

\label{gluingHForientations}
The orientation sheaf (in the sense of Definition \ref{IAorientationsheaf}) of every moduli space $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)$ can be canonically identified with $\mathfrak o_\sigma\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}$.  Moreover, under these identifications, the boundary maps on orientation sheaves induced (as in \eqref{sesofO}) by the structure maps:
\begin{align}
\label{hforproduct}\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[0\ldots k],p,q)\times\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[k\ldots n],q,r)&\to\partial\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)\\
\label{hforface}\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[0\ldots\hat k\ldots n],p,q)&\to\partial\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)
\end{align}
coincide with the maps:
\begin{align}
\label{hforproductor}\mathfrak o_\sigma\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,r}&\to\mathfrak o_{\sigma|[0\ldots k]}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}\otimes\mathfrak o_{\sigma|[k\ldots n]}\otimes\mathfrak o_{q,r}\\
\label{hforfaceor}\mathfrak o_\sigma\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}&\to\mathfrak o_{\sigma|[0\ldots\hat k\ldots n]}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}
\end{align}
induced by \eqref{simplexflowproductor}--\eqref{simplexflowfaceor} and \eqref{floerhoferoogluing}.

proof.raw:
Applying Lemmas \ref{singularchainshomotopyKsheaf} and \ref{associatedgradedhomotopysheaf}, we see that $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$ are both homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves.

Now let us calculate $H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K;A)_{IJ}$ and $H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$.  It follows from the submersion axiom that $X_{I,J,A}$ is a topological manifold of dimension $\operatorname{vdim}_AX+\dim E_A+\#I$ with boundary (not necessarily second countable or paracompact).\footnote{In fact, it carries a natural structure of a manifold with corners, which for the present purpose is mostly irrelevant.}  The boundary $\partial(X_{I,J,A})$ is a (not necessarily disjoint) union of two pieces, namely the locus where $x\in\partial X_J^\mathrm{reg}$ (which is precisely $(\partial X)_{I,J,A}$) and the locus where $t_\alpha=0$ for some $\alpha\in I$.  It is easy to see that the first piece $(\partial X)_{I,J,A}\subseteq\partial(X_{I,J,A})$ is a closed tamely embedded codimension zero submanifold with boundary.  Hence we may apply Poincar\'e--Lefschetz duality in the form of Lemma \ref{poincareduality} to see that:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H_c^{\bullet+\#I}(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J)\times\mathbb R_{>0}^I,\mathfrak o_\mathbb R^{\otimes I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{X_J^\mathrm{reg}\operatorname{rel}\partial})^{\Gamma_A}\\
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H_c^{\bullet+\#I}(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J)\times\mathbb R_{>0}^I,\mathfrak o_\mathbb R^{\otimes I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{X_J^\mathrm{reg}})^{\Gamma_A}
\end{align}
(the orientation sheaf of $X_{I,J,A}$ is given by $\mathfrak o_{X_J^\mathrm{reg}}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_A}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_\mathbb R^{\otimes I}$).  Now we apply the K\"unneth formula to conclude that:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J),\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{X_J^\mathrm{reg}\operatorname{rel}\partial})^{\Gamma_A}\\
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J),\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee\otimes\mathfrak o_{X_J^\mathrm{reg}})^{\Gamma_A}
\end{align}
Since $X$ is locally orientable, we have $\mathfrak o_{X_J^\mathrm{reg}}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_J}^\vee=\psi_{\varnothing J}^\ast\mathfrak o_X$, so we can write:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J),\psi_{\varnothing J}^\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})^{\Gamma_A}\\
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J),\psi_{\varnothing J}^\ast\mathfrak o_X)^{\Gamma_A}
\end{align}
Now $\Gamma_A$ acts through the projection $\Gamma_A\to\Gamma_J$, and $\psi_{\varnothing J}:\psi_{\varnothing J}^{-1}(K\cap V_I\cap V_J)\to K\cap V_I\cap V_J$ is exactly the quotient by $\Gamma_J$.  Hence by Lemma \ref{cechcohomologyfinitequotient} we get isomorphisms:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(K\cap V_I\cap V_J,\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\\
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet_c(K\cap V_I\cap V_J,\mathfrak o_X)
\end{align}
Now use Lemma \ref{cptcechembeddingproperty} to write this as:
\begin{align}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet(K,j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\\
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K;A)_{IJ}&=\check H^\bullet(K,j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_X)
\end{align}
Thus $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$ are both pure, and we manifestly have the desired isomorphisms \eqref{EisoI}--\eqref{EisoII}.

By the definition of the vertical identifications, the commutativity of the first two squares of \eqref{compatibilityoflesdiagram} reduces to the commutativity of the diagram of sheaves:
\begin{equation}\label{sheafcommutativity}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofF}}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}&H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofF}}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}&H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{Cpure}}}\\
\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofO}}&\mathfrak o_X\ar{r}{\eqref{sesofO}}&i_\ast\mathfrak o_{\partial X}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
It suffices to check commutativity on stalks, and to do this, we can work with the complexes on the right in Lemma \ref{localdescofISO}, for which the commutativity is clear.

In fact, the commutativity of the last square of \eqref{compatibilityoflesdiagram} is also a consequence of the commutativity of \eqref{sheafcommutativity}.  To see this, consider the following diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = small]
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\partial X;A)\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}}}&\ar{l}[swap]{\sim}H^\bullet[C^{\bullet+1}_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\to C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X;A)]\ar{r}\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}}}&H^{\bullet+1}_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{d}{\text{Prop \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}}}\\
\check H^\bullet(\partial X;H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A))\ar{d}{\sim}&\ar{l}[swap]{\sim}\check H^\bullet(X;[H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)[1]\to H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)])\ar{r}\ar{d}{\sim}&\check H^{\bullet+1}(X,H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A))\ar{d}{\sim}\\
\check H^\bullet(\partial X;\mathfrak o_{\partial X})&\ar{l}[swap]{\sim}\check H^\bullet(X;[\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial}[1]\to\mathfrak o_X])\ar{r}&\check H^{\bullet+1}(X,\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
The middle vertical arrow is defined because of the commutativity of the left square in \eqref{sheafcommutativity}.  The top left horizontal arrow is a quasi-isomorphism, and thus every left horizontal arrow is a quasi-isomorphism.  The bottom left square is commutative because of the commutativity of the right square in \eqref{sheafcommutativity}; the other squares are trivially commutative.  Reversing the left horizontal arrows, we see that the outermost square commutes, and this is exactly the last square in \eqref{compatibilityoflesdiagram}.

We may assume that $A$ is finite.  Certainly there is a map $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A')\to C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)$ which respects the map \eqref{Fpushforward}.  Hence it suffices to show that the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A')\ar{d}\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\ar[equal]{d}\\
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where the left vertical arrow is induced by the obvious pushforward on chains.  By definition of the isomorphisms above, it suffices to check commutativity of the corresponding diagram of sheaves with $\bullet=0$.  This can be checked locally, where it is clear.

We may assume that $A$ is finite.  There is a natural isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\sqcup Y\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)=C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\oplus C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(Y\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)
\end{equation}
compatible with the map to $C_{-\bullet}(E;A)$.  Thus it suffices to show that the following square of isomorphisms commutes:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\sqcup Y\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{d}\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&\check H^\bullet(X\sqcup Y;\mathfrak o_{X\sqcup Y\operatorname{rel}\partial})\ar[equal]{d}\\
H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\oplus H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(Y\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\oplus\check H^\bullet(Y;\mathfrak o_{Y\operatorname{rel}\partial})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
By definition of the horizontal maps, it suffices to check the commutativity of the corresponding diagram of sheaves on $X\sqcup Y$, which is clear.

We may replace $A$ with the finite subatlas $\varnothing\subseteq A$.  Now in this case, we have:
\begin{equation}
C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;\varnothing)=C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;\varnothing)_{\varnothing\varnothing}=C_{d-\bullet}(X_{\varnothing,\varnothing,\varnothing},X_{\varnothing,\varnothing,\varnothing}\setminus X_{\varnothing,\varnothing,\varnothing}^K)=C_{d-\bullet}(X,X\setminus K)
\end{equation}
It follows that the identification $\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})=H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;\varnothing)=H_{d-\bullet}(X)$ is simply the usual Poincar\'e duality isomorphism, and the map $H_{-\bullet}(X)\to H_{-\bullet}(E;\varnothing)\to R$ is the usual augmentation on $H_0$.  Hence the composition $\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\to R$ is pairing against the usual fundamental class of $X$.

The statement about orientation sheaves is a straightforward calculation which we omit.

Choose some smooth family of sections $\tilde s:B\times[0,1]\to E$ with $\tilde s^{-1}(0)$ compact, so that $\tilde s(\cdot,0)=s$ and $s_1:=\tilde s(\cdot,1)$ is transverse to the zero section.

Now $B\times[0,1]$ is a smooth manifold with boundary, so let $\tilde A$ be the implicit atlas on $\tilde X:=\tilde s^{-1}(0)$ from Definition \ref{IAonvectorbundlezeroset}.  Similarly, let $A'$ be the implicit atlas on $X':=(s')^{-1}(0)$.

Now we have two implicit atlases on $X$, namely $A$ and (the restriction to the boundary of) $\tilde A$.  On the other hand, we can exhibit $A$ as a subatlas of $\tilde A$ by the map on thickening datums sending $V_\alpha$ to $V_\alpha\times[0,1]$ and $\lambda_\alpha$ to its obvious extension.  Hence they induce the same virtual fundamental class (Lemma \ref{sameassubatlas}), so we may just write $[X]^\mathrm{vir}$ (dropping the subscript indicating which atlas we use).  The same reasoning applies to $X'$.

Now $\partial\tilde X=X\sqcup X'$, so we have $[X]^\mathrm{vir}=[X']^\mathrm{vir}$ as homology classes on $\tilde X$ (Lemmas \ref{disjointunionfc} and \ref{cobordismworks}).  Hence we have:
\begin{equation}
[X]^\mathrm{vir}=[X']^\mathrm{vir}
\end{equation}
as elements of $H^{\dim B-\dim E}(B;\mathfrak o_B\otimes\mathfrak o_E^\vee)^\vee$.

We know $X'=(X')^\mathrm{reg}$ (it is cut out transversally), so $X'$ is a closed smooth manifold and $[X']^\mathrm{vir}=[X']$ (Lemma \ref{fclasstransverse}).  But now $[X']=e_c(E,s)\cap[B]$ by elementary Poincar\'e duality.

Write $\mathfrak s_0$ in the form \eqref{SdataA}--\eqref{SdataB}.  Then we have:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s_0}=\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma|[j_{a_0}\ldots j_{a_1}],p_0,p_1)\times\cdots\times\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma|[j_{a_{m-1}}\ldots j_{a_m}],p_{m-1},p_m)
\end{equation}
Now we consider the $m$-cubical diagram:
\begin{equation}
\bigotimes_{i=1}^m\left[\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i)}\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i,\mathfrak s)\rightarrowtail\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i)\right]
\end{equation}
Now \eqref{generalizedboundaryinjective} is simply the map to the maximal vertex of the $m$-cube from the colimit over the $m$-cube minus the maximal vertex.  This is a cofibration for any cubical diagram of the form $\bigotimes_{i=1}^m[A_i\rightarrowtail B_i]$ where each $A_i\rightarrowtail B_i$ is a cofibration.  To see this, write $B_i=A_i\oplus P_i$ where $P_i$ is projective, and then the map is obviously injective with cokernel $\bigotimes_{i=1}^mP_i$.  A tensor product of projective modules is projective, as can be seen using either the tensor-hom adjunction or the fact that a module is projective iff it is a direct summand of a free module.

Let us abbreviate $A_\mathfrak s:=\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)$.

We proceed by induction on the cardinality of $\mathcal T$, the case $\mathcal T=\varnothing$ being clear.  Using the fact that a composition of cofibrations is again a cofibration, it suffices to consider the case $(\mathcal S,\mathcal T)=(\mathcal S\setminus\mathfrak s_0,\mathcal S)$ where $\mathfrak s_0\in\mathcal S$ is a maximal element.  Now $\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}A_\mathfrak s$ is the colimit of the following diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{colimitdiagrambuildup}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep = tiny]
&A_{\mathfrak s_0}\\
\smash{\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\begin{smallmatrix}\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S\cr\mathfrak s\prec\mathfrak s_0\end{smallmatrix}}}A_\mathfrak s\ar[tail]{ur}\ar[tail]{rd}\\
&\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S\setminus\mathfrak s_0}A_\mathfrak s
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where the top arrow is a cofibration by Lemma \ref{cofibrantinjectivelemma} and the bottom arrow is a cofibration by the induction hypothesis.  It follows that $\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S\setminus\mathfrak s_0}A_\mathfrak s\to\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}A_\mathfrak s$ is injective, with cokernel isomorphic to the cokernel of the top map above.  Hence it is a cofibration as needed.

Let us abbreviate $A_\mathfrak s:=\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)$.  Since $A_\mathfrak s$ is a tensor product of various $\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma',p',q')$, it follows (using Lemma \ref{tensorproductvanishing}) that $H_iA_\mathfrak s$ for $i<0$, so the left hand side of \eqref{tocolimitsurjective} vanishes for $i<0$.  Hence it remains just to show that \eqref{tocolimitsurjective} is surjective for $i\leq 0$.

We proceed by induction on the cardinality of $\mathcal S$, the case $\mathcal S=\varnothing$ being clear.  Let $\mathfrak s_0\in\mathcal S$ be any maximal element.  Now $\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}A_\mathfrak s$ is the colimit of \eqref{colimitdiagrambuildup}; it follows that we have the following exact sequence:
\begin{equation}\label{colimitSEScofibrant}
0\to\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\begin{smallmatrix}\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S\cr\mathfrak s\prec\mathfrak s_0\end{smallmatrix}}A_\mathfrak s\to A_{\mathfrak s_0}\oplus\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S\setminus\mathfrak s_0}A_\mathfrak s\to\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S}A_\mathfrak s\to 0
\end{equation}
By the induction hypothesis, it suffices to show that the second map above is surjective on $H_i$ for $i\leq 0$.  By the long exact sequence induced by \eqref{colimitSEScofibrant}, it suffices to show that $H_i\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\begin{smallmatrix}\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S\cr\mathfrak s\prec\mathfrak s_0\end{smallmatrix}}A_\mathfrak s=0$ for $i<0$.  Now this follows from the induction hypothesis.

By Lemma \ref{universalcoefficient}, we have:
\begin{align}
\label{notildeiso}H^0\operatorname{Hom}(A_\bullet,\mathbb Z)&\xrightarrow\sim\operatorname{Hom}(H_0A_\bullet,\mathbb Z)\\
\label{tildesurj}H^0\operatorname{Hom}(\tilde A_\bullet,\mathbb Z)&\twoheadrightarrow\operatorname{Hom}(H_0\tilde A_\bullet,\mathbb Z)
\end{align}
Let $f:A_0\to\mathbb Z$ denote the vertical map in the first diagram.  Using the surjectivity of \eqref{tildesurj}, there exists a cocycle $\tilde f:\tilde A_0\to\mathbb Z$ giving the desired map on homology.  Denote by $i:A_\bullet\rightarrowtail\tilde A_\bullet$ the inclusion.  Now by the commutativity of the second diagram, the difference $f-\tilde fi$ acts as zero on $H_0A_\bullet$.  Since \eqref{notildeiso} is an isomorphism, this difference is thus a coboundary $\delta g$ for some $g:A_{-1}\to\mathbb Z$.  Now extend $g$ to $\tilde g:\tilde A_{-1}\to\mathbb Z$ (using the fact that $A_{-1}\rightarrowtail\tilde A_{-1}$ splits) and let the dotted arrow be $\tilde f+\delta\tilde g$.

Let $d:R[[\mathcal P]]\to R[[\mathcal P]]$ denote the boundary operator associated to the vertex of $\sigma$, and let $1-\epsilon:R[[\mathcal P]]\to R[[\mathcal P]]$ denote the chain map associated to $\sigma$.  We must show that $\epsilon$ is chain homotopic to the zero map.  Since $\tilde Z_\bullet\to Z_\bullet$ is a trivial Kan fibration (Proposition \ref{trivialKanfibration}), there exists a $2$-simplex in $\tilde Z_\bullet$ all of whose edges are $\sigma$.  Associated to this $2$-simplex is a chain homotopy $h:R[[\mathcal P]]\to R[[\mathcal P]]$ between $(1-\epsilon)$ and $(1-\epsilon)\circ(1-\epsilon)$; in other words:
\begin{equation}
\epsilon=dh+hd+\epsilon^2
\end{equation}

Now we claim that the only nonzero ``matrix coefficients'' $c_{p,q}$ of $d,\epsilon,h$ are those for which $\mathcal X(\sigma^0,p,q)\ne\varnothing$.  By definition, $f_\sigma$ can have nonzero matrix coefficients $c_{p,q}$ only for $\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)\ne\varnothing$.  Hence hypothesis (\ref{highernonemptyissameaszero}) gives the desired claim as long as $p\ne q$.  For the diagonal matrix coefficients $c_{p,p}$, we argue separately as follows.  By degree considerations, only $\epsilon$ can have nonzero $c_{p,p}$.  It thus suffices to show that the matrix coefficient $c_{p,p}=[[E]]_{\sigma^1,p,p}(\tilde\lambda_{\sigma^1,p,p})$ of $f_{\sigma^1}=1-\epsilon$ equals $1$.  Note that no product/face maps have target $(\sigma^1,p,p)$, so $\lambda_{\sigma^1,p,p}$ is a cycle representing $\omega(\sigma^1,p,p)=1$.  Hence $\tilde\lambda_{\sigma^1,p,p}$ is a cycle representing $[E]$, and so $[[E]]_{\sigma^1,p,p}(\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,p})=1$ as needed.  Hence the claim is valid.  From the claim, we make the following two observations:
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{firstO}We have $\left|d(p)\right|_a,\left|\epsilon(p)\right|_a,\left|h(p)\right|_a\leq\left|p\right|_a$.
\item\label{secondO}For all $M<\infty$ and $p\in\mathcal P$, there exists $N<\infty$ such that any length $N$ composition of $d,\epsilon,h$ applied to $p$ has $a$-adic norm $\leq e^{-M}$.
\end{rlist}
(the second observation also uses Definition \ref{semisimplicialfcdef}(\ref{novikovfiniteness})).

Now iterating the identity $\epsilon=dh+hd+\epsilon^2$, we are led to the following infinite series:\footnote{The coefficients $\frac 1n\binom{2n-2}{n-1}$ are integers (Catalan numbers $C_{n-1}$), so writing this expression does not make any implicit assumptions on the ring $R$.}
\begin{equation}
\epsilon=\sum_{n=1}^\infty\frac 1n\binom{2n-2}{n-1}(hd+dh)^n
\end{equation}
which by observations (\ref{firstO})--(\ref{secondO}) converges in the $a$-adic topology when applied to any element of $R[[\mathcal P]]$.  Now we have $(hd+dh)^n=\sum_{k=0}^n(dh)^k(hd)^{n-k}=H_nd+dH_n$ where $H_n=\sum_{k=1}^nh(dh)^{k-1}(hd)^{n-k}$.  This gives the desired chain homotopy between $\epsilon$ and zero (again using observations (\ref{firstO})--(\ref{secondO}) to justify convergence of infinite sums).

The proof below shows that we may define $\mathbb H:=\widetilde\mathbb H\circ s$ for any section $s:Z_\bullet\to\tilde Z_\bullet$.

\textbf{Defining $\mathbb H$ over $0$-simplices.}\  Fix some $0$-simplex $v$ of $Z_\bullet$.  For every lift $\tilde v$, we have an object $\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v)$.  Furthermore, for every lift $\tilde e$ of the degenerate $1$-simplex $e$ over $v$, we get a map $\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v_1)\xrightarrow{\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde e)}\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v_2)$, which is the identity map if $\tilde v_1=\tilde v_2$.  Finally, for every lift $\tilde f$ of the degenerate $2$-simplex $f$ over $v$, the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation}\label{liftedF}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep = small]
&\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v_2)\ar{rd}{\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde e_{23})}&{}\cr
\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v_1)\ar{ur}{\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde e_{12})}\ar{rr}[swap]{\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde e_{13})}&&{\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v_3)}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $\tilde e_{12},\tilde e_{23},\tilde e_{13}$ are the edges of $\tilde f$.  Using that $\tilde Z_\bullet\to Z_\bullet$ is a trivial Kan fibration to conclude that we can always find lifts with specified boundary conditions, it follows that all $\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v)$ are canonically isomorphic.  Thus there exists a unique choice for $\mathbb H(v)$.

\textbf{Defining $\mathbb H$ over $1$-simplices.}\  Fix some $1$-simplex $e$ of $Z_\bullet$ with vertices $v_1,v_2$.  If $\tilde e$ is any lift of $e$, then we get a map $\mathbb H(v_1)=\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v_1)\xrightarrow{\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde e)}\widetilde\mathbb H(\tilde v_2)=\mathbb H(v_2)$.  Furthermore, this map $\mathbb H(v_1)\to\mathbb H(v_2)$ is seen to be independent of the choice of lift $\tilde e$ by lifting degenerate $2$-simplices over $e$.  Thus there exists a unique choice for $\mathbb H(e)$.

\textbf{Defining $\mathbb H$ over $n$-simplices for $n\geq 2$.}\  We just need to check that the following diagram commutes for all $2$-simplices $f$ in $Z_\bullet$:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep = small]
&\mathbb H(v_2)\ar{rd}{\mathbb H(e_{23})}&{}\cr
\mathbb H(v_1)\ar{ur}{\mathbb H(e_{12})}\ar{rr}[swap]{\mathbb H(e_{13})}&&{\mathbb H(v_3)}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $e_{12},e_{23},e_{13}$ are the edges of $f$.  This follows from lifting $f$ and the commutativity of \eqref{liftedF}.

We will show an equality of ``matrix coefficients'':
\begin{equation}\label{matrixcoeffsame}
([[E]]_{\sigma,p,q}\otimes\operatorname{id}_R)(\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,q})=\langle\omega(\sigma,p,q),[\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)]\rangle
\end{equation}
for $(\sigma,p,q)\in\tilde Z_\bullet$ with $\operatorname{vdim}\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)=0$.  Thus comparing \eqref{diagramndgformula} and \eqref{diagramndgformulaMS}, it follows that the two diagrams $\tilde Z_\bullet\to\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{dg}}(\mathsf{Ch}_{R[[H]]})$ in question, namely $\widetilde\mathbb H(\mathcal X)$ and $\mathbb H'(\mathcal X)\circ\pi$, coincide.  The desired isomorphism thus follows from the definition of $\mathbb H(\mathcal X)$ as the descent of $\widetilde\mathbb H(\mathcal X)$.

To prove \eqref{matrixcoeffsame}, argue as follows.  Since $\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)=\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)^\mathrm{reg}$, we know that if $\operatorname{vdim}\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)<0$ then $\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)=\varnothing$ and hence $\lambda_{\sigma,p,q}=0$ and $\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,q}=0$.  Now when $\operatorname{vdim}\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)=0$, we see that $\mu_{\sigma,p,q}=0$ and $\tilde\mu_{\sigma,p,q}=0$ (because all of the terms defining them involve a triple with negative dimension), so $\lambda_{\sigma,p,q}$ and $\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,q}$ are cycles.  Since the homology class of $\lambda_{\sigma,p,q}$ coincides with $\omega(\sigma,p,q)$, the left hand side of \eqref{matrixcoeffsame} coincides with the evaluation of $\omega(\sigma,p,q)$ on $[\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)]^\mathrm{vir}\in\check H^0(\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q);\mathfrak o_{\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)})^\vee$.  Now we are done since $[\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)]^\mathrm{vir}=[\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)]$ by Lemma \ref{fclasstransverse}.

By Poincar\'e duality, we have canonical isomorphisms:
\begin{equation}\label{PDforSSPD}
H_\bullet(M,M\setminus S^1p)=\begin{cases}H^0(S^1p;\mathfrak o_M)&\bullet=d\cr H^1(S^1p;\mathfrak o_M)&\bullet=d-1\cr 0&\text{otherwise}\end{cases}
\end{equation}
By excision and Lemma \ref{freeactionislocallytrivial}, the Gysin sequence reduces to a short exact sequence:
\begin{equation}\label{reducedgysinserreforSSPD}
0\to H_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(M,M\setminus S^1p)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}H_\bullet(M,M\setminus S^1p)\xrightarrow{\pi_\ast}H_\bullet^{S^1}(M,M\setminus S^1p)\to 0
\end{equation}
Combining this with \eqref{PDforSSPD}, we see that $H_\bullet^{S^1}(M,M\setminus S^1p)=0$ for $\bullet\ne d-1$, and that the following are both isomorphisms:
\begin{equation}\label{gysinweirdcomposition}
H_{d-1}(M,M\setminus S^1p)\xrightarrow{\pi_\ast}H_{d-1}^{S^1}(M,M\setminus S^1p)\xrightarrow{\pi^!}H_d(M,M\setminus S^1p)
\end{equation}
which yields the desired result.

As in the proof of Lemma \ref{CSTpure}, we use Lemmas \ref{singularchainshomotopyKsheaf} and \ref{associatedgradedhomotopysheaf} to see that $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$ are homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves.  More precisely, in the present context we need the statement of Lemma \ref{singularchainshomotopyKsheaf} for $C_\bullet^{S^1}$ in place of $C_\bullet$.  The proof of this lemma applies equally well to $C_\bullet^{S^1}$, noting that since $C_\bullet^{S^1}$ is a model of $S^1$-equivariant chains, it in particular satisfies Mayer--Vietoris in that following (total complex) is acyclic:
\begin{equation}
C_\bullet^{S^1}(U\cap U')\to C_\bullet^{S^1}(U)\oplus C_\bullet^{S^1}(U')\to C_\bullet^{S^1}(U\cup U')
\end{equation}

Now let us show purity.  By Lemma \ref{openclosedpurity}, it suffices to show that the restrictions of $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$ to $(V_I\cap V_J)/S^1$ and to $X/S^1\setminus(V_I\cap V_J)/S^1$ are pure.  The latter restriction is trivially pure, since both complexes are simply zero for $K\cap V_I\cap V_J=\varnothing$.  Hence it suffices to show that the restrictions of both complexes to $(V_I\cap V_J)/S^1$ are pure.

We consider the comparison maps:
\begin{align}
\label{HSTinjective}H^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}&\to H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K;A)_{IJ}\\
\label{HSTinjectiverel}H^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&\to H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}
\end{align}
These are Gysin maps $\pi^!$, and hence by the Gysin sequence, their kernels coincide (respectively) with the images of:
\begin{align}
\label{HSTzero}H^{\bullet-2}_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}&\xrightarrow{\cap e}H^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K;A)_{IJ}\\
\label{HSTzerorel}H^{\bullet-2}_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&\xrightarrow{\cap e}H^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(K\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}
\end{align}
where $e$ is (the pullback of) $e\in H^2_{S^1}(X_J^\mathrm{reg}/\Gamma_J)$.  Pick any $p\in V_J\subseteq X$.  By Lemma \ref{freeactionislocallytrivial}, this $e$ vanishes when restricted to small $S^1$-invariant compact neighborhoods of $S^1p\subseteq V_J=(s_J|X_J^\mathrm{reg})^{-1}(0)/\Gamma_J\subseteq X_J^\mathrm{reg}/\Gamma_J$.  It follows that \eqref{HSTzero}--\eqref{HSTzerorel} vanish for small $S^1$-invariant compact neighborhoods $K$ of $S^1p\subseteq V_J$.  Hence \eqref{HSTinjective}--\eqref{HSTinjectiverel} are injective for such $K$.  Now, using this injectivity and the fact that $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$ are pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves (Lemma \ref{CSTpure}), it follows that $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$ are pure on $V_J/S^1$, and hence on all of $X/S^1$.

It remains to identify $H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}$ and $H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}$.  Consider the comparison maps:
\begin{align}
H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A)_{IJ}&\to\pi_\ast H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A)_{IJ}\\
H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}&\to\pi_\ast H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)_{IJ}
\end{align}
which are maps of sheaves on $X/S^1$.  It suffices (by Lemma \ref{CSTpure}) to show that these are isomorphisms (which we will check on stalks, i.e.\ for $K=S^1p$).  Now this is just a calculation, similar to that in the proof of Lemma \ref{CSTpure}, except using an $S^1$-equivariant version of Poincar\'e--Lefschetz duality based on Lemma \ref{SSmanifoldPDbase} in place of Lemma \ref{poincareduality} (and it is in this calculation where we use the local $S^1$-orientability of $A$).

Consider the following diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
H^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(X;A)\ar{d}{\sim}\ar{rr}{\eqref{CSStimesSS}}&&H^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(X;A)\ar{d}{\sim}\\
\check H^\bullet(X/S^1;C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A))\ar{r}{\eqref{CSStimesSS}}\ar{d}{\sim}&\check H^\bullet(X/S^1,\pi_\ast C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A))\ar{r}{\pi^\ast}\ar{d}{\sim}&\check H^\bullet(X;C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A))\ar{d}{\sim}\\
\check H^\bullet(X/S^1;\tau_{\geq 0}C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A))\ar{r}{\eqref{CSStimesSS}}&\check H^\bullet(X/S^1,\pi_\ast\tau_{\geq 0}C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A))\ar{r}{\pi^\ast}&\check H^\bullet(X;\tau_{\geq 0}C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(-;A))\\
\check H^\bullet(X/S^1;H^0_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(-;A))\ar{u}[swap]{\sim}\ar{r}{\eqref{CSStimesSS}}\ar{d}{\sim}&\check H^\bullet(X/S^1,\pi_\ast H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A))\ar{r}{\pi^\ast}\ar{u}\ar{d}{\sim}&\check H^\bullet(X;H^0_\mathrm{vir}(-;A))\ar{u}[swap]{\sim}\ar{d}{\sim}\\
\check H^\bullet(X/S^1;\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_X)\ar[equal]{r}&\check H^\bullet(X/S^1,\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_X)\ar{r}{\pi^\ast}&\check H^\bullet(X;\mathfrak o_X)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
where the vertical isomorphisms are by Propositions \ref{CpureSS} and \ref{Cpure} (see also \eqref{isosforpurehtpysheafcech}).

Now each small square of this diagram above commutes (only the bottom left requires an argument; one shows that the corresponding diagram of sheaves commutes by checking it locally, where it is just a calculation).  Now the first square in Theorem \ref{Slocalfundamentaliso} is the same as the big square above, which commutes by a diagram chase (for which one should be careful of the fact that one of the vertical arrows is not an isomorphism).

An identical argument applies to the second square in Theorem \ref{Slocalfundamentaliso}.

We may assume that $A$ is finite.  Now by Theorem \ref{Slocalfundamentaliso}, we have a commutative diagram:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{tikzcd}
\check H^{d+\bullet}(X/S^1;\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\ar{d}{\pi^\ast}\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Slocalfundamentaliso}}}&H^{d+\bullet}_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar{d}{\eqref{CSStimesSS}}\ar{r}{\eqref{CSSpushforward}}&H_{-\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;A)\ar{d}{\pi^!}\\
\check H^{d+\bullet}(X;\mathfrak o_{X\operatorname{rel}\partial})\ar[equal]{r}{\text{Thm \ref{Fsheaffundamentaliso}}}&H^{d+\bullet}_\mathrm{vir}(X\operatorname{rel}\partial;A)\ar{r}{\eqref{Fpushforward}}&H_{-\bullet}(E;A)\ar{r}{[E_A]\mapsto 1}&R
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation*}
By definition, $[X]^\mathrm{vir}$ is the total composition of the bottom row for $\bullet=0$.  Now the desired statement follows since the rightmost vertical map is zero (by Lemma \ref{trivialactiongysin}, because $S^1$ acts trivially on $E_A$).

All $S^1$-MF-sets in this proof will share the same $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$, so we will omit the subscript $_\mathcal Y$ from the notation.

Let $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}$ denote the $S^1$-MF-set indexed by pairs $(n,f)$ consisting of an integer $n\geq 0$ and a PL map $f:\Delta^n\to BS^1$, where the stratifications and face identifications are as in Example \ref{standardsimplicialMFset}.  Now the complex $C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)$ is freely generated by maps $\Delta^n\to(X\times ES^1)/S^1$ whose composition with the projection $(X\times ES^1)/S^1\to BS^1$ is PL.  A straightforward approximation argument shows that the inclusion of this complex into the complex generated by all maps $\Delta^n\to(X\times ES^1)/S^1$ is a quasi-isomorphism.  Hence there is a canonical isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
H^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)\xrightarrow\sim H_\bullet((X\times ES^1)/S^1)=H_\bullet^{S^1}(X)
\end{equation}

As in the proof of Lemma \ref{saturatedcalculatesH} there exists a morphism of $S^1$-MF-sets $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\to\mathcal Y$ since $\mathcal Y$ is saturated.  This induces a chain map $C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)\to C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)$, which as before induces a canonical map on homology:
\begin{equation}
H^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)\to H^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)
\end{equation}
which is independent of the choice of morphism $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\to\mathcal Y$.  It suffices to show that this map is an isomorphism.

Fix a map $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\to\mathcal Y$, which we observe is necessarily injective.  Fix triangulations $T_i$ of $M_i$ as in the proof of Lemma \ref{saturatedcalculatesH}.  Such triangulations induce a map of complexes $C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)\to C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)$.  The composition $C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)\to C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)\to C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)$ is clearly the identity map.  It thus suffices to show that the reverse composition is chain homotopic to the identity.

As in the proof of Lemma \ref{saturatedcalculatesH}, we construct a new $S^1$-MF-set, namely $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\sqcup\{M_i\times[0,1]\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$, from the inclusion $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\hookrightarrow\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ and the triangulations $T_i$.  There are inclusions $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\hookrightarrow\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\hookrightarrow\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\sqcup\{M_i\times[0,1]\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$.  The desired chain homotopy may then be constructed as in the proof of Lemma \ref{saturatedcalculatesH}.

The fact that the induced map $H^{S^1}_\bullet(X)\to H_{\bullet+1}(X)$ is the Gysin map is left to the reader.

It suffices to perform the inductive step for a given $(\sigma,p,q)$.  First, we show that the colimits \eqref{colimitYone}--\eqref{colimitYtwo} exist, essentially by rewriting the proofs of Lemmas \ref{cofibrantinjectivelemma} and \ref{cofibranthaslotsofcofibrations} in the present context.  We actually show the more general statement that the colimits:
\begin{equation}\label{Tcolimit}
\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak t\in\mathcal T}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t)\qquad\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak t\in\mathcal T}\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t)
\end{equation}
exist for any $\mathcal T\subseteq\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)$ which is downward closed.  We show this by induction on the cardinality of $\mathcal T$.  If $\mathcal T=\varnothing$, existence is trivial.  For $\mathcal T$ nonempty, let $\mathfrak t_0\in\mathcal T$ be any maximal element, and observe that \eqref{Tcolimit} can be written as the colimit of the diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{colimitdiagramnice}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep = tiny]
&\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t_0)\\
\smash{\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\begin{smallmatrix}\mathfrak t\in\mathcal T\cr\mathfrak t\prec\mathfrak t_0\end{smallmatrix}}}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t)\ar[hook]{ur}\ar{rd}\\
&\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak t\in\mathcal T\setminus\mathfrak t_0}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Let us show that the top arrow is injective.  Write $\mathfrak t_0$ in the form \eqref{SdataA}--\eqref{SdataB}.  Then we have:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak t_0}=\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma|[j_{a_0}\ldots j_{a_1}],p_0,p_1)\times\cdots\times\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma|[j_{a_{m-1}}\ldots j_{a_m}],p_{m-1},p_m)
\end{equation}
Now we consider the $m$-cubical diagram:
\begin{equation}
\prod_{i=1}^m\left[\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i)}\mathcal Y(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i,\mathfrak s)\hookrightarrow\mathcal Y(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i)\right]
\end{equation}
Now the top arrow of \eqref{colimitdiagramnice} is simply the map to the maximal vertex of this $m$-cube from the colimit over the $m$-cube minus the maximal vertex (this is where we use the fact that $\mathcal T$ is downward closed).  This is injective for any cubical diagram of the form $\prod_{i=1}^m[A_i\hookrightarrow B_i]$ where each $A_i\hookrightarrow B_i$ is injective.  Now that the top arrow in \eqref{colimitdiagramnice} is injective, the existence of the colimit is clear (first take the colimit of underlying sets and then the rest of the structure extends in an obvious way), and hence the colimit in \eqref{Tcolimit} exists.  This reasoning applies equally well to $\mathcal Y_{S^1}$ (including $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$).

Now that we have shown that the colimits on the left hand side of \eqref{colimitYone}--\eqref{colimitYtwo} exist, it remains to define $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$ and $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)$.

We observed earlier that any $S^1$-MF-set injects into a saturated $S^1$-MF-set.  Let $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)$ be any saturated $S^1$-MF-set with an injection \eqref{colimitYtwo}.  Note that with this definition, the product/face maps with target $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)$ are obvious, as is their compatibility.

To define $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$, consider the following diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{biggluingcolimit}
\begin{tikzcd}
\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)\\
\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)}\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)\ar{u}\ar[hook]{r}&\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
The lower colimit is in the category of $S^1$-MF-sets.  It is clear from the inductive proof of its existence that this particular colimit commutes with the forgetful functor to MF-sets.  Hence we may equally well think of this colimit as taking place in the category of MF-sets, and with this perspective the definition of the vertical map is clear.  Now since the horizontal map is injective, the colimit of this diagram (in the category of MF-sets) clearly exists (first take the colimit in sets and then the rest of the structure is obvious).  We pick any saturated MF-set into which the colimit of \eqref{biggluingcolimit} embeds, and we call this saturated MF-set $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$.  Via the embedding of the upper colimit into $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$, it is tautological that the face/product maps with target $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$ exist and are compatible.  The map $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)\to\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$ is similarly tautological, as is the fact that it is compatible with the product/face maps.

\textbf{Part I.}\  We begin by reducing to a situation in which the partition \eqref{partitionsfreefixedII} is extended to every thickened moduli space, so that the product/face maps on thickened moduli spaces also take the form \eqref{PFfirst}--\eqref{PFlast}.  Precisely, consider a new implicit atlas $\tilde\mathcal A$ (on the same index set $\mathcal A$) in which the thickened moduli spaces are:
\begin{equation}\label{newatlastrickSS}
\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)_{\tilde I}^{\leq\mathfrak s}:=[\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\leq\mathfrak s}\setminus\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)^1]\sqcup[\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\leq\mathfrak s}\setminus\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)^0]
\end{equation}
Now these thickened moduli spaces are by definition equipped with a partition $\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)_{\tilde I}^{\leq\mathfrak s}=(\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)_{\tilde I}^{\leq\mathfrak s})^0\sqcup(\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)_{\tilde I}^{\leq\mathfrak s})^1$ (namely the partition \eqref{newatlastrickSS}), and the natural product/face maps for the implicit atlas $\tilde\mathcal A$ take the form \eqref{PFfirst}--\eqref{PFlast}.

The thickened moduli spaces of $\tilde\mathcal A$ are equipped with natural maps to those of $\mathcal A$ (compatibile with the product/face maps); these maps are not open embeddings, but nevertheless the proof of Lemma \ref{homologyopenreduction} applies to give a canonical isomorphism $\mathbb H(\mathcal X)_\mathcal A=\mathbb H(\mathcal X)_{\tilde\mathcal A}$.  Thus it suffices to produce an isomorphism $\mathbb H(\mathcal X)_{\tilde\mathcal A}=\mathbb H(\mathcal X^0)_{\tilde\mathcal A^0}$, where $\tilde\mathcal A^0$ is the implicit atlas on $\mathcal X^0$ with thickened moduli spaces $(\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)_{\tilde I}^{\leq\mathfrak s})^0$.  From now on, we will work exclusively with the atlas $\tilde\mathcal A$, which we now rather abusively rename as $\mathcal A$.

\textbf{Part II.}\  We will define an isomorphism $\mathbb H(\mathcal X)_\mathcal A=\mathbb H(\mathcal X^0)_{\mathcal A^0}$, where $\mathcal A^0$ is the implicit atlas on $\mathcal X^0$ with thickened moduli spaces $(\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\leq\mathfrak s})^0$.

Note that since we have coherent orientations $\omega$ and $S^1$ is connected, it follows that all the flow spaces are locally $S^1$-orientable (see Remark \ref{orientedthenSSoriented}), so we may use the $S^1$-localization machinery freely.

Let $\tilde Z_\bullet\to Z_\bullet$ denote the resolution (as in \S\ref{floerhomologyfromMFsets}) associated to $\mathcal X/Z_\bullet$ with the implicit atlas $\mathcal A$, and let $\tilde Z_\bullet^0\to Z_\bullet$ denote the resolution associated to $\mathcal X^0/Z_\bullet$ with the implicit atlas $\mathcal A^0$.

We will construct a diagram of the following shape:
\begin{equation}\label{differentresolutions}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep = large, column sep = tiny]
\tilde Z_\bullet\ar{drr}&\tilde Z_\bullet^a\ar{l}\ar{r}\ar{dr}&\tilde Z_\bullet^b\ar{d}&\tilde Z_\bullet^c\ar{l}\ar{r}\ar{dl}&\tilde Z_\bullet^0\ar{dll}\\
&&Z_\bullet
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
and natural diagrams $\widetilde\mathbb H,\widetilde\mathbb H^a,\widetilde\mathbb H^b,\widetilde\mathbb H^c,\widetilde\mathbb H^0$ from $\tilde Z_\bullet,\tilde Z_\bullet^a,\tilde Z_\bullet^b,\tilde Z_\bullet^c,\tilde Z_\bullet^0$ to $\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{dg}}(\mathsf{Ch}_{R[[H]]})$ (respectively).  We will also construct natural isomorphisms relating these and their pullbacks under the horizontal maps in \eqref{differentresolutions}.  Finally, we will show that each of the vertical maps \eqref{differentresolutions} satisfies the conclusions of Propositions \ref{trivialKanfibration} and \ref{degenerateID}.  The desired result follows easily from these statements; let us now start on the proof.

We may consider $\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)$ as a system of implicit atlases on $\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)^0$ and on $\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)^1$ separately.  Now consider the following maps (where the complexes on the left are defined analogously to those on the right as in \S\ref{SSaugmentedsec}):
\begin{align}
\label{partialSSpushforwardbigcomplex}C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\partial\mathcal X^0,\mathcal A)^+&\oplus C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\partial\mathcal X^1,\mathcal A)^+&&\to C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\partial\mathcal X;\mathcal A)^+\\
\label{relpartialSSpushforwardbigcomplex}C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial,\mathcal A)^+&\oplus C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial,\mathcal A)^+&&\to C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+
\end{align}
We give $C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial,\mathcal A)^+\oplus C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial,\mathcal A)^+$ the structure of an $\mathsf{F}$-module with product/face maps:
\begin{align}
\label{CSSproductfacesplitfirst}C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\otimes C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)&\to C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\partial\mathcal X^0;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\\
C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\otimes C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\partial\mathcal X^1;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\\
C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\otimes C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\partial\mathcal X^1;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\\
C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\otimes C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\partial\mathcal X^1;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\\
C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)&\to C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\partial\mathcal X^0;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)\\
\label{CSSproductfacesplitlast}C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)&\to C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\partial\mathcal X^1;\mathcal A)^+(\cdots)
\end{align}
just as in Definition \ref{Cproductface} (and using the chain models from \S\ref{SSaugmentedsec}).  These are compatible with \eqref{partialSSpushforwardbigcomplex}--\eqref{relpartialSSpushforwardbigcomplex} and \eqref{Ccomposition}--\eqref{Csimplexextension}.

Next, we consider $C_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)^+\oplus C_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)^+$ (as in \S\ref{SSaugmentedsec}).  Now, there are \emph{two} natural maps:
\begin{equation}\label{CESSpushforward}
\begin{tikzcd}
C_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)^+\oplus C_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)^+\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{\operatorname{id}\oplus{\pi^!}}\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{r}[swap]{\operatorname{id}\oplus 0}&C_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)^+
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
We give $C_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)^+\oplus C_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)^+$ the structure of an $\mathsf{F}$-module with product/face maps of the shape \eqref{CSSproductfacesplitfirst}--\eqref{CSSproductfacesplitlast} just as in Definition \ref{CEcomplexaugmented}.  Then both maps \eqref{CESSpushforward} are maps of $\mathsf{F}$-modules.  There is also a pushforward map:
\begin{equation*}
C^{\operatorname{vdim}\mathcal X+\bullet}_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial,\mathcal A)^+\oplus C^{\operatorname{vdim}\mathcal X+\bullet}_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial,\mathcal A)^+\to C_{-\bullet}(E;\mathcal A)^+\oplus C_{-\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)^+
\end{equation*}
which is a map of $\mathsf{F}$-modules.

Now let us define $\tilde Z_\bullet^a$, $\tilde Z_\bullet^b$, and $\tilde Z_\bullet^c$.  We modify the definition of $\tilde Z_\bullet\to Z_\bullet$ (i.e.\ Definition \ref{Lambdadef} as amended in \S\ref{floerhomologyfromMFsets}) as follows.  For $\tilde Z_\bullet^a=\tilde Z_\bullet^c$ we replace Definition \ref{Lambdadef}(\ref{lambdachoice},\ref{liftschoice}) with (\ref{lambdachoiceSS},\ref{liftschoiceSS}) below, and for $\tilde Z_\bullet^b$ we replace Definition \ref{Lambdadef}(\ref{lambdachoice},\ref{liftschoice},\ref{augmentationschoice}) with (\ref{lambdachoiceSS},\ref{liftschoiceSS},\ref{augmentationschoiceSS}) below.
\begin{rlist}
\item\label{lambdachoiceSS}An $H$-invariant system of chains:
\begin{equation*}
\lambda=\lambda^0\oplus\lambda^1\in C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial,\mathcal B)^+\oplus C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial,\mathcal B)^+
\end{equation*}
(degree $0$ and supported inside $\operatorname{supp}\mathcal X$) with the following property.  Note that $(\mu,\lambda)$ is a cycle in the mapping cone:
\begin{equation*}
\begin{matrix}
C^\bullet_\mathrm{vir}(\partial f^\ast\mathcal X^0;\mathcal B)^+\cr\oplus\cr C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(\partial f^\ast\mathcal X^1;\mathcal B)^+
\end{matrix}
\longrightarrow
\begin{matrix}
C^\bullet_{\mathrm{vir}}(f^\ast\mathcal X^0\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal B)^+\cr\oplus\cr C^\bullet_{S^1,\mathrm{vir}}(f^\ast\mathcal X^1\operatorname{rel}\partial;\mathcal B)^+
\end{matrix}
\end{equation*}
whose homology is identified with $\check H^\bullet(f^\ast\mathcal X^0;\mathfrak o_{f^\ast\mathcal X^0})\oplus\check H^\bullet((f^\ast\mathcal X^1)/S^1;\pi_\ast\mathfrak o_{f^\ast\mathcal X^1})$, which in degree zero simply equals $\check H^0(f^\ast\mathcal X;\mathfrak o_{f^\ast\mathcal X})$.  We require that the homology class of $(\mu,\lambda)$ equal $f^\ast\omega\in\check H^0(f^\ast\mathcal X;\mathfrak o_{f^\ast\mathcal X})$.
\item\label{liftschoiceSS}An $H$-invariant system of chains $\tilde\lambda$ for $Q[C_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)^+\oplus C_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)^+]$ (degree $\operatorname{gr}(q)-\operatorname{gr}(p)+\dim\sigma-1$ and supported inside $\operatorname{supp}\mathcal X$) whose image in $C_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)^+\oplus C_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)^+$ coincides with the image of $\lambda$.
\item\label{augmentationschoiceSS}An $H$-invariant map of $\mathsf{F}$-modules $[[E]]:Q[C_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)^+_\mathbb Z\oplus C_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)^+_\mathbb Z]\to\mathbb Z$ which sends the fundamental class in $H_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)^+_\mathbb Z$ to $1$ and is zero on $H_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)^+_\mathbb Z$.
\end{rlist}
The maps $\tilde Z_\bullet\leftarrow\tilde Z_\bullet^a\to\tilde Z_\bullet^b\leftarrow\tilde Z_\bullet^c\to\tilde Z_\bullet^0$ are defined as follows:
\begin{equation}\label{defofZabcmaps}
\begin{tikzcd}
\tilde Z_\bullet&\ar{l}\tilde Z_\bullet^a\ar{r}&\tilde Z_\bullet^b&\ar{l}\tilde Z_\bullet^c\ar{r}&\tilde Z_\bullet^0\cr
\lambda&\ar[mapsto]{l}[swap]{\eqref{relpartialSSpushforwardbigcomplex}}\lambda&&\lambda\ar[mapsto]{r}{\lambda=\lambda^0\oplus\lambda^1}&\lambda^0\cr
\tilde\lambda&\ar[mapsto]{l}[swap]{Q[\operatorname{id}\oplus\pi^!]}\tilde\lambda&&\tilde\lambda\ar[mapsto]{r}{Q[\operatorname{id}\oplus 0]}&\tilde\lambda^0\cr
&{}[[E]]\ar[mapsto]{r}{\circ Q[\operatorname{id}\oplus\pi^!]}&{}[[E]]&\ar[mapsto]{l}[swap]{\circ Q[\operatorname{id}\oplus 0]}\null[[E]]
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Note that Lemma \ref{trivialactiongysin} shows that the map $\tilde Z^a\to\tilde Z^b$ above produces an $[[E]]$ satisfying (\ref{augmentationschoiceSS}).

Now $\widetilde\mathbb H,\widetilde\mathbb H^a,\widetilde\mathbb H^b,\widetilde\mathbb H^c,\widetilde\mathbb H^0$ are defined via the matrix coefficients $c_{\sigma,p,q}$ defined as follows:
\begin{align*}
\text{for $\widetilde\mathbb H$:}\quad c_{\sigma,p,q}&:=([[E]]_{\sigma,p,q}\otimes\operatorname{id}_R)(\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,q})\\
\text{for $\widetilde\mathbb H^a$:}\quad c_{\sigma,p,q}&:=([[E]]_{\sigma,p,q}\otimes\operatorname{id}_R)(\operatorname{id}\oplus\pi^!)(\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,q})\\
\text{for $\widetilde\mathbb H^b$:}\quad c_{\sigma,p,q}&:=([[E]]_{\sigma,p,q}\otimes\operatorname{id}_R)(\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,q})\\
\text{for $\widetilde\mathbb H^c$:}\quad c_{\sigma,p,q}&:=([[E]]_{\sigma,p,q}\otimes\operatorname{id}_R)(\operatorname{id}\oplus 0)(\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,q})\\
\text{for $\widetilde\mathbb H^0$:}\quad c_{\sigma,p,q}&:=([[E]]_{\sigma,p,q}\otimes\operatorname{id}_R)(\tilde\lambda_{\sigma,p,q})
\end{align*}
These matrix coefficients give rise to diagrams in $\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{dg}}(\mathsf{Ch}_{R[[H]]})$ since $[[E]]$ is a map of $\mathsf{F}$-modules.  The isomorphisms between these diagrams and their pullbacks under the maps \eqref{defofZabcmaps} are evident.

It now remains to show that each of the maps $\tilde Z_\bullet^a,\tilde Z_\bullet^b,\tilde Z_\bullet^c\to\tilde Z_\bullet$ satisfies the conclusions of Propositions \ref{trivialKanfibration} and \ref{degenerateID}.  The proof of Proposition \ref{trivialKanfibration} applies to $\tilde Z_\bullet^a,\tilde Z_\bullet^b,\tilde Z_\bullet^c\to\tilde Z_\bullet$ as written (for $\tilde Z_\bullet^b$, the extension of $[[E]]$ step uses the fact that $H_\bullet(E;\mathcal A)\oplus H_{\bullet-1}^{S^1}(E;\mathcal A)$ is concentrated in degrees $\geq 0$ so that Lemma \ref{Hzeroofcofibrantcolimit} still applies).  The proof of Proposition \ref{degenerateID} also applies to $\tilde Z_\bullet^a,\tilde Z_\bullet^b,\tilde Z_\bullet^c\to\tilde Z_\bullet$ as written.  Now the uniqueness of descent from Lemma \ref{descentofdiagram} shows that there are canonical isomorphisms of the descents $\mathbb H=\mathbb H^a=\mathbb H^b=\mathbb H^c=\mathbb H^0$.

Fix a point in $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)$ (that is, a curve $u:C\to X$).  We will construct $\alpha\in A^\mathrm{GW}$ so that this point is contained in $\psi_{\varnothing\{\alpha\}}((s_\alpha|\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_{\{\alpha\}}^\mathrm{reg})^{-1}(0))$.

First, pick $D_\alpha\subseteq X$ satisfying the conclusion of Lemma \ref{stabilizationlemma}.

Now let $r_\alpha=\#u^{-1}(D_\alpha)$.  Adding $u^{-1}(D_\alpha)$ as extra marked points to $C$ gives a point in $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n+r_\alpha}/S_{r_\alpha}$, and we pick some local orbifold chart $\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha/\Gamma_\alpha\to\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n+r_\alpha}/S_{r_\alpha}$ covering this point.

Now let us consider the linearized $\bar\partial$ operator:
\begin{equation}\label{linearizeddelbarforthickening}
D\bar\partial(u,\cdot):C^\infty(C,u^\ast TX)\to C^\infty(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C u^\ast TX)
\end{equation}
($\tilde C$ being the normalization of $C$) where $C^\infty(C,u^\ast TX)\subseteq C^\infty(\tilde C,u^\ast TX)$ is the subspace of functions descending continuously to $C$.  If $(k,p)\in\mathbb Z_{\geq 1}\times(1,\infty)$ with $kp>2$, then we get a corresponding $D\bar\partial(u,\cdot)$ map $W^{k,p}\to W^{k-1,p}$ (the restriction on $(k,p)$ comes from the need to define $W^{k,p}(C,u^\ast TX)\subseteq W^{k,p}(\tilde C,u^\ast TX)$).  This operator is Fredholm; in particular its cokernel is finite-dimensional.

Suppose we have a finite-dimensional vector space $E_0$ equipped with a linear map:
\begin{equation}\label{lambdazero}
\tilde\lambda_0:E_0\to C^\infty(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C u^\ast TX)
\end{equation}
supported away from (the inverse image in $\tilde C$ of) the nodes and marked points of $C$.  Then the following conditions are equivalent (exercise using elliptic regularity):
\begin{rlist}
\item\eqref{lambdazero} is surjective onto the cokernel of $D\bar\partial(u,\cdot):C^\infty\to C^\infty$.
\item\eqref{lambdazero} is surjective onto the cokernel of $D\bar\partial(u,\cdot):W^{k,p}\to W^{k-1,p}$ for some $(k,p)$.
\item\eqref{lambdazero} is surjective onto the cokernel of $D\bar\partial(u,\cdot):W^{k,p}\to W^{k-1,p}$ for all $(k,p)$.
\end{rlist}
There exists such a pair $E_0$ and $\tilde\lambda_0$ satisfying these conditions since we may choose $k=1$ and $p>2$ and then remember that $C^\infty$ functions supported away from the nodes and marked points are dense in $W^{0,p}=L^p$.

Now pick any isomorphism to a fiber $\phi_\alpha:C\to\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha$ and extend $\tilde\lambda_0$ to a map $\lambda_0:E_0\to C^\infty(\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha\times X,\Omega^{0,1}_{\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha/\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha}\otimes_\mathbb C TX)$ supported away from the nodes and marked points of the fibers.  Define $E_\alpha=E_0[\Gamma]$ and define $\lambda_\alpha:E_\alpha\to C^\infty(\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha\times X,\Omega^{0,1}_{\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha/\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha}\otimes_\mathbb C TX)$ by $\Gamma_\alpha$-linear extension from $\lambda_0$.  It is now clear by definition that $u:C\to X$ is covered by $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_{\{\alpha\}}^\mathrm{reg}$, where $\alpha\in A^\mathrm{GW}$ is the element just constructed.

Instead of finding an $S^1$-invariant $D\subseteq M\times S^1\times\Delta^n$, we find a $D\subseteq M\times\Delta^n$ (which is clearly equivalent) and we ignore the $S^1$ factor in the codomain of $u$.  As in Lemma \ref{stabilizationlemmaforHF}, it suffices to show that for any unstable component $C_0\subseteq C$, either $C_0$ is a Morse flow line or there exists a point (other than a node) where $d((\operatorname{id}_M\times\ell)\circ u):TC_0\to TM\times T\Delta^n$ is injective.

If the projection $C_0\to S^1\times\mathbb R$ is constant, then $u:C_0\to M$ is a (nonconstant!) $J$-holomorphic sphere and we are done as in Lemma \ref{stabilizationlemmaforHF}.  Hence it suffices to treat the case when $u:C_0\to S^1\times\mathbb R$ is not constant.  Thus $u:C_0\to S^1\times\mathbb R$ is an isomorphism, so we can use $(t,s)\in S^1\times\mathbb R$ as coordinates on $C_0$.  Now we split into two cases.

First, suppose $\ell:\mathbb R\to\Delta^n$ is not constant.  If $u:C_0\to M$ is independent of the $S^1$ coordinate, then it is a Morse flow line, and we do not need to stabilize.  Otherwise, there is a point where $\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}\ne 0$, and since $\ell:\mathbb R\to\Delta^n$ has nonvanishing derivative everywhere, it follows that $du$ is injective at this point.

Second, suppose $\ell:\mathbb R\to\Delta^n$ is constant.  Then our map $u:C_0\to M$ satisfies:
\begin{equation}\label{pseudoholoncylinderformorseHamiltonian}
\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}+J\circ\frac{\partial u}{\partial s}=\nabla H
\end{equation}
Certainly $u:C_0\to M$ is not constant; otherwise it would be unstable (infinite automorphism group).  Thus $du$ is nonzero somewhere.  If $du$ has rank two somewhere, then we are done.  Thus let us suppose that this is not the case and show that $u|C_0$ is independent of the $t$ coordinate (and thus is a Morse flow line).  Thus there exists some open set $U\subseteq C_0=S^1\times\mathbb R$ where $du$ has rank $1$.  Inside $U$, we have $\ker du\subseteq TC_0=T(S^1\times\mathbb R)$ is an (integrable!) $1$-dimensional distribution, so $U$ is equipped with a $1$-dimensional foliation and $u$ is constant on the leaves.  Thus we have (locally) a factorization $u:S^1\times\mathbb R\xrightarrow r(-\epsilon,\epsilon)\xrightarrow wM$, the leaves of the foliation being given by $r^{-1}(\delta)$ for $\delta\in(-\epsilon,\epsilon)$.  Now \eqref{pseudoholoncylinderformorseHamiltonian} becomes:
\begin{equation}
\Bigl(\frac{\partial r}{\partial t}+\frac{\partial r}{\partial s}\cdot J\Bigr)\cdot w'(r(s,t))=(\nabla H)(w(r(s,t)))
\end{equation}
Since $du$ has rank $1$, we know that $w'(r(s,t))\ne 0$.  Hence the value of $r(s,t)$ determines the value of its derivative uniquely, i.e.\ $dr$ is constant along the leaves $r^{-1}(\delta)$ of the foliation.  It follows that the foliation is (locally) linear(!) and that we can follow any leaf infinitely in both directions and it never exits $U$ (since $dr=0$ outside $U$).  Now if any leaf had nonzero slope, it would force $u:C_0\to M$ to be constant, a contradiction.  Thus all leaves have slope zero; in other words $u$ is independent of the $S^1$-coordinate over $U=S^1\times U'$.  But now we see that $U'=\mathbb R$, since if $U'$ had boundary, it would imply that we have a Morse flow line reaching a critical point in finite time.  Thus $u$ is (globally) a Morse flow line.

Pick any $\omega$-compatible almost complex structure $J$ and consider the vertex $(J,H)\inJ\!H_\bullet(M)$.  Over this vertex, pick any complex $F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)$ which calculates $F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)$.  Then $F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)$ is a free $\mathbb Q[[\pi]]$-module of rank:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)\leq\#\operatorname{Fix}\phi_H
\end{equation}
(its rank equals the number of null-homotopic periodic orbits).  By Theorem \ref{HFcalculation}, the homology $F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)$ is free of rank:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)=\dim H_\bullet(M;\mathbb Q)
\end{equation}
Now by definition, there is a $\mathbb Q[[\pi]]$-linear boundary map $d:F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)\to F\mathbb C^{\bullet+1}(M)$ and by definition $\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)=\ker d/\operatorname{im} d$.  Now apply Lemma \ref{homologyhassmallerrank} to conclude that:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)\leq\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)
\end{equation}
Thus we are done.

Suppose $\mathcal F$ is a sheaf, and let us verify $\alpha^\ast\mathcal F$ is a $\mathcal K$-sheaf.  Axiom \eqref{sheafvanishing} is clear (take $A=\varnothing$ in \eqref{opensheafproperty}).  Axiom \eqref{sheafproperty} follows from \eqref{opensheafproperty} and Lemma \ref{intersectioncofinal} since direct limits are exact.  Axiom \eqref{sheafcontinuity} is clear from the definition also.

Suppose $\mathcal F$ is a $\mathcal K$-sheaf, and let us verify that $\alpha_\ast\mathcal F$ is a sheaf.  Let us first observe that (by induction using \eqref{sheafvanishing} and \eqref{sheafproperty}) if $\{K_\alpha\subseteq X\}_{\alpha\in A}$ is any collection of compact sets, all but finitely many of which are empty, then the following is exact:
\begin{equation}\label{compactfiniteexact}
0\to\mathcal F\Bigl(\bigcup_{\alpha\in A}K_\alpha\Bigr)\to\prod_{\alpha\in A}\mathcal F(K_\alpha)\to\prod_{\alpha,\beta\in A}\mathcal F(K_\alpha\cap K_\beta)
\end{equation}
Let us now verify axiom \eqref{opensheafproperty} for $\alpha_\ast\mathcal F$ for open sets $\{U_\alpha\subseteq X\}_{\alpha\in A}$.  Certainly \eqref{opensheafproperty} is the inverse limit of \eqref{compactfiniteexact} over all collections of compact subsets $\{K_\alpha\subseteq U_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ for which $K_\alpha=\varnothing$ except for finitely many $\alpha\in A$.  This is sufficient since inverse limit is left exact.

Now to see that the adjoint pair $\alpha^\ast\dashv\alpha_\ast$ is actually an equivalence of categories, it suffices to show that the natural morphisms $\mathcal F\to\alpha_\ast\alpha^\ast\mathcal F$ and $\alpha^\ast\alpha_\ast\mathcal G\to\mathcal G$ are isomorphisms.  In other words, we must show that following natural maps are isomorphisms:
\begin{align}
\label{Funit}\mathcal F(U)&\to\varprojlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}K\subseteq U\cr K\textrm{ compact}\end{smallmatrix}}\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}K\subseteq U'\cr U'\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F(U')\\
\label{Gunit}\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}K\subseteq U\cr U\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\varprojlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}K'\subseteq U\cr K'\textrm{ compact}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal G(K')&\to\mathcal G(K)
\end{align}
The right hand side of \eqref{Funit} can be thought of as the inverse limit of $\mathcal F(U')$ over all open $U'\subseteq U$ with $\overline{U'}\subseteq U$ and $\overline{U'}$ compact.  It is easy to see that the map from $\mathcal F(U)$ to this inverse limit is an isomorphism if $\mathcal F$ is a sheaf.  The left hand side of \eqref{Gunit} can be identified with the left hand side of \eqref{sheafcontinuity}, and thus the map to $\mathcal G(K)$ is an isomorphism if $\mathcal F$ is a $\mathcal K$-sheaf.

Write $K:=\bigcap_{\alpha\in A}K_\alpha$.  Now consider the diagram:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
\varinjlim\limits_{\alpha\in A}\varinjlim\limits_{\begin{smallmatrix}K_\alpha\subseteq U\cr U\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F(\overline U)\ar{r}\ar{d}&
\varinjlim\limits_{\begin{smallmatrix}\exists\alpha\in A:K_\alpha\subseteq U\cr U\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F(\overline U)\ar{r}&
\varinjlim\limits_{\begin{smallmatrix}K\subseteq U\cr U\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F(\overline U)\ar{d}\\
\varinjlim\limits_{\alpha\in A}\mathcal F(K_\alpha)\ar{rr}&&\mathcal F(K)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
The vertical maps are both isomorphisms by \eqref{sheafcontinuity}.  The first horizontal map is an isomorphism since $\{K_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A}$ is filtered.  It thus remains to show that if $K\subseteq U$ and $U$ is open, then there exists $\alpha\in A$ such that $K_\alpha\subseteq U$.  Since $K\subseteq U$, we have $(X\setminus U)\cap K=\varnothing$, so $\bigcap_{\alpha\in A}(X\setminus U)\cap K_\alpha=\varnothing$.  This is a filtered directed system of compact sets, and so the intersection being empty implies that one of the terms $(X\setminus U)\cap K_\alpha$ is empty, so $K_\alpha\subseteq U$ as desired.

For $K\subseteq X$, there are natural maps:
\begin{equation}\label{trivialKequivalence}
\begin{tikzcd}
\check H^\bullet_K(Y;f_!\mathcal F)\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{f^\ast}&\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{f_!}\check H^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
(for $f^\ast$: pull back the open cover) (for $f_!$: add $Y\setminus K$ to the open cover and extend by zero).  It is easy to see that $f_!$ and $f^\ast$ are inverses.  The desired map $f_!:\check H^\bullet_c(X;\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet_c(Y;f_!\mathcal F)$ is defined as the composition:
\begin{equation}
\varinjlim_{K\subseteq X}\check H^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)\overset{\eqref{trivialKequivalence}}=\varinjlim_{K\subseteq X}\check H^\bullet_K(Y;f_!\mathcal F)\to\varinjlim_{K\subseteq Y}\check H^\bullet_K(Y;f_!\mathcal F)
\end{equation}
We must show that the second map is an isomorphism; to see this, it suffices to show that the following is an isomorphism for all $K\subseteq Y$:
\begin{equation}\label{supportcompactinXorY}
\varinjlim_{K'\subseteq X\cap K}\check H^\bullet_{K'}(Y;f_!\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet_K(Y;f_!\mathcal F)
\end{equation}
We claim that for any \v Cech cochain $\beta$ for $f_!\mathcal F$ with supports in $K$ subordinate to an open cover of $Y$, there is a refinement on which the restriction of $\beta$ has supports in some $K'\subseteq X\cap K$.  It follows from the claim (using a cofinality argument) that \eqref{supportcompactinXorY} is an isomorphism.  To prove the claim, argue as follows.

First, choose a refinement for which only finitely many open sets $U_1,\ldots,U_n$ intersect $K$ and for which the remaining open sets cover $Y\setminus K$.  Pick open sets $V_i\subseteq U_i$ which cover $K$ and for which $\overline{V_i}$ is compact and $\overline{V_i}\subseteq U_i$ (this is always possible).

Now $\beta$ is a finite collection $\{\beta_I\in(f_!\mathcal F)(\bigcap_{i\in I}U_i)\}_{\varnothing\ne I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}}$.  We have:
\begin{equation}\label{restrictionsupport}
\operatorname{supp}\Bigl(\beta_I|\bigcap_{i\in I}V_i\Bigr)\subseteq\operatorname{supp}\beta_I\cap\bigcap_{i\in I}\overline{V_i}
\end{equation}
Since $\operatorname{supp}\beta_I\subseteq\bigcap_{i\in I}U_i$ is (relatively) closed and $\bigcap_{i\in I}\overline{V_i}\subseteq\bigcap_{i\in I}U_i$ is compact, it follows that the right hand side is compact.  Also, we have $\operatorname{supp}\beta_I\subseteq X$ (by definition of $f_!\mathcal F$).  Hence the right hand side of \eqref{restrictionsupport} is a compact subset of $X$.  It follows that the restriction of $\beta$ to the refinement obtained by replacing $U_i$ with $V_i$ for $i=1,\ldots,n$ has support in a compact subset of $X$.

Let us denote by $\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F;\{U_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A})$ (resp.\ $\check H^\bullet(X;\alpha^\ast\mathcal F;\{K_i\}_{i=1}^n)$) the argument of the direct limit \eqref{cechcomplexI} (resp.\ \eqref{cechcomplexII}).

Since $X$ is compact, every open cover has a finite compact refinement.  This gives a map $\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet(X;\alpha^\ast\mathcal F)$.  To show that this map is an isomorphism, it suffices to show that for any fixed finite compact cover $\{K_i\}_{i=1}^n$, the following is an isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}X=\bigcup_{\alpha\in A}U_\alpha\cr\text{open cover refined by }\{K_i\}_{i=1}^n\end{smallmatrix}}\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F;\{U_\alpha\}_{\alpha\in A})\to\check H^\bullet(X;\alpha^\ast\mathcal F;\{K_i\}_{i=1}^n)
\end{equation}
By a cofinality argument, we can change the directed system on the left side to be open covers $\{U_i\}_{i=1}^n$ with $U_i\supseteq K_i$.  Hence it suffices to show that the following is an isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\varinjlim_{\{U_i\supseteq K_i\}_{i=1}^n}\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F;\{U_i\}_{i=1}^n)\to\check H^\bullet(X;\alpha^\ast\mathcal F;\{K_i\}_{i=1}^n)
\end{equation}
This is clear from the definition of $\alpha^\ast$ and from Lemma \ref{intersectioncofinal}.

There is clearly a long exact sequence:
\begin{equation}\label{cechmappingconeles}
\cdots\to\check H^{\bullet-1}(X;\mathcal G^\bullet)\to\check H^\bullet(X;[\mathcal F^\bullet\to\mathcal G^{\bullet-1}])\to\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F^\bullet)\to\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal G^\bullet)\to\cdots
\end{equation}
Hence it suffices to show that if $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is acyclic then $\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F^\bullet)=0$.  This is true because then each \v Cech complex has a finite filtration whose associated graded is acyclic.

It suffices to show that for all $\alpha\in\mathcal F(K)$, there exists a finite compact cover $X=\bigcup_{i=1}^nK_i$ such that $\mathcal F(K)\to\bigoplus_{i=1}^n\mathcal F(K\cap K_i)$ annihilates $\alpha$.  We consider the commutative diagram:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
\varinjlim_{K\subseteq U}\mathcal F(\overline U)@>>>\prod_{p\in K}\varinjlim_{p\in U}\mathcal F(\overline U)\cr
@VVV@VVV\cr
\mathcal F(K)@>>>\prod_{p\in K}\mathcal F_p
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
where the vertical maps are both isomorphisms.  Now the vanishing of $\mathcal F_p$ and a compactness argument shows that there are finitely many open sets $U_i\subseteq X$ covering $K$ such that $\alpha$ vanishes in $\mathcal F(K\cap\overline{U_i})$ for all $i$.  Thus the compact cover $X=(X\setminus\bigcup_{i=1}^nU_i)\cup\overline{U_1}\cup\cdots\cup\overline{U_n}$ has the desired properties.

For any $K\subseteq X$, we have a commutative diagram:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet(K)@>\sim>>\check H^\bullet(K;\mathcal F^\bullet)\cr
@VVV@VVV\cr
H^\bullet\mathcal G^\bullet(K)@>\sim>>\check H^\bullet(K;\mathcal G^\bullet)
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
The rows are isomorphisms by Proposition \ref{homotopysheafcechquasiisomorphism}.  If $\mathcal F^\bullet_p\to\mathcal G^\bullet_p$ is a quasi-isomorphism, then the right vertical map is a quasi-isomorphism by Proposition \ref{cechdeterminedbystalks}.

The isomorphism \eqref{cechsimplecanonicaliso} is defined as the composition of the following isomorphisms:
\begin{equation}\label{isosforpurehtpysheafcech}
\begin{matrix}
H^\bullet[\mathcal F^\bullet_0(X)\to\cdots\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-n}_n(X)]\cr
\downarrow\cr
\check H^\bullet(X;[\mathcal F^\bullet_0\to\cdots\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-n}_n])\cr
\downarrow\cr
\check H^\bullet(X;[\tau_{\geq 0}\mathcal F^\bullet_0\to\cdots\to\tau_{\geq n}\mathcal F^{\bullet-n}_n])\cr
\uparrow\cr
\check H^\bullet(X;[H^0\mathcal F^\bullet_0\to\cdots\to H^n\mathcal F^{\bullet-n}_n])
\end{matrix}
\end{equation}
The maps are isomorphisms for the following reasons.  Map one: $[\mathcal F^\bullet_0\to\cdots\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-n}_n]$ is a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf (Lemma \ref{associatedgradedhomotopysheaf}), and a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf calculates its own $\check H^\bullet$ (Proposition \ref{homotopysheafcechquasiisomorphism}).  Map two: the map of coefficient $\mathcal K$-presheaves is a quasi-isomorphism (Lemma \ref{cechsimplebasic}) and thus induces an isomorphism on $\check H^\bullet$ (Lemma \ref{qicech}).  Map three: the map of coefficient $\mathcal K$-presheaves is a quasi-isomorphism on stalks (by purity), and thus induces an isomorphism on $\check H^\bullet$ (Proposition \ref{cechdeterminedbystalks}).

It suffices to show the weak vanishing property for $\mathcal F^\bullet$.  Let $\alpha\in H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K)$ be arbitrary with $i<0$.  By Remark \ref{nbhdintersectiongood} and Lemma \ref{anycompactintersectionworks}, the following is a quasi-isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}Z\subseteq V\cr V\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap\overline V)\to\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap Z)
\end{equation}
Since $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap Z)=0$ by strong vanishing for $i^\ast\mathcal F^\bullet$, we see that the image of $\alpha$ in $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap\overline V)$ vanishes for some open $V\supseteq Z$.  Now applying \eqref{homotopysheafproperty} to $K=(K\cap\overline V)\cup(K\setminus V)$, we see that the vanishing of the image of $\alpha$ in $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap\overline V)$ implies that $\alpha$ ``comes from'' the cohomology of $[\mathcal F^\bullet(K\setminus V)\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-1}(K\cap\overline V\cap(K\setminus V))$].  On the other hand, this latter group vanishes in degrees $i<0$ by strong vanishing for $j^\ast\mathcal F^\bullet$.  Thus $\alpha=0$, so we have even shown strong vanishing for $\mathcal F^\bullet$.

Statement (\ref{scacyc}) is obvious.

Statement (\ref{scpatch}) can be deduced from Mayer--Vietoris using a form of the nine lemma as we now explain.  Let us write $U:=X\setminus A$ and $V:=X\setminus B$.  Now consider the following total complex:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
C_\bullet(U\cap V)@>>>C_{\bullet+1}(U)\oplus C_{\bullet+1}(V)@>>>C_{\bullet+2}(U\cup V)\cr
@VVV@VVV@VVV\cr
C_{\bullet+1}(X)@>>>C_{\bullet+2}(X)\oplus C_{\bullet+2}(X)@>>>C_{\bullet+3}(X)\cr
@VVV@VVV@VVV\cr
C_{\bullet+2}(X,U\cap V)@>>>C_{\bullet+3}(X,U)\oplus C_{\bullet+3}(X,V)@>>>C_{\bullet+4}(X,U\cup V)\cr
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
The columns are acyclic (by definition of relative chains), and hence the total complex is acyclic as well.  The first row is acyclic by Mayer--Vietoris, and the second row is obviously acyclic.  Thus the third row is acyclic, as needed.

Statement (\ref{sccont}) is true because the map is in fact an isomorphism on the chain level.  It is clearly surjective; to show injectivity we must show that a singular chain on $X$ which is disjoint from $K$ is in fact disjoint from $K_\alpha$ for some $\alpha\in A$.  This follows because the standard $n$-simplex is compact.

Let $X^+$ be the one-point compactification of $X$.  Define a complex of $\mathcal K$-presheaves $\mathcal F^\bullet$ on $X^+$:
\begin{equation}\label{purehomotopyKsheafinPD}
\mathcal F^\bullet(K):=[C_{n-1-\bullet}(N,N\setminus K)\to C_{n-\bullet}(M,M\setminus K)]
\end{equation}
(where on the right hand side by $K$ we really mean $K\cap X$).  Applying Lemmas \ref{singularchainshomotopyKsheaf} and \ref{associatedgradedhomotopysheaf}, we see that $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.

We claim that $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.  Certainly the homology of $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is bounded below, because the singular chain complex of a topological manifold has homology bounded above and $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is built out of these.  Now, it is easy to calculate:
\begin{equation}\label{stalkhomologyofPDpure}
H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet_p=\begin{cases}\mathbb Z&p\in M^\circ\cup N^\circ\cr 0&p\notin M^\circ\cup N^\circ\end{cases}
\end{equation}
concentrated in degree zero (see \cite[p231 \S3.3]{hatcher} for the special case $N=\partial M=\varnothing$).  Hence $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.  In fact, it is not hard to see (using the adjunction $j_!\dashv j^\ast$) that \eqref{stalkhomologyofPDpure} lifts to an isomorphism of sheaves $f_!i^\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_M\to H^0\mathcal F^\bullet$ where $f:X\hookrightarrow X^+$.

Now we conclude:
\begin{equation*}
H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet(X^+)\overset{\text{Prop \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}}}=\check H^\bullet(X^+;f_!i^\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_M)\overset{\text{Lem \ref{cptcechembeddingproperty}}}=\check H^\bullet_c(X;i^\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_M)
\end{equation*}
Now observe that $H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet(X^+)$ is the left hand side of \eqref{poincaredualitygoal}.

Certainly $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf by Lemma \ref{pihomotopysheaf}.  Since $A$ is finite, it is easy to see that $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet_{IJ}$ being bounded below implies that $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet$ is bounded below.

Now let us calculate $\mathcal F^\bullet_p$ using the spectral sequence associated to the $p$-filtration.  The $E_1$ term is concentrated along the $q=0$ row since $(H^q\mathcal F^\bullet_{IJ})_p$ is concentrated in degree zero; thus there are no further differentials after the $E_1$ page.  On the $E_1$ page the differentials coincide precisely with the differentials in the definition of $\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{I\subseteq J\subseteq A}(\mathcal G_{IJ})_p$ (regarding $\mathcal G_{IJ}$ as complexes concentrated in degree zero).  Hence we have an isomorphism:
\begin{equation}\label{FGiso}
H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet_p=H^\bullet\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{I\subseteq J\subseteq A}(\mathcal G_{IJ})_p
\end{equation}
To calculate the right hand side of \eqref{FGiso}, let us start with the trivial observation that:
\begin{equation}
(\mathcal G_{IJ})_p=\begin{cases}\mathcal G_p&p\in U_I\cap U_J\cr 0&p\notin U_I\cap U_J\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Now consider $(2^A)_p:=\{I\subseteq A:p\in U_I\}$, which satisfies the following properties:
\begin{rlist}
\item$(2^A)_p$ has a maximal element (restatement of (\ref{nonnestedintersectionbig})).
\item$I,K\in(2^A)_p$ implies $J\in(2^A)_p$ for $I\subseteq J\subseteq K$ (restatement of (\ref{nestedintersectionOK})).
\end{rlist}
These two properties imply that $\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{I\subseteq J\subseteq A}(\mathcal G_{IJ})_p$ is simply $\mathcal G_p$ tensored with the simplicial chain complex of the nerve of $(2^A)_p$ (which is contractible).  Hence \eqref{FGiso} gives a canonical isomorphism of stalks $H^0\mathcal F^\bullet_p=\mathcal G_p$ (and thus in particular $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is pure).

Now it remains to construct a canonical isomorphism of sheaves $\mathcal G\xrightarrow\sim H^0\mathcal F^\bullet$.  Over the open set $U_I$, we define this isomorphism to be the composition $\mathcal G_{II}\xrightarrow\sim H^0\mathcal F_{II}^\bullet\to H^0\mathcal F^\bullet$ (the second map being the inclusion of a $p=0$ subcomplex in the homotopy colimit).  At any point $p\in U_I$, this specializes to the isomorphism of stalks $\mathcal G_p=H^0\mathcal F^\bullet_p$ defined earlier.  Hence since $\{U_I\}_{I\subseteq A}$ is an open cover of $X$, these isomorphisms patch together to give the desired global isomorphism.

The first term $\bigl(\nabla\xi+T_\nabla(\xi,du_\alpha)\bigr)^{0,1}_y$ comes from differentiating:
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\xi\to u_\alpha}\left[\bar\partial_y\exp_{u_\alpha}\xi\right]
\end{equation}
The second two terms come from differentiating:
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\xi\to u_\alpha}\left[\lambda(e_0)(\alpha,y,x_1,\ldots,x_r,\cdot,(\exp_{u_\alpha}\xi)(\cdot))\right]
\end{equation}
where we use the fact that $\frac d{dt}x_i(\exp_{u_\alpha}(t\xi))=-\operatorname{proj}_{TC_\alpha}\xi(x_i)$.  The last term comes from differentiating:
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\xi\to u_\alpha}\left[\lambda(\operatorname{proj}_E\xi)(\alpha,y,x_1,\ldots,x_r,\cdot,(\exp_{u_\alpha}\xi)(\cdot))\right]
\end{equation}
We leave the calculations to the reader.

The operator $D_{\alpha,y}$ equals $(\nabla\xi)^{0,1}_y$ plus compact terms (by Lemma \ref{linearizedformula}), so it is equivalent to show that $\xi\mapsto(\nabla\xi)^{0,1}_y$ is Fredholm (and we may forget about $E$).  Here $\nabla$ is really the pullback of $\nabla$ via $u_\alpha$.  Thus it suffices to show that if $V$ is a smooth complex vector bundle over a nodal Riemann surface $C$ equipped with a smooth connection $\nabla$, then the horizontal maps:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = large]
W^{k,2,\delta}(C,V)\ar{r}{\xi\mapsto(\nabla\xi)^{0,1}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C V)\\
W^{k,2}(C,V)\ar[hook]{u}\ar{r}{\xi\mapsto(\nabla\xi)^{0,1}}&W^{k-1,2}(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C V)\ar[hook]{u}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
are Fredholm.  For the bottom map, the Fredholm property is standard.  Indeed, $W^{k,2}(C,V)$ lies inside $W^{k,2}(\tilde C,V)$ as a closed subspace of finite codimension, and $\nabla^{0,1}:W^{k,2}(\tilde C,V)\to W^{k-1,2}(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C V)$ is Fredholm by elliptic regularity on the compact Riemann surface $\tilde C$.  For the top map, the Fredholm property follows from results of Lockhart--McOwen \cite{lockhartmcowen} concerning the Fredholmness (in weighted Sobolev spaces) of elliptic operators on manifolds with cylindrical ends.  Specifically, $W^{k,2,\delta}(C,V)$ lies inside $W^{k,2,\delta}(\tilde C,V)$ as a closed subspace of finite codimension, and the map $\nabla^{0,1}:W^{k,2,\delta}(\tilde C,V)\to W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C V)$ is Fredholm by the theory of Lockhart--McOwen \cite{lockhartmcowen}.  This uses the fact that the weight $\delta\in(0,1)$ is not an eigenvalue of the asymptotic linearized operator in any end (the set of eigenvalues in any end is precisely $\mathbb Z$, corresponding to the powers $\{z^n\}_{n\in\mathbb Z}$ on $D^2\setminus 0$).  Let us also remark that both of these Fredholmness statements are easier at $p=2$ than for general $p\in(1,\infty)$, since for $p=2$ one can use Fourier analysis (after localizing the problem with a partition of unity).

Now it remains to show that the induced maps on kernel and cokernel are isomorphisms.  The map on kernels is obviously injective, and the map on cokernels is easily seen to be surjective (use the fact that the image of the top horizontal map is closed and the fact that the rightmost vertical inclusion is dense).

For the surjectivity of the map on kernels and the injectivity of the map on cokernels, it suffices to show that if $D_{\alpha,y}\xi=\eta$ for $\xi\in W^{k,2,\delta}$ and $\eta\in W^{k-1,2}$, then $\xi\in W^{k,2}$.  To show $\xi\in W^{k,2}$, it suffices to argue locally on one side of a given node.

Certainly (the vector field part of) $\xi$ extends continuously to $C_\alpha$ (by definition of $W^{k,2,\delta}$), and this continuous extension satisfies $D_{\alpha,y}\xi=\eta+\epsilon$ where $\epsilon$ is a distribution on $\tilde C_\alpha$ valued in $\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TX$ and supported inside the (inverse images of the) nodes.  By elliptic regularity, it suffices to show that $\epsilon=0$.

\newlength\arrowwidthawesome
\settowidth{\arrowwidthawesome}{$\nrightarrow$}
Now for smooth test functions $\varphi$ supported inside a small neighborhood of the support of $\epsilon$, we have:
\begin{equation}
\langle\epsilon,\varphi\rangle=\langle D_{\alpha,y}\xi-\eta,\varphi\rangle=\langle\xi,D_{\alpha,y}^\ast\varphi\rangle-\langle\eta,\varphi\rangle
\end{equation}
We thus obtain the bound $\left|\langle\epsilon,\varphi\rangle\right|\leq c\left\|\varphi\right\|_{1,1}$ since $D_{\alpha,y}^\ast$ is a first order operator and $\xi,\eta$ are bounded (as they are continuous).  On the other hand, $\epsilon$ is supported at a finite set of points, so it is a linear combination of $\delta$-functions and their derivatives.  Hence $\epsilon$ does not satisfy the bound $\left|\langle\epsilon,\varphi\rangle\right|\leq c\left\|\varphi\right\|_{1,1}$ (recall that $W^{1,1}\hookrightarrow\hspace{-\arrowwidthawesome}\nrightarrow C^0$ since we are in two dimensions) unless $\epsilon=0$, as desired.

Away from the ends/necks of $C_\alpha$, the expression is zero when $y=0$ (since $(0,0,u_0,\{x_i^0\},e_0)\in\overline{\mathcal M}(X)$) and more generally it follows easily that its $j$th derivative is bounded pointwise by $c_j\left|y\right|$.  In the ends of $C_\alpha$, the expression is zero.

In a given neck $[0,6S]\times S^1\subseteq C_\alpha$, argue as follows.  Recall that $\lambda$ vanishes in the ends/necks, so we just have to bound $\bar\partial_yu_\alpha$.  Now $\bar\partial_yu_\alpha$ is only nonzero over $[S-1,S]\cup[5S,5S+1]$; by symmetry we may just work over $[S-1,S]$.  Now since $u_0$ is smooth, it and all its derivatives decay exponentially in any cylindrical end $[0,\infty)\times S^1$ (since every end is holomorphically conjugate to the map $[0,\infty)\times S^1\to D^2$ given by $z=e^{-s-it}$).  Precisely, we have the following pointwise bound over $[S-1,S]$:
\begin{equation}\label{uzeroexponentialdecay}
\left|D^ju_0(s,t)\right|\leq c_je^{-S}
\end{equation}
(derivatives with respect to $s$ and $t$).  It follows from the definition of $u_\alpha$ that the same bound holds for $u_\alpha$.  Thus the contribution of $[S-1,S]$ to the norm of $\bar\partial_yu_\alpha$ is bounded by a constant times $e^{S\delta}e^{-S}$.

Away from the ends/necks, the norm is bounded by $c\cdot\left|y\right|\left\|\kappa\right\|$.  In the ends, the expression is zero.  In the necks, argue as follows.

The expression is only nonzero over $[S-1,S+1]\cup[5S-1,5S+1]$; by symmetry we may just work over $[S-1,S+1]$.  Then since $u_0$ and $\kappa$ are smooth, we have (by the same reasoning used to justify \eqref{uzeroexponentialdecay}) the following pointwise bound over $[S-1,S+1]$:
\begin{equation}
\left|D^j\kappa_\alpha(s,t)\right|\leq c_je^{-S}\left\|\kappa\right\|
\end{equation}
(derivatives with respect to $s$ and $t$, in local coordinates \eqref{localPTcoords}).  Thus the overall contribution of $[S-1,S+1]$ to the total norm of the expression is bounded by a constant times $e^{S\delta}e^{-S}\left\|\kappa\right\|$.

In simpler terms, we bound the operator norm of the difference between the two diagonal compositions:
\begin{equation}\label{betterPTcommutingoperatornormbound}
\left\|\mathsf{PT}\circ D_{0,y}-D_{0|\alpha,y}\circ\mathsf{PT}\right\|\leq c\cdot\sum_{i=1}^de^{-S_i}
\end{equation}
(this trivially implies the claimed statement).  To show \eqref{betterPTcommutingoperatornormbound}, observe that the two operators only differ over the $[S-1,\infty)$ subset of each end $[0,\infty)\times S^1\subseteq C_0$.  Using estimates \eqref{uzeroexponentialdecay} arising from the fact that $u_0$ is smooth, we obtain the desired bound.

We work by induction on $n$.  The case $n=1$ is clear: $\epsilon(1,c,\delta)=\infty$.

Now assume $n\geq 2$.  Let us also assume without loss of generality that $c\geq 1$.  Applying \eqref{keytocheckforARI} to $i=n$ and $\xi=Q_nB_{n-1}\eta$, we see that:
\begin{equation}
\left\|D_{n-1}G_{n-1}Q_nB_{n-1}-\mathbf 1_{Y_{n-1}}\right\|\leq\epsilon\cdot\left\|Q_n\right\|\left\|B_{n-1}\right\|\leq\epsilon c^2
\end{equation}
Let us require that $\epsilon\leq\frac 12c^{-2}$, so the above bound is $\leq\frac 12$.  Then by Lemma \ref{bootstrapinverse} applied to $T=G_{n-1}Q_nB_{n-1}$, we see that there exists $Q_{n-1}$ with $D_{n-1}Q_{n-1}=\mathbf 1_{Y_{n-1}}$ and:
\begin{equation}
\left\|Q_{n-1}\right\|\leq 2c^3\qquad\left\|Q_{n-1}-G_{n-1}Q_nB_{n-1}\right\|\leq 2\epsilon c^5
\end{equation}
Now we see that:
\begin{multline}\label{approxclosetogiven}
\left\|D_1G_1\cdots G_{n-2}G_{n-1}Q_nB_{n-1}B_{n-2}\cdots B_1-D_1G_1\cdots G_{n-2}Q_{n-1}B_{n-2}\cdots B_1\right\|\\
\leq c^{2n-3}\left\|G_{n-1}Q_nB_{n-1}-Q_{n-1}\right\|\leq 2\epsilon c^{2n+2}
\end{multline}
Let us require that $\epsilon\leq\frac 14\delta c^{-2n-2}$, so the above bound is $\leq\frac 12\delta$.  Let us also require that $\epsilon\leq\epsilon(n-1,2c^3,\frac 12\delta)$ (which exists by the induction hypothesis), so that:
\begin{equation}\label{inductionbound}
\left\|D_1G_1\cdots G_{n-2}Q_{n-1}B_{n-2}\cdots B_1-\mathbf 1_{Y_1}\right\|\leq\frac 12\delta
\end{equation}
Combining \eqref{approxclosetogiven} and \eqref{inductionbound}, we get the desired bound \eqref{approxbootstrappingworks}.

This is similar to McDuff--Salamon \cite[p68, Proposition 3.5.3]{mcduffsalamonJholsymp}.

We have already remarked that $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi$ is defined for $\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$.

Let $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}'(\zeta,\xi)$ denote the derivative of $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}$ at $\zeta$ applied to $\xi$.  So, for instance, $D_{\alpha,y}(\xi):=\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}'(0,\xi)$.  It suffices to show that:
\begin{equation}\label{derivislip}
\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}'(0,\xi)-\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}'(\zeta,\xi)\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}\leq c\cdot\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}
\end{equation}
for $\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$ uniformly as $(\alpha,y)\to 0$ (one recovers \eqref{quadestgoal} by integrating $\int_{\xi_1}^{\xi_2}\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}'(0,d\zeta)-\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}'(\zeta,d\zeta)$).

For $\zeta\in W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TX)_D\oplus E$, let:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal F_{\alpha,y,\zeta}:W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,(\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta)^\ast TX)_D\oplus E\to W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C(\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta)^\ast TX)
\end{equation}
denote the $\bar\partial$-section based at $\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta:C_\alpha\to X$ and $e_0+\operatorname{proj}_E\zeta$ (so, for example, $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}:=\mathcal F_{\alpha,y,0}$).  Let:
\begin{equation}
D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}:W^{k,2,\delta}(C_\alpha,(\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta)^\ast TX)_D\oplus E\to W^{k-1,2,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C(\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta)^\ast TX)
\end{equation}
denote the derivative of $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y,\zeta}$ at zero.  Of course, $D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}$ may be calculated as in Lemma \ref{linearizedformula}, and the result is the same (i.e.\ we just substitute $(\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta,e_0+\operatorname{proj}_E\zeta)$ in place of $(u_\alpha,e_0)$).

Now the first step in proving \eqref{derivislip} is to express $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}'(\zeta,\xi)$ in terms of $D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}$.  To do this, we observe that:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(a)=\left[\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}a\to u_\alpha}\circ\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to\exp_{u_\alpha}a}\right]\left[\mathcal F_{\alpha,y,\zeta}\Bigl((\exp_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}^{-1}\exp_{u_\alpha}a)\oplus(\operatorname{proj}_Ea-\operatorname{proj}_E\zeta)\Bigr)\right]
\end{equation}
We now differentiate with respect to $a$ and evaluate at $a=\zeta$ and $\dot a=\xi$.  We find:
\begin{multline}\label{changebasisderivative}
\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}'(\zeta,\xi)=\Biggl[\frac d{da}\biggr|_{\begin{smallmatrix}a=\zeta\cr\dot a=\xi\end{smallmatrix}}\Bigl(\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}a\to u_\alpha}\circ\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to\exp_{u_\alpha}a}\Bigr)\Biggr]\bigl(\mathcal F_{\alpha,y,\zeta}(0)\bigr)\\
+\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to u_\alpha}\Biggl[D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}\Biggl(\frac d{da}\biggr|_{\begin{smallmatrix}a=\zeta\cr\dot a=\xi\end{smallmatrix}}\Bigl(\exp_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}^{-1}\exp_{u_\alpha}a\Bigr)\oplus\operatorname{proj}_E\xi\Biggr)\Biggr]
\end{multline}
We rewrite the first term:
\begin{equation}
\Biggl[\frac d{da}\biggr|_{\begin{smallmatrix}a=\zeta\cr\dot a=\xi\end{smallmatrix}}\Bigl(\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}a\to u_\alpha}\circ\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to\exp_{u_\alpha}a}\Bigr)\Biggr]\mathsf{PT}_{u_\alpha\to\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}\bigl(\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(\zeta)\bigr)
\end{equation}
We know that $\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(\zeta)\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}$ is bounded uniformly for $(\alpha,y)\to 0$ and $\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$ (Lemma \ref{mappregluingissmall} implies that $\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(0)\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}$ is bounded as $(\alpha,y)\to 0$, and from this one may derive a bound on $\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(\zeta)\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}$ in terms of $\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}$).  The operator $[\frac d{da}(\mathsf{PT}\circ\mathsf{PT})]\mathsf{PT}$ in front is of the form $H(\zeta,\xi)$ for a smooth (non-linear) bundle map $H:TX\oplus TX\to\operatorname{End}(TX)$ (defined in a neighborhood of $\zeta=\xi=0$).  Since $H$ satisfies $H(0,\cdot)=H(\cdot,0)=0$, it follows that $\left\|H(\zeta,\xi)\right\|_{k,2,\delta}$ is bounded by $c\cdot\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}$ for $\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta},\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$.  Hence the $\left\|\cdot\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}$-norm of the first term in \eqref{changebasisderivative} is bounded by $c\cdot\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}$, so for the purposes of proving \eqref{derivislip} it may be ignored.

The second term in \eqref{changebasisderivative} is approximated by $\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to u_\alpha}\left[D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}\left(\mathsf{PT}_{u_\alpha\to\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}\xi\right)\right]$ with error:
\begin{equation}\label{errorexpression}
\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to u_\alpha}\Biggl[D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}\Biggl(\biggl[\mathsf{PT}_{u_\alpha\to\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}\xi-\frac d{da}\biggr|_{\begin{smallmatrix}a=\zeta\cr\dot a=\xi\end{smallmatrix}}\Bigl(\exp_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}^{-1}\exp_{u_\alpha}a\Bigr)\biggr]\oplus 0\Biggr)\Biggr]
\end{equation}
which we may write as:
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to u_\alpha}\Biggl[D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}\Biggl(\mathsf{PT}_{u_\alpha\to\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}\biggl[\xi-\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to u_\alpha}\frac d{da}\biggr|_{\begin{smallmatrix}a=\zeta\cr\dot a=\xi\end{smallmatrix}}\Bigl(\exp_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}^{-1}\exp_{u_\alpha}a\Bigr)\biggr]\oplus 0\Biggr)\Biggr]
\end{equation}
Now the $\left\|\cdot\right\|_{(k-1,2,\delta)\to(k-1,2,\delta)}$ norm of the outer $\mathsf{PT}$ is bounded uniformly for $(\alpha,y)\to 0$ and $\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$, as is the $\left\|\cdot\right\|_{(k,2,\delta)\to(k-1,2,\delta)}$ norm of $D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}$ and the $\left\|\cdot\right\|_{(k,2,\delta)\to(k,2,\delta)}$ norm of the following $\mathsf{PT}$.  The difference $\xi-\mathsf{PT}\frac d{da}()$ is of the form $H(\zeta,\xi)$ for a smooth (non-linear) bundle map $H:TX\oplus TX\to TX$ (defined in a neighborhood of $\zeta=\xi=0$).  Since $H$ satisfies $H(0,\cdot)=H(\cdot,0)=0$, it follows that $\left\|H(\zeta,\xi)\right\|_{k,2,\delta}$ is bounded by $c\cdot\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}$ for $\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta},\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$.  Hence the error \eqref{errorexpression} has $\left\|\cdot\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}$ bounded by $c\cdot\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}$.

Thus we have reduced the estimate \eqref{derivislip} to proving:
\begin{equation}\label{quadreducedtoPTcomp}
\left\|D_{\alpha,y}-\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta\to u}\circ D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}\circ\mathsf{PT}_{u_\alpha\to\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta}\right\|_{(k,2,\delta)\to(k-1,2,\delta)}\leq c\cdot\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta}
\end{equation}
We calculated $D_{\alpha,y}$ in Lemma \ref{linearizedformula}, and $D_{\alpha,y,\zeta}$ may be expressed in exactly the same way (specifically, it is obtained by taking the expression for $D_{\alpha,y}$ and replacing every occurence of $u_\alpha$ with $\exp_{u_\alpha}\zeta$ and $e_0$ by $e_0+\operatorname{proj}_E\zeta$).  Now we compare term by term to prove \eqref{quadreducedtoPTcomp}.  We omit the details of this calculation.

In fact, we will show that $\kappa_{\alpha,y}$ is given explicitly as the limit of the Newton iteration:
\begin{align}
\xi_0&:=\kappa_\alpha\\
\xi_n&:=\xi_{n-1}-Q_{\alpha,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi_{n-1}
\end{align}
By Lemma \ref{contractionprop}, the map $\xi\mapsto\xi-Q_{\alpha,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi$ is a $\frac 12$-contraction mapping when restricted to:
\begin{equation}\label{domainofcontraction}
\{\xi\in\kappa_\alpha+\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}:\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'\}
\end{equation}
To finish the proof, it suffices to show that (for sufficiently small $(\alpha,y,\kappa)$) \eqref{domainofcontraction} is nonempty and is mapped to itself by $\xi\mapsto\xi-Q_{\alpha,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi$.

We know that $\left\|\kappa_\alpha\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\to 0$ as $\kappa\to 0$ (uniformly in $(\alpha,y)$), so \eqref{domainofcontraction} is nonempty.  By using Proposition \ref{quadestimate} with $(\xi_1,\xi_2)=(0,\kappa_\alpha)$ and Lemmas \ref{mappregluingissmall} and \ref{pregluingKestimate}, we conclude that:
\begin{equation}\label{kernelclosetoholomorphic}
\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\kappa_\alpha\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}\to 0
\end{equation}
as $(\alpha,y,\kappa)\to 0$.  Since the operator norm of $Q_{\alpha,y}$ is bounded uniformly as $(\alpha,y)\to 0$, we see that $\kappa_\alpha$ is almost fixed by $\xi\mapsto\xi-Q_{\alpha,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi$ as $(\alpha,y,\kappa)\to 0$.  It then follows from the contraction property that $\xi\mapsto\xi-Q_{\alpha,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}\xi$ maps \eqref{domainofcontraction} to itself.

Suppose that:
\begin{equation}
(\alpha,y,u_{\alpha,y,\kappa},e_{\alpha,y,\kappa})=(\alpha',y',u_{\alpha',y',\kappa'},e_{\alpha',y',\kappa'})
\end{equation}
We see immediately that $(\alpha,y)=(\alpha',y')$.  Now we see that:
\begin{align}
\exp_{u_\alpha}\kappa_{\alpha,y}&=\exp_{u_\alpha}(\kappa')_{\alpha,y}\\
\operatorname{proj}_E\kappa_{\alpha,y}&=\operatorname{proj}_E(\kappa')_{\alpha,y}
\end{align}
Since the norms of $\kappa_{\alpha,y}$ and $\kappa_{\alpha,y}'$ go to zero as $(\alpha,y,\kappa,\kappa')\to 0$ (in $W^{k,2,\delta}$, and hence in $C^0$) and the injectivity radius of the exponential map is fixed, we see that $\kappa_{\alpha,y}=(\kappa')_{\alpha,y}$.  It follows that $\kappa_\alpha-(\kappa')_\alpha\in\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}$, but since $K_\alpha\cap\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}=0$ we conclude $\kappa_\alpha=(\kappa')_\alpha$ and hence $\kappa=\kappa'$.

The key ingredient in this proof is our precise control of the image of the right inverse $Q_{\alpha,y}$ (specifically, that $\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}=\ker L_\alpha$).

Suppose $(\alpha_i,y_i,\kappa_i)\to(\alpha,y,\kappa)$ is a convergent net.\footnote{We could restrict to sequences rather than nets since $\mathbb C^d\times\mathbb R^{\dim\overline{\mathcal M}^d}\times K$ is first countable.  However, this would not make the argument any simpler.}  We will show that:
\begin{equation}
(u_{\alpha_i,y_i,\kappa_i},e_{\alpha_i,y_i,\kappa_i})\to(u_{\alpha,y,\kappa},e_{\alpha,y,\kappa})
\end{equation}

First, we claim that $\left\|(\kappa_i)_{\alpha_i,y_i}-\kappa_{\alpha_i,y_i}\right\|_\infty\to 0$.  In fact, we will show the stronger statement that $\left\|(\kappa_i)_{\alpha_i,y_i}-\kappa_{\alpha_i,y_i}\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\to 0$.  Since the Newton iteration converges uniformly, it suffices to show that $\bigl\|(\kappa_i)_{\alpha_i,y_i}^{n}-\kappa_{\alpha_i,y_i}^{n}\bigr\|_{k,2,\delta}\to 0$ for all $n\geq 0$, where $\kappa_{\alpha,y}^{n}$ denotes the $n$th step of the Newton iteration converging to $\kappa_{\alpha,y}$.  It is easy to verify this for $n=0$:
\begin{equation}
\left\|(\kappa_i)_{\alpha_i,y_i}^{0}-\kappa_{\alpha_i,y_i}^{0}\right\|_{k,2,\delta}=\left\|(\kappa_i-\kappa)_{\alpha_i}\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\to 0
\end{equation}
The inductive step follows from the fact that $Q_{\alpha_i,y_i}$ is uniformly bounded and $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,y_i}$ is uniformly Lipschitz (e.g.\ as a consequence of Proposition \ref{quadestimate}).  Now from the claim, we see that it suffices to show that:
\begin{equation}
(u_{\alpha_i,y_i,\kappa},e_{\alpha_i,y_i,\kappa})\to(u_{\alpha,y,\kappa},e_{\alpha,y,\kappa})
\end{equation}

Recall that by definition:
\begin{align}
u_{\alpha,y,\kappa}&=\exp_{u_\alpha}\kappa_{\alpha,y}&\kappa_{\alpha,y}&=\kappa_\alpha+\xi&\text{for some }\xi&\in\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}\\
u_{\alpha_i,y_i,\kappa}&=\exp_{u_{\alpha_i}}\kappa_{\alpha_i,y_i}&\kappa_{\alpha_i,y_i}&=\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_i&\text{for some }\xi_i&\in\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha_i,y_i}
\end{align}
Now we define $\xi_{\alpha_i}\in W^{k,2,\delta}(C_{\alpha_i},u_{\alpha_i}^\ast TX)_D\oplus E$ by ``pregluing'' $\xi$ from $C_\alpha$ to $C_{\alpha_i}$ as follows.  Note that we may assume without loss of generality that at the nodes where $\alpha\ne 0$, we also have $\alpha_i\ne 0$.  Away from the ends/necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$, we set $\xi_{\alpha_i}=\xi$.  Over the ends of $C_{\alpha_i}$, note there is a corresponding end of $C_\alpha$, so we may also simply set $\xi_{\alpha_i}=\xi$ over the ends of $C_{\alpha_i}$.  Over the necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$ for which $\alpha=0$, we define $\xi_{\alpha_i}$ via \eqref{kernelpregluingformula} (note that this is reasonable since $\xi$ is smooth on $C_{\alpha}$).  Over the necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$ for which $\alpha\ne 0$ we define $\xi_{\alpha_i}$ as:
\begin{equation}\label{xitoIpregluingforcontinuous}
\xi_{\alpha_i}(s,t):=\mathsf{PT}_{u_{\alpha}(f_i(s),t)\to u_{\alpha_i}(s,t)}[\xi(f_i(s),t))]
\end{equation}
where $f_i:[0,6S_i]\to[0,6S]$ is defined as follows:
\begin{align}
f_i(s):=&\begin{cases}
s&s\leq S-2\cr
s-3S_i+3S&3S_i-2S+2\leq s\leq 3S_i+2S-2\cr
s-6S_i+6S&6S_i-S+2\leq s\cr
\end{cases}\\
f_i([S-2,3S_i-2S+2])&\subseteq[S-2,S+2]\\
f_i([3S_i+2S-2,6S_i-S+2])&\subseteq[5S-2,5S+2]
\end{align}
and is smooth with absolutely bounded derivatives of all orders.  More informally, $f_i$ is smooth and matches up $[0,S-2],[S+2,5S-2],[5S+2,6S]\subseteq[0,6S]$ with corresponding intervals of the same length inside $[0,6S_i]$, symmetrically.

Now the $C^0$-distance between:
\begin{align}
u_{\alpha,y,\kappa}=\exp_{u_\alpha}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)&:C_\alpha\to X\quad\text{and}\cr
\exp_{u_{\alpha_i}}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})&:C_{\alpha_i}\to X
\end{align}
goes to zero (this is easy to see from \eqref{kernelpregluingformula} and \eqref{xitoIpregluingforcontinuous}).  Hence it suffices to show that $\left\|\xi_i-\xi_{\alpha_i}\right\|_\infty\to 0$.  In fact, we will prove the stronger statement that:
\begin{equation}\label{correctedgetsbetter}
\left\|\xi_i-\xi_{\alpha_i}\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\to 0
\end{equation}

First, we claim that we may assume that:
\begin{equation}\label{pregluedisntbig}
\limsup_i\left\|\xi_{\alpha_i}\right\|_{k,2,\delta}
\end{equation}
is arbitrarily small by taking $(\alpha,y,\kappa)$ sufficiently close to zero.  We know $\|F_{\alpha,y}\kappa_\alpha\|_{k-1,2,\delta}\to 0$ as $(\alpha,y,\kappa)\to 0$ by \eqref{kernelclosetoholomorphic}.  Now since the Newton iteration is uniformly convergent and $Q_{\alpha,y}$ is uniformly bounded, it follows that $\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta}\to 0$ as $(\alpha,y,\kappa)\to 0$.  We may easily bound $\|\xi_{\alpha_i}\|_{k,2,\delta}$ in terms of $\|\xi\|_{k,2,\delta}$ and the desired claim follows.

Now since the $\limsup$ \eqref{pregluedisntbig} can be assumed to be arbitrarily small, we may, in particular, assume that for sufficiently large $i$, $\|\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i}\|_{k,2,\delta}\leq c'$ for the constant $c'>0$ from Proposition \ref{contractionprop} for which \eqref{domainofcontraction} is a domain of contraction.  By construction, the fact that $\xi\in\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,y}=\ker L_\alpha$ implies that $\xi_{\alpha_i}\in\ker L_{\alpha_i}=\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha_i,y_i}$.  Hence $\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i}$ lies in the domain of contraction \eqref{domainofcontraction} for $(\alpha_i,y_i,\kappa)$ where the Newton iteration applies.  By definition, we have $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_i)=0$ and $\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_i$ is in the same domain of contraction.  Since the Newton iteration is a $\frac 12$-contraction on this domain and $Q_{\alpha_i,y_i}$ is uniformly bounded, to show \eqref{correctedgetsbetter}, it suffices to show that:
\begin{equation}\label{pregluedcorrectionalmosthol}
\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta}\to 0
\end{equation}

To prove \eqref{pregluedcorrectionalmosthol}, first recall that $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)=0$.

Away from the ends/necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$, the expression $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$ differs from $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)=0$ only in terms of the complex structure $j_{y_i}$ in place of $j_y$.  Clearly this difference goes to zero as $y_i\to y$.

Over the ends of $C_{\alpha_i}$, the expression $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$ coincides with $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)=0$.

Over the necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$, we bound $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$ as follows.  Fix a neck $[0,6S_i]\subseteq C_{\alpha_i}$.  If $\alpha\ne 0$ for this neck, then the desired estimate follows easily from the definition of $\xi_{\alpha_i}$, $\kappa_{\alpha_i}$, and $u_{\alpha_i}$, and the fact that $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)=0$.  If $\alpha=0$ for this neck, then we argue as follows.  The expression $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$ is only nonzero over $[S_i-1,S_i+1]\cup[5S_i-1,5S_i+1]$; by symmetry we may just bound it over $[S_i-1,S_i+1]$.  As in the proof of Lemma \ref{mappregluingissmall}, we win because of the weights: the expression in question has derivatives bounded by $e^{-S}$ and is weighted by $e^{\delta S}$.

This proof is essentially lifted from McDuff--Salamon \cite[p99, Lemma 4.7.3]{mcduffsalamonJholsymp}.

Let $\lambda_\mathrm{std}:=\frac 12\sum_jx_j\,dy_j-y_j\,dx_j$.  Note the identity:
\begin{equation}\label{zdzbaridentity}
\sum_jz_j\,d\bar z_j=\frac 12\sum_jd\left|z_j\right|^2-2i\lambda_\mathrm{std}
\end{equation}
Let $\gamma:S^1\to\mathbb R^{2n}$ be a smooth loop.  Write the Fourier series $\gamma(t)=\sum_ka_ke^{ikt}\in\mathbb C^n$ where $a_k\in\mathbb C^n$.  Now using \eqref{zdzbaridentity}, we see that:
\begin{equation*}
\int_{S^1}\gamma^\ast\lambda_\mathrm{std}=\frac i2\int_{S^1}\sum_j\gamma^\ast(z_j\,d\bar z_j)=\frac i2\int_{S^1}\sum_{k,\ell}a_k\bar a_\ell(-i\ell)e^{i(k-\ell)t}\,dt=\pi\sum_kk\left|a_k\right|^2
\end{equation*}
We may also calculate:
\begin{equation}
\int_{S^1}\Bigl|\frac{d\gamma}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,dt=\int_{S^1}\sum_{k,\ell}a_k\bar a_\ell(ik)(-i\ell)e^{i(k-\ell)t}\,dt=2\pi\sum_kk^2\left|a_k\right|^2
\end{equation}
Hence we conclude that:
\begin{equation}\label{sympisoperimetric}
\left|\int_{S^1}\gamma^\ast\lambda_\mathrm{std}\right|\leq\frac 12\int_{S^1}\Bigl|\frac{d\gamma}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,dt
\end{equation}

Let:
\begin{equation}
E(r):=\int_{[-r,r]\times S^1}\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,ds\,dt\approx\int_{[-r,r]\times S^1}u^\ast\omega_\mathrm{std}=\int_{\partial[-r,r]\times S^1}u^\ast\lambda_\mathrm{std}
\end{equation}
($\approx$ means equality up to a factor which can be made arbitrarily close to $1$ by taking $\epsilon>0$ sufficiently small).  Applying \eqref{sympisoperimetric}, we conclude that:
\begin{equation}\label{boundonenergyfromboundary}
E(r)\lesssim\frac 12\int_{\partial[-r,r]\times S^1}\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,dt
\end{equation}
($\lesssim$ means inequality up to a factor which can be made arbitrarily close to $1$ by taking $\epsilon>0$ sufficiently small).  The right hand side above equals $\frac 12E'(r)$, so we have $E'(r)\gtrsim 2E(r)$, and hence $E'(r)\geq 2\mu E(r)$ (for $\epsilon>0$ sufficiently small), from which we conclude that $E(r)\leq e^{2\mu\cdot(r-R)}E(R)$.  Using \eqref{boundonenergyfromboundary} to bound $E(R)$, we see that:
\begin{equation}
E(r)\lesssim e^{2\mu\cdot(r-R)}\frac 12\int_{\partial[-R,R]\times S^1}\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,dt
\end{equation}
We have:
\begin{equation}
E(r)\approx\frac 12\int_{[-r,r]\times S^1}\biggl[\Bigl|\frac{du}{ds}\Bigr|^2+\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\biggr]\,ds\,dt
\end{equation}
Hence we conclude that:
\begin{equation}
\int_{[-r,r]\times S^1}\biggl[\Bigl|\frac{du}{ds}\Bigr|^2+\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\biggr]\,ds\,dt\lesssim e^{ 2\mu\cdot(r-R)}\int_{\partial[-R,R]\times S^1}\Bigl|\frac{du}{dt}\Bigr|^2\,dt
\end{equation}
which is the desired estimate.

This proof is essentially lifted from McDuff--Salamon \cite[p80, Lemma 4.3.1]{mcduffsalamonJholsymp}.

Let $w:=\frac 12u_x^2+\frac 12u_y^2$ (using the standard inner product $g_\mathrm{std}$), so $w:[0,1]^2\to\mathbb R_{\geq 0}$.  Now we calculate:
\begin{equation}\label{formulaforDeltaw}
w_{xx}+w_{yy}=\Bigl[u_{xx}^2+2u_{xy}^2+u_{yy}^2\Bigr]+\Bigl[u_x\cdot(u_{xxx}+u_{xyy})+u_y\cdot(u_{xxy}+u_{yyy})\Bigr]
\end{equation}
Now differentiating \eqref{formulaforDeltau} yields:
\begin{align*}
u_{xxx}+u_{xyy}=&\ddot J(u,u_x,u_y)\circ u_x+\dot J(u,u_{xy})\circ u_x+\dot J(u,u_y)\circ u_{xx}\\
&-\ddot J(u,u_x,u_x)\circ u_y-\dot J(u,u_{xx})\circ u_y-\dot J(u,u_x)\circ u_{xy}\\
u_{xxy}+u_{yyy}=&\ddot J(u,u_y,u_y)\circ u_x+\dot J(u,u_{yy})\circ u_x+\dot J(u,u_y)\circ u_{xy}\\
&-\ddot J(u,u_x,u_y)\circ u_y-\dot J(u,u_{xy})\circ u_y-\dot J(u,u_x)\circ u_{yy}
\end{align*}
Now we conclude that:
\begin{align*}
\bigl|u_x\cdot(u_{xxx}&+u_{xyy})+u_y\cdot(u_{xxy}+u_{yyy})\bigr|\\
&\leq 2\left\|J\right\|_{C^2}\cdot(\left|u_x\right|^3\left|u_y\right|+\left|u_x\right|\left|u_y\right|^3)\\
&\qquad+2\left\|J\right\|_{C^1}\cdot(\left|u_x\right|^2\left|u_{xy}\right|+\left|u_x\right|\left|u_y\right|\left|u_{xx}\right|+\left|u_y\right|^2\left|u_{xy}\right|+\left|u_x\right|\left|u_y\right|\left|u_{yy}\right|)\\
&\leq c\left\|J\right\|_{C^2}w^2+c\left\|J\right\|_{C^1}w\cdot(\left|u_{xx}\right|+\left|u_{xy}\right|+\left|u_{yy}\right|)\\
&\leq c\left\|J\right\|_{C^2}w^2+c\left\|J\right\|_{C^1}^2w^2+\frac 1{100}(u_{xx}^2+2u_{xy}^2+u_{yy}^2)
\end{align*}
Plugging this into \eqref{formulaforDeltaw}, we conclude that:
\begin{equation}
\Delta w=w_{xx}+w_{yy}\geq -c\cdot (\left\|J\right\|_{C^2}+\left\|J\right\|_{C^1}^2)w^2
\end{equation}
Now we may apply a mean value inequality McDuff--Salamon \cite[p81, Lemma 4.3.2]{mcduffsalamonJholsymp} or Wehrheim \cite[p306, Theorem 1.1]{wehrheimmeanvalue} to see that there exist $\epsilon>0$ and $c<\infty$ such that if $\int_{[0,1]^2}w\,dx\,dy<\epsilon$, then $w(\frac 12,\frac 12)\leq c\cdot\int_{[0,1]^2}w\,dx\,dy$.  Thus we are done.

For more details see McDuff--Salamon \cite[p533, \S B.4]{mcduffsalamonJholsymp}.

The $\bar\partial$-equation for $u$ may be written as:
\begin{equation}
u_y=J(u)\circ u_x
\end{equation}
Differentiating with respect to $x$ and to $y$, we conclude that:
\begin{align}
u_{xy}&=\dot J(u,u_x)\circ u_x+J(u)\circ u_{xx}\\
u_{xx}&=\dot J(u,u_y)\circ u_x+J(u)\circ u_{xy}
\end{align}
Combining these and using the fact that $\dot J$ and $J$ anticommute, we conclude that:
\begin{equation}\label{formulaforDeltau}
\Delta u=u_{xx}+u_{yy}=\dot J(u,u_y)\circ u_x-\dot J(u,u_x)\circ u_y
\end{equation}
Now a standard elliptic bootstrapping argument based on \eqref{formulaforDeltau} gives the desired result.  By hypothesis, we have an $L^\infty$ bound in terms of $\left\|J\right\|_{C^1}$ on the right hand side of \eqref{formulaforDeltau}, which gives a $W^{2,p}$ bound ($2<p<\infty$) on $u$ in terms of $\left\|J\right\|_{C^1}$.  Now we have a $W^{1,p}$ bound on the right hand side of \eqref{formulaforDeltau} in terms of $\left\|J\right\|_{C^2}$, which gives a $W^{3,p}$ bound on $u$ in terms of $\left\|J\right\|_{C^2}$.  Iterating, we get a $W^{k,p}$ bound on $u$ in terms of $\left\|J\right\|_{C^{k-1}}$, which is enough.  There is no need to worry about elliptic regularity estimates near the boundary since we can shrink the domain slightly after each iteration.

In simpler terms, we bound the operator norm of the difference between the two diagonal compositions:
\begin{equation}\label{betterPTcommutingoperatornormboundHF}
\left\|\mathsf{PT}\circ D_{0,0,0}-D_{0|\alpha,0,0}\circ\mathsf{PT}\right\|\leq c\left|\alpha\right|^\epsilon
\end{equation}
(this trivially implies the claimed statement).  To show \eqref{betterPTcommutingoperatornormboundHF}, observe that the two operators only differ over the $S^1\times[S-1,\infty)$ subset of each end.  Now it follows from Lemma \ref{linearizedformulaHF} that the contribution to the operator norm over $S^1\times[S-1,\infty)$ is bounded by a constant times the $C^k$-distance between $u_0$ and $u_{0|\alpha}$ over $S^1\times[0,\infty)$.  The estimates \eqref{uzerobubble}, \eqref{uzeromain} imply that this distance is bounded by a constant times $\left|\alpha\right|^\epsilon$ (to be precise, the bubble ends contribute $\left|\alpha\right|^{1/6-\rho}$ and each main end contributes $\left|\alpha\right|^{\delta_i/6-\rho}$ for any $\rho>0$).

We consider the following diagram, which is a copy of the top square in \eqref{biginversediagramHF} with one extra vector space and some extra maps (to be defined below).
\begin{equation}\label{topdiagrambigger}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep = tiny]
{}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})\ar[shift left=0.5ex]{dd}{\mathrm{break}'}\ar[rounded corners,to path={(\tikztostart) -- ([xshift=2ex]\tikztostart.east) -- ([xshift=2ex]\tikztotarget.east) \tikztonodes -- (\tikztotarget)}]{dddd}{\mathrm{break}}\\
W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E\ar{ru}{D_{\alpha,w,y}}\\
{}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})\ar[shift right=-0.5ex]{uu}{\mathrm{glue}}\ar[shift left=0.5ex]{dd}{(\operatorname{id}^{1,0})^{-1}}\\
W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM)_{D,H}\oplus E\ar{uu}{\mathrm{glue}}\ar{rd}{D_{0|\alpha,0,y}}\ar{ru}{D_{\alpha,w,y}}\\
{}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_y(\cdot)))})\ar[shift right=-0.5ex]{uu}{\operatorname{id}^{1,0}}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Since the almost complex structure $J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))$ is a function of a point in $C_\alpha$, we should remark immediately on what we mean by $W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})$.  Away from the ends, the curve $C_0$ is identified canonically with $C_\alpha$, and this identification extends holomorphically to a (non-injective) map from $C_0$ to $C_\alpha$ defined outside the $S^1\times[4S,\infty)$ subsets of the ends.  Thus there is a well-defined such $W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}$ space of sections on $C_0$ defined outside the $S^1\times[4S,\infty)$ subsets of the ends, valued in $\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))}$.  Now, for instance, the middle horizontal map $D_{\alpha,w,y}$ can be seen as giving a section in this $W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}$ space defined outside $S^1\times[4S,\infty)$.  In the proof below, it is convenient to use this $W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}$ space of sections defined outside $S^1\times[4S,\infty)$, though we must be careful that the expressions we write are well-defined.

Let us define the rest of the maps in \eqref{topdiagrambigger}.  The vertical map $\operatorname{break}$ has been factored as $(\operatorname{id}^{1,0})^{-1}\circ\operatorname{break}'$ in the obvious way.  Finally, let us define the map:
\begin{equation}
W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})\xrightarrow{\mathrm{glue}}W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})
\end{equation}
Let $\eta\in W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_y}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,w}(A_y(\cdot)))})$.  Away from the necks of $C_\alpha$, $\operatorname{glue}(\eta)$ is simply $\eta$.  In any particular bubble neck $S^1\times[0,6S]\subseteq C_\alpha$, we define:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{glue}(\eta)(t,s):=\begin{cases}
\eta(t,s)&s\leq 2S\cr
\chi(4S-s)\eta(t,s)+\chi(4S-s')\eta(t',s')&2S\leq s\leq 4S\cr
\eta(t',s')&4S\leq s
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
(this definition also applies over the main necks with the obvious adjustment).  Note that $\operatorname{glue}\circ\operatorname{break}'$ is the identity map.

Now suppose that $D_{0|\alpha,0,y}\xi=\operatorname{break}(\eta)$; we must show that:
\begin{equation}
\left\|D_{\alpha,w,y}(\operatorname{glue}(\xi))-\eta\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}\leq c\cdot\bigl(\left|\alpha\right|^\epsilon+\left|w\right|\bigr)\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}
\end{equation}
Using the triangle inequality and the fact that $\eta=\operatorname{glue}(\operatorname{break}'(\eta))=\operatorname{glue}(\operatorname{id}^{1,0}(D_{0|\alpha,0,y}\xi))$, we conclude that $\left\|D_{\alpha,w,y}(\operatorname{glue}(\xi))-\eta\right\|$ is bounded above by:
\begin{equation*}
\left\|D_{\alpha,w,y}(\operatorname{glue}(\xi))-\operatorname{glue}(D_{\alpha,w,y}(\xi))\right\|+\left\|\operatorname{glue}\left[D_{\alpha,w,y}(\xi)-\operatorname{id}^{1,0}(D_{0|\alpha,0,y}(\xi))\right]\right\|
\end{equation*}
We now estimate each term separately.

To estimate $\left\|D_{\alpha,w,y}(\operatorname{glue}(\xi))-\operatorname{glue}(D_{\alpha,w,y}(\xi))\right\|$, argue as follows.  The difference is only nonzero over the regions $S^1\times([2S,2S+1]\cup[4S-1,4S])$ of each neck.  Now the norm is bounded by $\left\|\xi\right\|e^{-2S\delta}$ (calculation left to the reader), where the factor of $e^{-2S\delta}$ comes as the ratio of the weight given to $S^1\times[4S-1,4S]$ inside a neck vs inside an end; this gives the desired bound since $\delta>0$.

To estimate $\left\|\operatorname{glue}\left[D_{\alpha,w,y}(\xi)-\operatorname{id}^{1,0}(D_{0|\alpha,0,y}\xi)\right]\right\|$, argue as follows.  This is bounded by a constant times the $(k-1,2,\delta,\delta)$-norm of $D_{\alpha,w,y}(\xi)-\operatorname{id}^{1,0}(D_{0|\alpha,0,y}\xi)$ over the complement of the subsets $S^1\times[4S,\infty)$ of the ends.  Now this is bounded by $(\left|\alpha\right|^\epsilon+\left|w\right|)\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}$ using the reasoning from Lemma \ref{approxmiddleHF}.

The proof is identical to the proof of Lemma \ref{quadestimate}, with the evident notational differences, $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}$ for $\mathcal F_{\alpha,y}$, and Lemma \ref{linearizedformulaHF} for Lemma \ref{linearizedformula}.  As with Lemma \ref{quadestimate}, we actually prove the stronger statement that:
\begin{equation}\label{derivislipHF}
\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}'(0,\xi)-\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}'(\zeta,\xi)\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}\leq c\cdot\left\|\zeta\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}
\end{equation}

The proof is essentially identical to the proof of Proposition \ref{newtoniteration}; we write it out anyway.

In fact, we will show that $\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}$ is given explicitly as the limit of the Newton iteration:
\begin{align}
\xi_0&:=\kappa_\alpha\\
\xi_n&:=\xi_{n-1}-Q_{\alpha,w,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi_{n-1}
\end{align}
By Lemma \ref{contractionpropHF}, the map $\xi\mapsto\xi-Q_{\alpha,w,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi$ is a $\frac 12$-contraction mapping when restricted to:
\begin{equation}\label{domainofcontractionHF}
\{\xi\in\kappa_\alpha+\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}:\left\|\xi\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}\leq c'\}
\end{equation}
To finish the proof, it suffices to show that (for sufficiently small $(\alpha,w,y,\kappa)$) \eqref{domainofcontractionHF} is nonempty and is mapped to itself by $\xi\mapsto\xi-Q_{\alpha,w,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi$.

We know that $\left\|\kappa_\alpha\right\|_{k,2,\delta,\delta}\to 0$ as $\kappa\to 0$ (uniformly in $(\alpha,w,y)$), so \eqref{domainofcontractionHF} is nonempty.  By using Proposition \ref{quadestimateHF} with $(\xi_1,\xi_2)=(0,\kappa_\alpha)$ and Lemmas \ref{mappregluingissmallHF} and \ref{pregluingKestimateHF}, we conclude that:
\begin{equation}\label{kernelclosetoholomorphicHF}
\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\kappa_\alpha\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}\to 0
\end{equation}
as $(\alpha,w,y,\kappa)\to 0$.  Since the operator norm of $Q_{\alpha,w,y}$ is bounded uniformly as $(\alpha,w,y)\to 0$, we see that $\kappa_\alpha$ is almost fixed by $\xi\mapsto\xi-Q_{\alpha,w,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi$ as $(\alpha,w,y,\kappa)\to 0$.  It then follows from the contraction property that $\xi\mapsto\xi-Q_{\alpha,w,y}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\xi$ maps \eqref{domainofcontractionHF} to itself.

The proof follows the same basic outline as the proof of Proposition \ref{continuityofgluing}; some parts of the proof are identical, and we will omit these.  The key ingredient is our precise control of the image of the right inverse $Q_{\alpha,w,y}$ (specifically, that $\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}=\ker L_\alpha$).

Suppose $(\alpha_i,w_i,y_i,\kappa_i)\to(\alpha,w,y,\kappa)$ is a convergent net.\footnote{We could restrict to sequences rather than nets since $\mathbb C^d\times(\mathbb R_{\geq 0}^{k-1})^{\leq\mathfrak s}\times\mathbb R^\ast\times\prod_{i=1}^{k\prime}(H^i)^{\leq\mathfrak s}\times K$ is first countable.  However, this would not make the argument any simpler.}  We will show that:
\begin{equation}
(u_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i,\kappa_i},e_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i,\kappa_i})\to(u_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa},e_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa})
\end{equation}
First, observe that the argument from the proof of Proposition \ref{continuityofgluing} applies verbatim to give that in fact it suffices to show that:
\begin{equation}
(u_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i,\kappa},e_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i,\kappa})\to(u_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa},e_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa})
\end{equation}
Now recall that by definition:
\begin{align}
u_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa}&=\exp_{u_\alpha}\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}&\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}&=\kappa_\alpha+\xi&\text{for some }\xi&\in\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}
\end{align}
Now we define $\xi_{\alpha_i}\in W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_{\alpha_i},u_{\alpha_i}^\ast TX)_D\oplus E$ by ``pregluing'' $\xi$ from $C_\alpha$ to $C_{\alpha_i}$ as follows.  Note that we may assume without loss of generality that at the nodes where $\alpha\ne 0$, we also have $\alpha_i\ne 0$.  Away from the ends/necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$, we set $\xi_{\alpha_i}=\xi$.  Note that for every end of $C_{\alpha_i}$, there is a corresponding end of $C_\alpha$, so we may also simply set $\xi_{\alpha_i}=\xi$ over the ends of $C_{\alpha_i}$.  Over the necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$ for which $\alpha=0$, we define $\xi_{\alpha_i}$ via \eqref{kernelpregluingformulaHFbubble}--\eqref{kernelpregluingformulaHFmain} (note that this is reasonable since $\xi$ satisfies the estimates \eqref{kappabubble}, \eqref{kappamain} as a consequence of Proposition \ref{neckestimateHF}).  Over the necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$ for which $\alpha\ne 0$, we define $\xi_{\alpha_i}$ as:
\begin{equation}
\xi_{\alpha_i}(s,t):=\mathsf{PT}_{u_{\alpha}(t,f_i(s))\to u_{\alpha_i}(t,s)}[\xi(t,f_i(s)))]
\end{equation}
where $f_i:[0,6S_i]\to[0,6S]$ is smooth and satisfies:
\begin{align}
f_i(s):=&\begin{cases}
s&s\leq S-2\cr
s-3S_i+3S&3S_i-2S+2\leq s\leq 3S_i+2S-2\cr
s-6S_i+6S&6S_i-S+2\leq s\cr
\end{cases}\\
f_i([S-2,3S_i-2S+2])&\subseteq[S-2,S+2]\\
f_i([3S_i+2S-2,6S_i-S+2])&\subseteq[5S-2,5S+2]
\end{align}
so that $f_i\to\operatorname{id}$ uniformly in all derivatives as $S_i\to S$.  More informally, $f_i$ is smooth and matches up $[0,S-2],[S+2,5S-2],[5S+2,6S]\subseteq[0,6S]$ with corresponding intervals of the same length inside $[0,6S_i]$, symmetrically.

Now at this point we appeal to the corresponding arguments from the proof of Proposition \ref{continuityofgluing}, which apply as written (using the fact that $\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}=\ker L_\alpha$) and imply that it suffices to show that:
\begin{equation}\label{pregluedcorrectionalmostholHF}
\left\|\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})\right\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}\to 0
\end{equation}
Recall that $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)=0$; we will use this to estimate $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$.

Away from the ends/necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$, it is clear by definition that $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})\to\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)$ uniformly in all derivatives.  Thus the contribution to the norm of $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$ over this region approaches zero.

Over the necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$ which correspond to necks of $C_\alpha$, we again have uniform convergence in all derivatives, so the contribution of these regions approaches zero as well.

We estimate the contribution over the ends of $C_{\alpha_i}$ (recall that these necessarily correspond to ends of $C_\alpha$).  Over main ends, we have $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})=\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)$.  Now the convergence $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})\to\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)$ uniformly in all derivatives is valid near the bubble nodes in the usual metric on $C_{\alpha_i}=C_\alpha$.  It follows that we also have convergence in the relevant $\delta$-weighted Sobolev norm since $\delta<1$.  Thus the contribution of this region to the norm approaches zero.

Finally, let us estimate the contribution to the norm over the necks of $C_{\alpha_i}$ which correspond to ends of $C_\alpha$.  We treat main necks and bubble necks separately.  Over main necks, $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$ is supported inside $S^1\times([S-1,S+1],[5S-1,5S+1])$.  The contribution of this region to its norm goes to zero, as follows using the exponential decrease on $\kappa$ and $\xi$ from Proposition \ref{neckestimateHF} and the fact that $\delta<\delta_i$.  Over bubble ends, $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$ converges to $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}(\kappa_\alpha+\xi)$ uniformly in all derivatives over the complement of $S^1\times[S-1,5S+1]$ (measured with respect to the usual metric on $C_0$), and hence the contribution of this region to the weighted norm goes to zero since $\delta<1$.  Over $S^1\times([S-1,S+1]\cup[5S-1,5S+1])$, the smoothness of $\kappa$ and $\xi$ over $C_\alpha$ and the fact that $\delta<1$ shows that the contribution of this region to the norm goes to zero (since $S\to\infty$).  Finally, over $S^1\times[S+1,5S-1]$, both $u_{\alpha_i}$ and $\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i}$ are constant, so $\mathcal F_{\alpha_i,w_i,y_i}(\kappa_{\alpha_i}+\xi_{\alpha_i})$ is simply:
\begin{equation*}
\mathsf{PT}_{\exp_{u_0(n)}(\kappa(n)+\xi(n))\to u_0(n)}^{\ell_{\alpha_i,w_i}(A_{y_i}(\cdot))}\Bigl(2d(\operatorname{proj}_{S^1}A_{y_i})\otimes X_{H((\ell_{\alpha_i,w_i}\times\operatorname{id}_{S^1})(A_{y_i}(\cdot)))}\Bigr)^{0,1}_{j,J(\ell_{\alpha_i,w_i}(A_{y_i}(\cdot)))}
\end{equation*}
The norm of this expression over $S^1\times[S+1,5S-1]$ approaches zero since $A_{y_i}=O(e^{-s})$ in all derivatives and $\delta<1$.

By assumption, we have:
\begin{align}
u_x+J(u)u_y&=0\\
(u+\xi)_x+J(u+\xi)(u+\xi)_y&=0
\end{align}
which together imply that:
\begin{equation}
\xi_x+J(u+\xi)\xi_y=[J(u)-J(u+\xi)]u_y
\end{equation}
which we prefer to write as:
\begin{equation}\label{ellipticforxi}
(\partial_x+B\partial_y)\xi=A(\xi)
\end{equation}
where $B:[0,1]^2\to\operatorname{End}(\mathbb R^{2n})$ denotes $J(u+\xi)$ and $A:[0,1]^2\times\mathbb R^{2n}\to\mathbb R^{2n}$ denotes $A(\xi)=[J(u)-J(u+\xi)]u_y$.  By definition, we have $A(0)=0$ and:
\begin{equation}\label{boundonA}
\left|D^kA\right|\leq c_k
\end{equation}
for some $c_k<\infty$ depending only on upper bounds on $\left\|J\right\|_{C^\ell}$ and $\left\|u\right\|_{C^\ell}$ for some $\ell=\ell(k)<\infty$.  By definition, we have $B^2=-1$; let us now argue that we also have:
\begin{equation}\label{boundonB}
\left|D^kB\right|\leq c_k
\end{equation}
for sufficiently small $\epsilon>0$ and $c_k<\infty$ as before.  Indeed, for sufficiently small $\epsilon>0$, we may apply the Gromov--Schwarz lemma to $u+\xi$ and conclude that $|D\xi|\leq c<\infty$ depending only on upper bounds on $\left\|J\right\|_{C^\ell}$ and $\left\|u\right\|_{C^\ell}$ for some $\ell<\infty$.  Now Lemma \ref{Woneinftycontrolsall} applied to $u+\xi$ gives $|D^k\xi|\leq c_k<\infty$, which implies the desired bound on the derivatives of $B$.

Now we apply $(\partial_x-B\partial_y)$ to both sides of \eqref{ellipticforxi} to obtain:
\begin{equation}
\Delta\xi=(\partial_x-B\partial_y)A(\xi)-((\partial_x-B\partial_y)B)(\xi_y)
\end{equation}
For any smooth function $\phi:[0,1]^2\to\mathbb R_{\geq 0}$ supported in the interior of $[0,1]^2$, we thus have:
\begin{equation*}
\Delta(\phi\xi)=\xi\Delta\phi+2\phi_x\xi_x+2\phi_y\xi_y+\phi\cdot(\partial_x-B\partial_y)A(\xi)-\phi\cdot((\partial_x-B\partial_y)B)(\xi_y)
\end{equation*}
Now the desired result follows from the usual bootstrapping of the elliptic regularity estimates for the Laplacian on $\mathbb R^2$, namely $\left\|f\right\|_{H^{s+2}}\leq c_s\left\|f+\Delta f\right\|_{H^s}$ (the first step being the case $s=-1$).  We may shrink the support of $\phi$ slightly at each step, so there is no need to worry about regularity near the boundary.

To identify the orientation sheaf of $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)$ with $\mathfrak o_\sigma\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}$, it suffices to identify the orientation sheaf of every $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_\sigma\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}$ in a compatible way (for all finite subsets $I\subseteq\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}$).  Moreover, it suffices to make this identification over the open subset $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\mathrm{reg}\circ}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ where the domain curve is smooth (it then automatically extends uniquely to all of $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^\mathrm{reg}$, by virtue of the local topological description of $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ given by the gluing map); we may also check compatibility with the inclusions $I\subseteq J$ over $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\mathrm{reg}\circ}$.  Now the kernel $\tilde K=\ker D$ of the linearization \eqref{linearizedstd} forms a vector bundle over $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\mathrm{reg}\circ}$, and the orientation sheaf of $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\mathrm{reg}\circ}$ is isomorphic to $\mathfrak o_\sigma\otimes\mathfrak o_{\tilde K}$ (to see this, one must distinguish the two cases $\dim\sigma>0$ and $\dim\sigma=0$).  Now we have $\mathfrak o_{\tilde K}=\mathfrak o_D=\mathfrak o_{D_\text{hol}}=\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}$; this defines a fiberwise isomorphism of $\mathfrak o_{\tilde K}$ with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}$, and since the operators $D$ and $D_\text{hol}$ vary nicely over the base $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\mathrm{reg}\circ}$, it is easy to see that this is in fact an isomorphism of sheaves.  Thus we have the desired identification.

Now let us show that the boundary map induced by \eqref{hforface} coincides with the tautological map \eqref{hforfaceor} (this just amounts to chasing definitions).  Let $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_{\overline{\mathcal M}}(\sigma,p,q)$ denote the stratum $(\sigma|[0\ldots\hat k\ldots n],p,q)$ (i.e.\ the stratum consisting of trajectories over $\sigma$ which factor through $\sigma|[0\ldots\hat k\ldots n]$).  Now on the space $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[0\ldots\hat k\ldots n],p,q)=\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}$, we have three implicit atlases:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal A(\sigma|[0\ldots\hat k\ldots n],p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}\subseteq\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s}\supseteq\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}
\end{equation}
(note that the two occurences of $\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}$ refer to the top elements of two different strata posets); in fact (the index set of) the middle atlas is the disjoint union of (the index sets of) the atlases on the right and on the left.  The reasoning used above to identify the orientation sheaf of $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[0\ldots\hat k\ldots n],p,q)=\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}$ under the leftmost implicit atlas applies equally well for all three atlases (and, in particular, the resulting identifications coincide).  Hence, for the purpose of identifying the boundary map on orientation sheaves, it suffices to consider the single atlas $\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}$ on $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)$ and the inclusion of the boundary stratum $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)$.  Now it suffices to check that the desired compatibility holds for the inclusion $(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^{\mathrm{reg}\circ}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)_I^{\mathrm{reg}\circ}$ (for all finite subsets $I\subseteq\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}$), and this follows by definition.

Now let us show that the boundary map induced by \eqref{hforproduct} coincides with the tautological map \eqref{hforproductor}.  First, we chase definitions as above.  Let $\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_{\overline{\mathcal M}}(\sigma,p,r)$ denote the stratum $(\sigma|[0\ldots k],p,q)$--$(\sigma|[k\ldots n],q,r)$ (i.e.\ the stratum consisting of Floer trajectories over $\sigma$ which are broken at vertex $k$ and periodic orbit $q\in\mathcal P_{H(k)}$).  Now on the space $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[0\ldots k],p,q)\times\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[k\ldots n],q,r)=\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)^{\leq\mathfrak s}$, we have three implicit atlases:
\begin{equation*}
\mathcal A(\sigma|[0\ldots k],p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}\sqcup\mathcal A(\sigma|[k\ldots n],q,r)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}\subseteq\mathcal A(\sigma,p,r)^{\geq\mathfrak s}\supseteq\mathcal A(\sigma,p,r)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}
\end{equation*}
and in fact (the index set of) the middle atlas is the disjoint union of (the index sets of) the atlases on the right and on the left.  The reasoning used above to identify the orientation sheaves of $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[0\ldots k],p,q)$ and $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[k\ldots n],q,r)$ applies equally well to identify the orientation sheaf of their product $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[0\ldots k],p,q)\times\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma|[k\ldots n],q,r)=\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)^{\leq\mathfrak s}$ under all three atlases above (the fact that the thickening datums in the latter two atlases do not treat the portions of the trajectories from $p$ to $q$ and from $q$ to $r$ ``independently'' does not cause any problems).  Hence for the purposes of identifying the boundary map on orientation sheaves, it suffices to consider the single atlas $\mathcal A(\sigma,p,r)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}$ on $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)$ and the inclusion of the boundary stratum $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)^{\leq\mathfrak s}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)$.  As before, it suffices to check that the desired compatibility holds for the inclusion $(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ (for all finite subsets $I\subseteq\mathcal A(\sigma,p,r)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}$), and moreover, this may be checked on the locus where the domain curve is smooth except for the required node over $k$ asymptotic to $q\in\mathcal P_{H(k)}$.

It will be convenient to assume that $0<k<n$, so let us first deduce the case of general $0\leq k\leq n$ from the case $0<k<n$.  Fix $\sigma$ and $0\leq k\leq n=\dim\sigma$.  We consider the degenerate simplex $\sigma'\to\sigma$ given by ``doubling vertex $0$'' if $k=0$ and ``doubling vertex $n$'' if $k=n$ (i.e.\ if $0=k<n$, we consider $\Delta^{n+1}\to\Delta^n$ given by superimposing the first two vertices of $\Delta^{n+1}$, if $0<k=n$, we consider $\Delta^{n+1}\to\Delta^n$ given by superimposing the last two vertices of $\Delta^{n+1}$, and if $0=k=n$ we consider $\Delta^2\to\Delta^0$); let $k'=1$ if $k=0$ and let $k'=n'-1$ if $k=n$, where $n'=\dim\sigma'$, and note that $0<k'<n'$.  Now there is a natural map $\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}\to\mathcal A(\sigma',p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}$ given by pulling back along $\sigma'\to\sigma$, and $\sigma'\to\sigma$ maps flow lines to flow lines (for the flow from Definition \ref{simplexflows}).  Hence, given any point $x\in\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ with a single node over $k$ asymptotic to $q\in\mathcal P_{H(k)}$, we may lift it (canonically, up to translating the part(s) of the trajectory over $0=k$ and/or over $k=n$) to a point $x'\in\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma',p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ with a single node over $k'$ asymptotic to $q\in\mathcal P_{H'(k')}=\mathcal P_{H(k)}$.  Moreover, there is a germ of homeomorphism $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma',p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}=\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}\times\mathbb R^{\mathbf 1(k=0)+\mathbf 1(k=n)}$ between neighborhoods of $x'$ and $x\times 0$.  Now the desired compatibility of orientations for $(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^{\leq\mathfrak s})^\mathrm{reg}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ follows from the compatibility for $(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma',p,r)_I^{\leq\mathfrak s'})^\mathrm{reg}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma',p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ (which has from $0<k'<n'$).  Hence we may assume without loss of generality that $0<k<n$.

Now we have come to the heart of the matter, where we will need to analyze the gluing map.  To review: we have identified the orientation sheaf of $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_\sigma\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,r}$, and we have identified the orientation sheaf of $(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}$  with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{\sigma|[0\ldots k]}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}\otimes\mathfrak o_{\sigma|[k\ldots n]}\otimes\mathfrak o_{q,r}$ (recall that $\mathfrak s$ denotes the stratum consisting of trajectories broken at vertex $k$ and periodic orbit $q\in\mathcal P_{H(k)}$).  We must show that the boundary map on orientation sheaves induced by the inclusion $(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}\hookrightarrow\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ is the expected map \eqref{hforproductor}, and it suffices to check this over the locus where the domain curve is smooth except for the required node over $k$ asymptotic to $q\in\mathcal P_{H(k)}$.  We may further assume that $0<k<n$.

We consider the gluing setup for $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}$ at a point in $(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}$ where $C_0$ is smooth except for the required node over vertex $k$ asymptotic to $q\in\mathcal P_{H(k)}$.  In other words, $C_0\setminus\{q_0,q_1,q_2\}=S^1\times\mathbb R\sqcup S^1\times\mathbb R$, and $u_0:S^1\times\mathbb R\sqcup S^1\times\mathbb R\to M$ is a trajectory from $p$ to $q$ and from $q$ to $r$.  From \S\ref{choosingDH}, we take $L=L'=0$ (i.e.\ no points $p_i$ or $p_i'$), and we may take $D=H=\varnothing$.  From \S\ref{gluingandvaryingjAl}, there is one gluing parameter $\alpha\in\mathbb R_{\geq 0}$, there is no nontrivial variation in $(j_0,A_0)$ (i.e.\ $y\in\mathbb R^\ast=\mathbb R^0$), and there is some variation in $\ell_0$ parameterized by $w\in\prod_{i=1}^{2\prime}H^i$.  As usual, we denote by $K\subseteq W^{k,2,\delta}(C_0,u_0^\ast TM)$ the kernel of the linearized operator (we shall omit the subscript $D,H$ since $L=L'=0$).  Now the gluing construction gives rise to a gluing map:
\begin{align}
K\times\mathbb R_{\geq 0}\times\prod_{i=1}^{2\prime}H^i&\to\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)_I^\mathrm{reg}\\
(\kappa,\alpha,w)&\mapsto(u_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa},e_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa})
\end{align}
($y=0$).  Now if we restrict to $\alpha=0$, this map realizes the identification of the orientation sheaf of $(\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,r)^{\leq\mathfrak s}_I)^\mathrm{reg}$ with $\mathfrak o_K\otimes\mathfrak o_{\sigma|[0\ldots k]}\otimes\mathfrak o_{\sigma|[k\ldots n]}$.  Thus it suffices to show that for fixed (sufficiently small) $\alpha>0$ and fixed $w=0$, the gluing map is differentiable in the $K$ direction, and that its derivative (a map from $K$ to the kernel of the linearized operator at the glued map $u_{\alpha,w,y,\kappa}$) agrees (on orientation lines) with the Floer--Hofer map \eqref{floerhoferoogluing} (recall that $\mathfrak o_K$ is identified with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}\otimes\mathfrak o_{q,r}$ and that the orientation line of the kernel at the glued map is identified with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,r}$).

For fixed $(\alpha,w,y)$, the gluing map is given by:
\begin{equation}\label{restrictedgluingmap}
K\xrightarrow{\kappa\mapsto\kappa_\alpha}K_\alpha\xrightarrow{\kappa_\alpha\mapsto\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}^{-1}(0)
\end{equation}
The second map is defined \emph{a priori} by a Newton iteration, however a more natural description \emph{a posteriori} is that $\{\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}\}=\{\kappa_\alpha+\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}\}\pitchfork\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}^{-1}(0)$ is the unique (necessarily transverse) intersection in a $(k,2,\delta,\delta)$-neighborhood of zero of fixed size.  Note that in this neighborhood of fixed size, $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}^{-1}(0)$ is a (highly differentiable) submanifold, since $\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}$ is highly differentiable and $D_{\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}$ is surjective as observed in the proof of Lemma \ref{mapsstufftoregHF}.  From this description of the second map, it is clearly differentiable.  Thus the derivative of the restricted gluing map \eqref{restrictedgluingmap} at a given $\kappa$ is given by:
\begin{equation}
T_\kappa K\xrightarrow{\dot\kappa\mapsto\dot\kappa_\alpha}T_{\kappa_\alpha}K_\alpha\xrightarrow{\operatorname{proj}_{Q_{\alpha,w,y}}}T_{\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}^{-1}(0)=\ker D_{\kappa_{\alpha,w,y}}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}
\end{equation}
The second map is ``projection with respect to $Q_{\alpha,w,y}$'', i.e.\ the map induced by identifying both the domain and codomain with $W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)/\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,w,y}$.  It suffices to study the derivative at $\kappa=0$, namely the first line of the following commuting diagram:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = small]
T_0K\ar{rr}{\dot\kappa\mapsto\dot\kappa_\alpha}&&T_0K_\alpha\ar{rr}{\operatorname{proj}_{Q_{\alpha,w,y}}}\ar{dr}[swap]{\operatorname{proj}_{Q_{\alpha,w,y}}}&&\ker D_{(0)_{\alpha,w,y}}\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}\ar{dl}{\operatorname{proj}_{Q_{\alpha,w,y}}}\\
&&&\ker D_{\alpha,w,y}
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
The rightmost diagonal map is orientation preserving (i.e.\ it commutes with the identification of the orientation lines of the domain and codomain with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,r}$), because $\operatorname{proj}_{Q_{\alpha,w,y}}$ gives a local trivialization of the bundle $\ker D\mathcal F_{\alpha,w,y}$ with respect to which the orientation is constant by definition.  Now the leftmost diagonal map is just $\dot\kappa_\alpha\mapsto\dot\kappa_\alpha-Q_{\alpha,w,y}D_{\alpha,w,y}\dot\kappa_\alpha$.  Hence it suffices to show that the following composition (note that we have changed notation from $\dot\kappa$ back to $\kappa$):
\begin{equation}\label{concretestufftocheckorientations}
\ker D_{0,0,0}=K\xrightarrow{\kappa\mapsto\kappa_\alpha}K_\alpha\xrightarrow{\kappa_\alpha\mapsto\kappa_\alpha-Q_{\alpha,w,y}D_{\alpha,w,y}\kappa_\alpha}\ker D_{\alpha,w,y}
\end{equation}
acts as \eqref{floerhoferoogluing} on orientations (with respect to the previously defined isomorphisms $\mathfrak o_K=\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}\otimes\mathfrak o_{q,r}$ and $\mathfrak o_{\ker D_{\alpha,w,y}}=\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,r}$).

We analyze \eqref{concretestufftocheckorientations} as follows.  For convenience, we may as well assume that $w=0$, and recall that necessarily $y=0$.  Now let us consider the following diagram:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}\label{orientationsinversediagram}
K_{\alpha,0,0}\ar[hook,yshift=-0.5ex]{r}\ar[leftarrow,yshift=0.5ex]{r}{\operatorname{proj}_{T_{\alpha,0,0}}}&W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)\oplus E_I\ar{r}{D_{\alpha,0,0}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})\ar{d}{\mathrm{break}}\\
K_{0|\alpha,0,0}\ar[hook,yshift=-0.5ex]{r}\ar[leftarrow,yshift=0.5ex]{r}{\operatorname{proj}_{T_{0|\alpha,0,0}}}&W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM)\oplus E_I\ar{u}{\mathrm{glue}}\ar{r}{D_{0|\alpha,0,0}}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})\ar[leftrightarrow]{d}{\mathsf{PT}}\\
K_{0,0,0}\ar[hook,yshift=0.5ex]{r}\ar[leftarrow,yshift=-0.5ex]{r}[swap]{\operatorname{proj}_{Q_{0,0,0}}}\ar{ru}{\kappa\mapsto\kappa_{0|\alpha}}&W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_0^\ast TM)\oplus E_I\ar[leftrightarrow]{u}{\mathsf{PT}}\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{D_{0,0,0}}&\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{Q_{0,0,0}}W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_0^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
The right half is just a copy of \eqref{biginversediagramHF} (with the middle square collapsed since $y=0$).  The left half consists of the inclusions of the kernels of the operators on the right half, as well as the projection maps associated to the images of the (approximate) right inverses $Q_{0,0,0}$, $T_{0|\alpha,0,0}:=\mathsf{PT}\circ Q_{0,0,0}\circ\mathsf{PT}$, and $T_{\alpha,0,0}=\mathrm{glue}\circ\mathsf{PT}\circ Q_{0,0,0}\circ\mathsf{PT}\circ\mathrm{break}$ (recall that $\operatorname{im} T_{\alpha,0,0}=\operatorname{im} Q_{\alpha,0,0}$).  The diagonal map $\kappa\mapsto\kappa_{0|\alpha}$ is defined by cutting off as in Definition \ref{kernelpregluingHF}; thus $\kappa_\alpha=\mathrm{glue}(\kappa_{0|\alpha})$.

Now the map \eqref{concretestufftocheckorientations} which we would like to analyze may be written as the composition $\kappa\mapsto\operatorname{proj}_{T_{\alpha,0,0}}(\mathrm{glue}(\kappa_{0|\alpha}))$ from \eqref{orientationsinversediagram}.  Now, we know that $\|D_{0|\alpha,0,0}\kappa_{0|\alpha}\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}=\|D_{\alpha,0,0}\kappa_\alpha\|_{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}$, which is small by Lemma \ref{pregluingKestimateHF}.  It follows that the map we would like to analyze is well-approximated (as $\alpha\to 0$) by the map $\kappa\mapsto\operatorname{proj}_{T_{\alpha,0,0}}(\mathrm{glue}(\operatorname{proj}_{T_{0|\alpha,0,0}}\kappa_{0|\alpha}))$.  In particular, since we are only interested in its action on orientations, it suffices to consider the latter map $\kappa\mapsto\operatorname{proj}_{T_{\alpha,0,0}}(\mathrm{glue}(\operatorname{proj}_{T_{0|\alpha,0,0}}\kappa_{0|\alpha}))$.

Now we claim that the map $K_{0,0,0}\to K_{0|\alpha,0,0}$ given by $\kappa\mapsto\operatorname{proj}_{T_{0|\alpha,0,0}}\kappa_{0|\alpha}$ preserves orientation (the orientation lines of the domain and codomain are both identified with $\mathfrak o_{E_I}\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}\otimes\mathfrak o_{q,r}$).  To see this, simply observe the map (as well as its domain and codomain and their orientations) vary continuously over $\alpha\in[0,\epsilon)$, and that the statement is true for $\alpha=0$ because then the map is the identity map.  Hence we have reduced the problem to showing that the map:
\begin{equation}\label{reducedmaporientations}
W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM)\supseteq K_{0|\alpha,0,0}\xrightarrow{\mathrm{glue}}W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)\oplus E_I\xrightarrow{\operatorname{proj}_{T_{\alpha,0,0}}}K_{\alpha,0,0}
\end{equation}
acts by \eqref{floerhoferoogluing}.

To analyze the map ${\operatorname{proj}_{T_{\alpha,0,0}}}\circ{\mathrm{glue}}:K_{0|\alpha,0,0}\to K_{\alpha,0,0}$, argue as follows.  For any finite-dimensional vector space $F$ and a map $\Lambda:F\to W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_0^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})$ supported away from the ends, we may consider the following modified version of \eqref{orientationsinversediagram}:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}\label{orientationsinversediagramcompl}
K_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}\ar[hook,yshift=-0.5ex]{r}\ar[leftarrow,yshift=0.5ex]{r}{\operatorname{proj}_{T_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}}}&F\oplus W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_\alpha,u_\alpha^\ast TM)\oplus E_I\ar{r}{\Lambda\oplus D_{\alpha,0,0}^t}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_\alpha,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_\alpha,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_\alpha^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{\alpha,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})\ar{d}{\mathrm{break}}\\
K_{0|\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}\ar[hook,yshift=-0.5ex]{r}\ar[leftarrow,yshift=0.5ex]{r}{\operatorname{proj}_{T_{0|\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}}}&F\oplus W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM)\oplus E_I\ar{u}{\operatorname{id}\oplus\mathrm{glue}}\ar{r}{\Lambda\oplus D_{0|\alpha,0,0}^t}&W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_{0|\alpha}^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})\ar[leftrightarrow]{d}{\mathsf{PT}}\\
K_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}\ar[hook,yshift=0.5ex]{r}\ar[leftarrow,yshift=-0.5ex]{r}[swap]{\operatorname{proj}_{Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}}}\ar{ru}{\kappa\mapsto\kappa_{0|\alpha}}&F\oplus W^{k,2,\delta,\delta}(C_0,u_0^\ast TM)\oplus E_I\ar[leftrightarrow]{u}{\operatorname{id}\oplus\mathsf{PT}}\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{\Lambda\oplus D_{0,0,0}^t}&\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}}W^{k-1,2,\delta,\delta}(\tilde C_0,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C_0,j_0}\otimes_\mathbb C u_0^\ast TM_{J(\ell_{0,0}(A_0(\cdot)))})
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Here $t\in[0,1]$ indicates that the terms \eqref{firstLterm}--\eqref{lastLterm} carry a factor of $t$.  The estimates from Lemmas \ref{approxbottomHF} and \ref{approxtopHF} apply to this modified diagram as well.  Thus as long as we fix a bounded right inverse $Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}$ of $D_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}$, the rest of the diagram makes sense (and, in particular, $T_{0|\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}$ and $T_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}$ are approximate right inverses) for sufficiently small $\alpha>0$.  Furthermore, in any family of $(\Lambda,t,Q^{\Lambda,t})$, the kernels $K_{0,0,0}$, $K_{0|\alpha,0,0}$, $K_{\alpha,0,0}$ form vector bundles, and the identifications of their orientation lines with $\mathfrak o_F\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,q}\otimes\mathfrak o_{q,r}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_I}$ and $\mathfrak o_F\otimes\mathfrak o_{p,r}\otimes\mathfrak o_{E_I}$ (respectively) vary continuously.

Now the map:
\begin{equation}\label{KmapLt}
K_{0|\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}\xrightarrow{{\operatorname{proj}_{T_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}}}\circ{\mathrm{glue}}}K_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}
\end{equation}
is exactly the map we wish to analyze when $F=0$, $\Lambda=0$, $t=1$, and $Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}=0\oplus Q_{0,0,0}$.  More generally, if we allow $F$ nonzero (but still $\Lambda=0$), this map is simply our desired map plus the identity map on $F$.  Since the space of acceptable maps $\Lambda$ is contractible, it suffices to show that \eqref{KmapLt} has the desired action on orientations for any single pair $(F,\Lambda)$, $t=1$, and $Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}=0\oplus Q_{0,0,0}$.

Now by compactness of $[0,1]$, there exists a pair $(F,\Lambda)$ so that $\Lambda\oplus D_{0,0,0}^t$ is surjective for all $t\in[0,1]$.  Fix such a pair $(F,\Lambda)$, and also fix a continuously varying family of bounded right inverses $Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}$ with $Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,1}=0\oplus Q_{0,0,0}$.  Since the kernels form a bundle over the base $[0,1]$ and their orientations vary continuously, it suffices to analyze \eqref{KmapLt} for this $(F,\Lambda)$, $t=0$, and this $Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,0}$.

Now the Floer--Hofer map \eqref{floerhoferoogluing} is \emph{defined} (see \cite[Proposition 9]{floerhoferorientations}) by the property that it is induced by a certain map $K_{0|\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,0}\to K_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,0}$ closely related to \eqref{KmapLt}; clearly it suffices to show that the difference between the Floer--Hofer map and \eqref{KmapLt} is very small.  Consider the map:
\begin{equation}\label{KmapLtLtwo}
K_{0|\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}\xrightarrow{{\operatorname{proj}_{L^2}}\circ{\mathrm{glue}}}K_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}
\end{equation}
in the setting of the above choices of $(F,\Lambda)$, $t=0$, and $Q_{0,0,0}^{\Lambda,t}$, where instead of projecting off of $\operatorname{im} T_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,0}$, we use the $L^2$-orthogonal projection.  The difference between \eqref{KmapLtLtwo} and \eqref{KmapLt} is exactly ${\operatorname{proj}_{L^2}}\circ Q^{\Lambda,0}_{\alpha,0,0}\circ(\Lambda\oplus D^0_{\alpha,0,0})\circ{\mathrm{glue}}$, which has small norm by the estimate in Lemma \ref{approxtopHF}.  Thus it suffices to compare \eqref{KmapLtLtwo} to the Floer--Hofer map.  Now the Floer--Hofer map is given by:
\begin{equation}\label{KmapLtFH}
K_{0|\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,0}\xrightarrow{{\operatorname{proj}_{L^2}}\circ{\mathrm{glue}'}}K_{\alpha,0,0}^{\Lambda,0}
\end{equation}
for a certain map $\mathrm{glue}'$ (see \cite[Proposition 9]{floerhoferorientations}).  However, the norm of the difference $\mathrm{glue}-\mathrm{glue}'$ is very small due to the exponential decay of elements of the kernel (here, we may use the explicit description of $K_{0|\alpha,0,0}$ as the image of $\kappa\mapsto\kappa_{0|\alpha}-Q_{0|\alpha,0,0}D_{0|\alpha,0,0}\kappa_{0|\alpha}$).  Thus we are done.

remark.raw:
\label{equivalenceofIA}
The most natural notion of ``equivalence'' between two implicit atlases $A$ and $B$ on a space $X$ seems to be the existence of a chain of inclusions of implicit atlases $A\subseteq C_1\supseteq\cdots\subseteq C_n\supseteq B$.  It is common to speak of ``cobordisms'' between Kuranishi structures or Kuranishi atlases; the analogous notion of cobordism between implicit atlases (say on the same index set $A$) on a space $X$ is an implicit atlas with boundary on $X\times[0,1]$ whose restriction to the boundary coincides with the first (resp.\ second) implicit atlas on $X\times\{0\}$ (resp.\ $X\times\{1\}$).

It is an easy consequence of the VFC machinery that the virtual fundamental class is invariant under both notions of equivalence.

\label{orbifoldfiberproductrmk}
Let $\mathcal X,\mathcal Y,\mathcal Z$ be orbifolds, and fix maps of orbifolds $\mathcal X,\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Z$.  Then the \emph{orbifold fiber product} $\mathcal X\times_\mathcal Z\mathcal Y$ is simply the categorical $2$-fiber product in the weak $2$-category of orbifolds.  It exists whenever $\mathcal X\times\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Z\times\mathcal Z$ is transverse to the diagonal $\mathcal Z\to\mathcal Z\times\mathcal Z$ (in particular, it exists if at least one of the maps $\mathcal X,\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Z$ is \emph{\'etale} as in the above example of $X_\alpha\to X$).

Thurston \cite[Proof of Proposition 13.2.4]{thurston} gives an explicit hands-on definition of $\mathcal X\times_\mathcal Z\mathcal Y$ in the case $\mathcal X,\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Z$ are both \'etale by working locally on $\mathcal Z$ and then gluing.  In a more modern perspective (defining orbifolds as certain stacks on the site of smooth manifolds), we may define the orbifold fiber product by the following universal property:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Hom}(S,\mathcal X\times_\mathcal Z\mathcal Y):=\operatorname{Hom}(S,\mathcal X)\times_{\operatorname{Hom}(S,\mathcal Z)}\operatorname{Hom}(S,\mathcal Y)
\end{equation}
for any smooth manifold $S$.  The right hand side denotes the $2$-fiber product in the weak $2$-category of groupoids, which admits the explicit description:
\begin{equation}
G_1\times_HG_2:=\Bigl\{(g_1,g_2,\theta)\Bigm|g_1\in G_1,\,g_2\in G_2,\,\theta\!:\!f_1(g_1)\to f_2(g_2)\Bigr\}
\end{equation}
(with the obvious notion of isomorphism between triples $(g_1,g_2,\theta)$) for groupoids $G_1,G_2,H$ and functors $f_i:G_i\to H$.

The orbifold fiber product usually does not coincide with the fiber product of the underlying topological spaces $X\times_YZ$, though there is at least always a map $\mathcal X\times_\mathcal Z\mathcal Y\to X\times_ZY$.  The construction \eqref{fpoverorbifold} would not work if we used the fiber product of topological spaces.

\label{directsumisbetter}
To construct the overlap charts $X_I$, it is crucial that we are able to take the abstract direct sum of the obstruction spaces $E_\alpha$.  Hence, it is better to think of them as abstract vector spaces equipped with maps to $E$ over $V_\alpha$, rather than as trivialized subbundles of $E$ over $V_\alpha$ (which also imposes the unnecessary restriction that $\lambda_\alpha$ be injective), since the latter category is not closed under direct sum.

To illustrate this distinction further, let us mention a problematic alternative version of \eqref{fredholmsectionchart}:
\begin{equation}\label{fredholmsectionchartwrong}
X_I:=\Bigl\{x\in\bigcap_{\alpha\in I}V_\alpha\Bigm|s(x)\in\Bigl(\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha\Bigr)(E_I)\Bigr\}
\end{equation}
This definition agrees with \eqref{fredholmsectionchart} if the following map is injective:
\begin{equation}\label{lambdatotal}
\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}\lambda_\alpha:\bigcap_{\alpha\in I}V_\alpha\times\bigoplus_{\alpha\in I}E_\alpha\to p^{-1}\Bigl(\bigcap_{\alpha\in I}V_\alpha\Bigr)
\end{equation}
but in general it can be different, and indeed, if \eqref{lambdatotal} fails to be injective, the definition \eqref{fredholmsectionchartwrong} isn't particularly useful.  Note that this can occur even if we add the requirement that each $\lambda_\alpha$ be injective.

The importance of using the direct sum was independently observed by McDuff--Wehrheim \cite{mcduffwehrheim}, and it is implicit in Fukaya--Oh--Ohta--Ono \cite{foootechnicaldetails}.

\label{chainfixing}
We use $C_\bullet(X)$ (resp.\ $C^\bullet(X)$) to denote singular simplicial chains (resp.\ cochains) on a space $X$, and we use $C_\bullet(X,Y)$ to denote the cokernel of $C_\bullet(Y)\hookrightarrow C_\bullet(X)$ for $Y\subseteq X$ (``relative chains'').

Let us also recall the ``Eilenberg--Zilber map'' $C_\bullet(X)\otimes C_\bullet(Y)\to C_\bullet(X\times Y)$ for spaces $X$ and $Y$, corresponding to the standard subdivision of $\Delta^n\times\Delta^m$ into $\binom{n+m}n$ copies of $\Delta^{n+m}$.  The Eilenberg--Zilber map is associative (in the sense that it gives rise to a unique map $C_\bullet(X)\otimes C_\bullet(Y)\otimes C_\bullet(Z)\to C_\bullet(X\times Y\times Z)$) and commutative (in the sense that the following diagram commutes:
\begin{equation}\label{chainproductcommutes}
\begin{CD}
C_\bullet(X)\otimes C_\bullet(Y)@>>>C_\bullet(X\times Y)\cr
@VVV@VVV\cr
C_\bullet(Y)\otimes C_\bullet(X)@>>>C_\bullet(Y\times X)
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
for all $X$ and $Y$).  We should point out that this commutativity fails for some other common models of singular chains, for example singular cubical chains (modulo degeneracies).

\label{alsogetsteenrod}
There are (at least) three natural ``homology groups'' which one can assign to a compact Hausdorff space $X$:
\begin{rlist}
\item\emph{Dual of \v Cech cohomology} $\check H^\bullet(X)^\vee$ ($^\vee$ denotes dual, i.e.\ $\operatorname{Hom}(-,R)$).
\item\emph{\v Cech homology} $\check H_\bullet(X)$ (the inverse limit of the homology of nerves of finite covers).
\item\emph{Steenrod--Sitnikov homology} $\overline H_\bullet(X)$ (the homology of the homotopy/derived inverse limit of nerves of finite covers; see \S\ref{steenrodhomologysec}).
\end{rlist}
These are successively more refined, in the sense that there are natural maps:
\begin{equation}
\overline H_\bullet(X)\to\check H_\bullet(X)\to\check H^\bullet(X)^\vee
\end{equation}
If $X$ is homeomorphic to a finite CW-complex, then there are natural isomorphisms:
\begin{equation}
\overline H_\bullet(X)=H_\bullet(X)\qquad\check H_\bullet(X)=H_\bullet(X)\qquad\check H^\bullet(X)^\vee=H^\bullet(X)^\vee
\end{equation}
It follows that a virtual fundamental class in any of these groups can be used for all the applications we are aware of.  On the other hand, there are some potential advantages to working with the more refined homology groups:
\begin{rlist}
\item$\overline H_\bullet$ and $\check H_\bullet$ retain torsion information.
\item$\overline H_\bullet$ and $\check H_\bullet$ have a natural ``extension of scalars'' map for any map of rings $R\to S$.
\item$\overline H_\bullet$ has the expected long exact sequence for pairs of spaces.
\end{rlist}

\label{Lambdaandpullback}
Let $\mathcal X/Z_\bullet$ be an $H$-equivariant flow category diagram with implicit atlas $\mathcal A$ and coherent orientations $\omega$.  Let $f:Y_\bullet\to Z_\bullet$ be a map, and consider $f^\ast\mathcal X/Y_\bullet$ with implicit atlas $f^\ast\mathcal A$ and coherent orientations $f^\ast\omega$.  Then there is a canonical fiber diagram relating the resolutions:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
