\begin{proof}
Since $\mathcal Y$ is saturated, there exists a morphism of MF-sets $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\to\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ (where $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}$ is as in Example \ref{standardsimplicialMFset}).  Since $C_\bullet(X)=C^{\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}}_\bullet(X)$ by definition, we obtain a chain map $C_\bullet(X)\to C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)$.  The resulting map:
\begin{equation}\label{homologyYcomparison}
H_\bullet(X)\to H_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)
\end{equation}
is independent of the choice of morphism $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\to\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$, as can be seen as follows.  There is an MF-set $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\sqcup\{\Delta^n\times[0,1]\}_{n\geq 0}\sqcup\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}$ where each $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}$ is as in Example \ref{standardsimplicialMFset}, where $\Delta^n\times[0,1]$ is given the product stratification ($[0,1]$ stratified by $\{\{0\},\{1\},(0,1)\}$), and $\Delta^n\times\{0\}$ (resp.\ $\Delta^n\times\{1\}$) is identified with the first (resp.\ second) copy of $\Delta^n$.  Since $\mathcal Y$ is saturated, it follows that for any pair of morphisms $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\to\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ there is a morphism $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\sqcup\{\Delta^n\times[0,1]\}_{n\geq 0}\sqcup\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\to\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ whose restriction to the two copies of $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}$ are the two given morphisms.  From this data one easily constructs a chain homotopy between the two maps $C_\bullet(X)\to C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)$.  Hence the map \eqref{homologyYcomparison} is canonical.

Now let us show that \eqref{homologyYcomparison} is an isomorphism.  Fix a map $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\to\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$.  Fix triangulations $T_i$ of $M_i$ for which each $M_i^{\leq\mathfrak s}$ is a union of simplices, which are compatible with the face identifications, and which restrict to the tautological triangulation of $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}$.  Such triangulations may be constructed by induction.  By triangulation, we mean a triangulation in which each simplex is equipped with a total order on its set of vertices, compatible with its faces (i.e.\ a semisimplicial set rather than a simplicial complex).  Such triangluations induce a map of complexes $C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\to C_\bullet(X)$, and the composition $C_\bullet(X)\to C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\to C_\bullet(X)$ is clearly the identity map.  It suffices to show that the other composition is chain homotopic to the identity map.

Now let us define a new MF-set in terms of the map $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\to\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ and the triangulations $T_i$.  Note that the stratification of $M_i$ by the face poset $\mathcal F(T_i)$ of $T_i$ refines the stratification by $\mathcal S_i$; in other words we have maps $M_i\to\mathcal F(T_i)\to\mathcal S_i$.  The objects of the new MF-set are $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\sqcup\{M_i\times[0,1]\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$, where $M_i\times[0,1]$ is stratified by the following strata:
\begin{align}
M_i^\mathfrak s\times\{0\}&\quad\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_i\\
M_i^\mathfrak s\times(0,1)&\quad\mathfrak s\in\mathcal S_i\\
M_i^\mathfrak t\times\{1\}&\quad\mathfrak t\in\mathcal F(T_i)
\end{align}
Thus the poset of strata of $M_i\times[0,1]$ is $\mathcal S_i\sqcup\mathcal S_i\sqcup\mathcal F(T_i)$.  For the face identifications, $M_i\times\{0\}$ is identified tautologically with $M_i$, and each of the closed strata $M_i^{\leq\mathfrak t}\times\{1\}$ ($\mathfrak t\in\mathcal F(T_i)$) is identified with the corresponding $\Delta^n$ (considered as an object of $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ via the inclusion $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\to\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$).

Now we have a chain of inclusions $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0}\hookrightarrow\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\hookrightarrow\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\sqcup\{M_i\times[0,1]\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$.  Since $\mathcal Y$ is saturated, there in fact exists a map backwards $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\sqcup\{M_i\times[0,1]\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\to\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ (acting identically on $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$).  Using this map, we obtain a chain map $C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\to C_{\bullet+1}^\mathcal Y(X)$ (precompose chains with the projection $M_i\times[0,1]\to M_i$).  This map is a chain homotopy between the identity map and the composition $C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\to C_\bullet(X)\to C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)$, so we are done.
\end{proof}

Equip $S^1$ with its standard PL structure, for which the group operations are PL.

\begin{definition}\label{SSMFdef}
An \emph{$S^1$-MF-set} is an MF-set $\mathcal Y$ along with a principal $S^1$-bundle $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$ where $(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$ (and hence also $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$) is an increasing union of compact polyhedra (we do not require $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$ to be contractible), and for every $i\in\mathcal Y$, a pullback diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{SSMFstructuremaps}
\begin{tikzcd}
M_i\ar{r}\ar{d}&(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{d}\\
N_i\ar{r}&(BS^1)_\mathcal Y
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
with PL maps where $N_i$ (and hence also $M_i$) is a PL manifold, and where the stratification on $M_i$ is pulled back from $N_i$, such that these diagrams are compatible with the face identifications.  A map of $S^1$-MF-sets $\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Y'$ is said to be injective iff it is injective as a map of sets and the map $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y'}$ is injective.

There is a forgetful functor from $S^1$-MF-sets to MF-sets, where we remember $M_i$ (of course, there is another natural forgetful functor remembering $N_i$, though we will never use it).  When speaking of a morphism $\mathcal Y\to\mathcal Y'$ where $\mathcal Y$ is an $S^1$-MF-set and $\mathcal Y'$ is an MF-set, we implicitly apply the forgetful functor to $\mathcal Y$.

The category of $S^1$-MF-sets has a natural symmetric monoidal structure: given $S^1$-MF-sets $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ and $\{M_j'\}_{j\in\mathcal Y'}$, we may define their product $\{M_i\times M_j'\}_{(i,j)\in\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}$, which is again an $S^1$-MF-set, via the diagonal $S^1$-action on $M_i\times M_j'$ and $(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}:=(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\times(ES^1)_{\mathcal Y'}$ with the diagonal action.  The forgetful functor from $S^1$-MF-sets to MF-sets is clearly a symmetric monoidal functor.  It also makes sense to take the product of an $S^1$-MF-set and an MF-set.

For an $S^1$-MF-set $\mathcal Y$, let $C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)$ (for $X$ an $S^1$-space) denote the complex generated by commuting diagrams of $S^1$-equivariant maps:
\begin{equation}\label{SSchaindiagram}
\begin{tikzcd}
M_i\ar{r}\ar{d}&X\times(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{r}\ar{d}&(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{d}\\
N_i\ar{r}&(X\times(ES^1)_\mathcal Y)/S^1\ar{r}&(BS^1)_\mathcal Y
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
($i\in\mathcal Y$), where the outer square coincides with \eqref{SSMFstructuremaps}, with differential given by the obvious sum over all codimension one faces.  A generator \eqref{SSchaindiagram} resides in degree $\dim N_i$.  Note that there are natural compatible maps:
\begin{align}
\mathcal C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y}(X')&\to C_{\bullet+1}^\mathcal Y(X)\\
C_\bullet^\mathcal Y(X)\otimes\mathcal C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y'}(X')&\to C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}(X\times X')\\
C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y}(X)\otimes\mathcal C_\bullet^{S^1,\mathcal Y'}(X')&\to C_{\bullet+1}^{S^1,\mathcal Y\times\mathcal Y'}(X\times X')
\end{align}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
An $S^1$-MF-set $\mathcal Y$ is called \emph{saturated} iff $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$ is contractible and every pullback diagram:
\begin{equation}
\begin{tikzcd}
M\ar{r}\ar{d}&(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\ar{d}\\
N\ar{r}&(BS^1)_\mathcal Y
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
where $N$ (and thus $M$) is a PL manifold with cells, along with strictly transitive identifications of the faces of positive codimension with elements of $\mathcal Y$ (as in Definition \ref{SSMFdef}), is isomorphic to some $i\in\mathcal Y$.

Note that the notions of saturation for MF-sets and $S^1$-MF-sets are different: an $S^1$-MF-set is \emph{never} saturated as an MF-set.
\end{definition}

Every $S^1$-MF-set $\mathcal Y$ embeds into a saturated $S^1$-MF-set $\mathcal Y_\infty$, which may be constructed (again, non-canonically) by first embedding $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y$ into something contractible, and then proceeding as in the case of MF-sets.

\begin{proof}
All $S^1$-MF-sets in this proof will share the same $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$, so we will omit the subscript $_\mathcal Y$ from the notation.

Let $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}$ denote the $S^1$-MF-set indexed by pairs $(n,f)$ consisting of an integer $n\geq 0$ and a PL map $f:\Delta^n\to BS^1$, where the stratifications and face identifications are as in Example \ref{standardsimplicialMFset}.  Now the complex $C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)$ is freely generated by maps $\Delta^n\to(X\times ES^1)/S^1$ whose composition with the projection $(X\times ES^1)/S^1\to BS^1$ is PL.  A straightforward approximation argument shows that the inclusion of this complex into the complex generated by all maps $\Delta^n\to(X\times ES^1)/S^1$ is a quasi-isomorphism.  Hence there is a canonical isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
H^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)\xrightarrow\sim H_\bullet((X\times ES^1)/S^1)=H_\bullet^{S^1}(X)
\end{equation}

As in the proof of Lemma \ref{saturatedcalculatesH} there exists a morphism of $S^1$-MF-sets $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\to\mathcal Y$ since $\mathcal Y$ is saturated.  This induces a chain map $C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)\to C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)$, which as before induces a canonical map on homology:
\begin{equation}
H^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)\to H^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)
\end{equation}
which is independent of the choice of morphism $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\to\mathcal Y$.  It suffices to show that this map is an isomorphism.

Fix a map $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\to\mathcal Y$, which we observe is necessarily injective.  Fix triangulations $T_i$ of $M_i$ as in the proof of Lemma \ref{saturatedcalculatesH}.  Such triangulations induce a map of complexes $C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)\to C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)$.  The composition $C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)\to C^{S^1,\mathcal Y}_\bullet(X)\to C^{S^1,\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}}_\bullet(X)$ is clearly the identity map.  It thus suffices to show that the reverse composition is chain homotopic to the identity.

As in the proof of Lemma \ref{saturatedcalculatesH}, we construct a new $S^1$-MF-set, namely $\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\sqcup\{M_i\times[0,1]\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$, from the inclusion $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\hookrightarrow\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$ and the triangulations $T_i$.  There are inclusions $\{\Delta^n\}_{n\geq 0,f:\Delta^n\to BS^1}\hookrightarrow\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\hookrightarrow\{M_i\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}\sqcup\{M_i\times[0,1]\}_{i\in\mathcal Y}$.  The desired chain homotopy may then be constructed as in the proof of Lemma \ref{saturatedcalculatesH}.

The fact that the induced map $H^{S^1}_\bullet(X)\to H_{\bullet+1}(X)$ is the Gysin map is left to the reader.
\end{proof}

Note the similarity of \eqref{colimitYone}--\eqref{colimitYtwo} with Definition \ref{Fmodulecofibrantdef}.  One should think of these conditions as being: ``$\mathcal Y$ and $\mathcal Y_{S^1}$ are both cofibrant''.

\begin{proof}
It suffices to perform the inductive step for a given $(\sigma,p,q)$.  First, we show that the colimits \eqref{colimitYone}--\eqref{colimitYtwo} exist, essentially by rewriting the proofs of Lemmas \ref{cofibrantinjectivelemma} and \ref{cofibranthaslotsofcofibrations} in the present context.  We actually show the more general statement that the colimits:
\begin{equation}\label{Tcolimit}
\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak t\in\mathcal T}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t)\qquad\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak t\in\mathcal T}\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t)
\end{equation}
exist for any $\mathcal T\subseteq\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)$ which is downward closed.  We show this by induction on the cardinality of $\mathcal T$.  If $\mathcal T=\varnothing$, existence is trivial.  For $\mathcal T$ nonempty, let $\mathfrak t_0\in\mathcal T$ be any maximal element, and observe that \eqref{Tcolimit} can be written as the colimit of the diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{colimitdiagramnice}
\begin{tikzcd}[row sep = tiny]
&\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t_0)\\
\smash{\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\begin{smallmatrix}\mathfrak t\in\mathcal T\cr\mathfrak t\prec\mathfrak t_0\end{smallmatrix}}}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t)\ar[hook]{ur}\ar{rd}\\
&\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak t\in\mathcal T\setminus\mathfrak t_0}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak t)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
Let us show that the top arrow is injective.  Write $\mathfrak t_0$ in the form \eqref{SdataA}--\eqref{SdataB}.  Then we have:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak t_0}=\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma|[j_{a_0}\ldots j_{a_1}],p_0,p_1)\times\cdots\times\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma|[j_{a_{m-1}}\ldots j_{a_m}],p_{m-1},p_m)
\end{equation}
Now we consider the $m$-cubical diagram:
\begin{equation}
\prod_{i=1}^m\left[\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i)}\mathcal Y(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i,\mathfrak s)\hookrightarrow\mathcal Y(\sigma|[j_{a_{i-1}}\ldots j_{a_i}],p_{i-1},p_i)\right]
\end{equation}
Now the top arrow of \eqref{colimitdiagramnice} is simply the map to the maximal vertex of this $m$-cube from the colimit over the $m$-cube minus the maximal vertex (this is where we use the fact that $\mathcal T$ is downward closed).  This is injective for any cubical diagram of the form $\prod_{i=1}^m[A_i\hookrightarrow B_i]$ where each $A_i\hookrightarrow B_i$ is injective.  Now that the top arrow in \eqref{colimitdiagramnice} is injective, the existence of the colimit is clear (first take the colimit of underlying sets and then the rest of the structure extends in an obvious way), and hence the colimit in \eqref{Tcolimit} exists.  This reasoning applies equally well to $\mathcal Y_{S^1}$ (including $(ES^1)_\mathcal Y\to(BS^1)_\mathcal Y$).

Now that we have shown that the colimits on the left hand side of \eqref{colimitYone}--\eqref{colimitYtwo} exist, it remains to define $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$ and $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)$.

We observed earlier that any $S^1$-MF-set injects into a saturated $S^1$-MF-set.  Let $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)$ be any saturated $S^1$-MF-set with an injection \eqref{colimitYtwo}.  Note that with this definition, the product/face maps with target $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)$ are obvious, as is their compatibility.

To define $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$, consider the following diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{biggluingcolimit}
\begin{tikzcd}
\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)}\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)\\
\displaystyle\operatornamewithlimits{colim}_{\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal X(\sigma,p,q)}\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)\ar{u}\ar[hook]{r}&\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
The lower colimit is in the category of $S^1$-MF-sets.  It is clear from the inductive proof of its existence that this particular colimit commutes with the forgetful functor to MF-sets.  Hence we may equally well think of this colimit as taking place in the category of MF-sets, and with this perspective the definition of the vertical map is clear.  Now since the horizontal map is injective, the colimit of this diagram (in the category of MF-sets) clearly exists (first take the colimit in sets and then the rest of the structure is obvious).  We pick any saturated MF-set into which the colimit of \eqref{biggluingcolimit} embeds, and we call this saturated MF-set $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$.  Via the embedding of the upper colimit into $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$, it is tautological that the face/product maps with target $\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$ exist and are compatible.  The map $\mathcal Y_{S^1}(\sigma,p,q)\to\mathcal Y(\sigma,p,q)$ is similarly tautological, as is the fact that it is compatible with the product/face maps.
\end{proof}

