\begin{proof}
After possibly shrinking $U$, choose a local projection $\pi:U\to T_pN$.  Then let $D$ be $\pi^{-1}$ of a regular value of $\pi\circ f$ (which exists by Sard's theorem).
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let us observe that if $C_0\subseteq C$ is any unstable irreducible component, we must have $u:C_0\to X$ is nonconstant.  Hence since $u$ is $J$-holomorphic, it follows that there is a point on $C_0$ (which is not a node or marked point) where $du$ is injective.  It follows using Lemma \ref{sortofsard} that there exists $D\subseteq X$ such that $u\pitchfork D$ and adding $u^{-1}(D)$ to $C$ as marked points makes $C$ stable.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Fix a point in $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)$ (that is, a curve $u:C\to X$).  We will construct $\alpha\in A^\mathrm{GW}$ so that this point is contained in $\psi_{\varnothing\{\alpha\}}((s_\alpha|\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_{\{\alpha\}}^\mathrm{reg})^{-1}(0))$.

First, pick $D_\alpha\subseteq X$ satisfying the conclusion of Lemma \ref{stabilizationlemma}.

Now let $r_\alpha=\#u^{-1}(D_\alpha)$.  Adding $u^{-1}(D_\alpha)$ as extra marked points to $C$ gives a point in $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n+r_\alpha}/S_{r_\alpha}$, and we pick some local orbifold chart $\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha/\Gamma_\alpha\to\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n+r_\alpha}/S_{r_\alpha}$ covering this point.

Now let us consider the linearized $\bar\partial$ operator:
\begin{equation}\label{linearizeddelbarforthickening}
D\bar\partial(u,\cdot):C^\infty(C,u^\ast TX)\to C^\infty(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C u^\ast TX)
\end{equation}
($\tilde C$ being the normalization of $C$) where $C^\infty(C,u^\ast TX)\subseteq C^\infty(\tilde C,u^\ast TX)$ is the subspace of functions descending continuously to $C$.  If $(k,p)\in\mathbb Z_{\geq 1}\times(1,\infty)$ with $kp>2$, then we get a corresponding $D\bar\partial(u,\cdot)$ map $W^{k,p}\to W^{k-1,p}$ (the restriction on $(k,p)$ comes from the need to define $W^{k,p}(C,u^\ast TX)\subseteq W^{k,p}(\tilde C,u^\ast TX)$).  This operator is Fredholm; in particular its cokernel is finite-dimensional.

Suppose we have a finite-dimensional vector space $E_0$ equipped with a linear map:
\begin{equation}\label{lambdazero}
\tilde\lambda_0:E_0\to C^\infty(\tilde C,\Omega^{0,1}_{\tilde C}\otimes_\mathbb C u^\ast TX)
\end{equation}
supported away from (the inverse image in $\tilde C$ of) the nodes and marked points of $C$.  Then the following conditions are equivalent (exercise using elliptic regularity):
\begin{rlist}
\item\eqref{lambdazero} is surjective onto the cokernel of $D\bar\partial(u,\cdot):C^\infty\to C^\infty$.
\item\eqref{lambdazero} is surjective onto the cokernel of $D\bar\partial(u,\cdot):W^{k,p}\to W^{k-1,p}$ for some $(k,p)$.
\item\eqref{lambdazero} is surjective onto the cokernel of $D\bar\partial(u,\cdot):W^{k,p}\to W^{k-1,p}$ for all $(k,p)$.
\end{rlist}
There exists such a pair $E_0$ and $\tilde\lambda_0$ satisfying these conditions since we may choose $k=1$ and $p>2$ and then remember that $C^\infty$ functions supported away from the nodes and marked points are dense in $W^{0,p}=L^p$.

Now pick any isomorphism to a fiber $\phi_\alpha:C\to\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha$ and extend $\tilde\lambda_0$ to a map $\lambda_0:E_0\to C^\infty(\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha\times X,\Omega^{0,1}_{\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha/\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha}\otimes_\mathbb C TX)$ supported away from the nodes and marked points of the fibers.  Define $E_\alpha=E_0[\Gamma]$ and define $\lambda_\alpha:E_\alpha\to C^\infty(\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha\times X,\Omega^{0,1}_{\overline{\mathcal C}_\alpha/\overline{\mathcal M}_\alpha}\otimes_\mathbb C TX)$ by $\Gamma_\alpha$-linear extension from $\lambda_0$.  It is now clear by definition that $u:C\to X$ is covered by $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)_{\{\alpha\}}^\mathrm{reg}$, where $\alpha\in A^\mathrm{GW}$ is the element just constructed.
\end{proof}

We have now shown the following.

\begin{proof}
Let $J_0$ and $J_1$ be any two smooth $\omega$-tame almost complex structures on $X$.  We denote by $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_0)$ and $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_1)$ the corresponding moduli spaces of stable maps (denoted earlier by simply $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X)$ when we considered just a fixed $J$).

There exists a smooth path of $\omega$-tame almost complex structures $J_{[0,1]}=\{J_t\}_{t\in[0,1]}$ connecting $J_0$ and $J_1$.  Let us consider the corresponding ``parameterized'' moduli space of stable maps $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_{[0,1]})$.  The construction from \S\ref{gromovwittenimplicitatlassubsection} gives an implicit atlas with boundary $A^\mathrm{GW}$ on $\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_{[0,1]})$ whose restriction to $\partial\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_{[0,1]}):=\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_0)\sqcup\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_1)$ agrees with $A^\mathrm{GW}$ on these spaces.  Hence it follows using Lemmas \ref{disjointunionfc} and \ref{cobordismworks} that $[\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_0)]^\mathrm{vir}=[\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_1)]^\mathrm{vir}$ in $\check H^\bullet(\overline{\mathcal M}_{g,n}^\beta(X,J_{[0,1]}))^\vee$, which is enough.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
As in the proof of Lemma \ref{stabilizationlemma}, we use (an appropriate variant for manifolds with corners of) Lemma \ref{sortofsard} (which we may also use to avoid the periodic orbits in question).  It thus suffices to show that for any unstable component $C_0$ of $C$, there exists a point $p\in C_0$ where $d((\operatorname{id}_{M\times S^1}\times\ell)\circ u):TC_0\to TM\times TS^1\times T\Delta^n$ injective.  To find such a point, we split into two cases.

First, suppose $u:C_0\to S^1\times\mathbb R$ is constant.  Then $u:C_0\to M$ is a (nonconstant!) $J$-holomorphic sphere, and thus has a point of injectivity of $du$.

Second, suppose $u:C_0\to S^1\times\mathbb R$ is not constant.  Then $u:C_0\to S^1\times\mathbb R$ is an isomorphism; let us use $(t,s)\in S^1\times\mathbb R$ as coordinates on $C_0$.  Now $\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}\in TM\times TS^1$ has nonzero coordinate in the $TS^1$ component (everywhere), and $\frac{\partial u}{\partial s}\in TM\times TS^1$ has zero coordinate in the $TS^1$ coordinate (everywhere).  Hence if $du$ is everywhere noninjective, we find that $\frac{\partial u}{\partial s}=0\in TM\times TS^1$ everywhere.  It follows that $u:C_0\to M\times S^1$ is independent of the $\mathbb R$ coordinate, and thus is simply a trivial cylinder mapping onto a periodic orbit.  Now the stability condition (the automorphism group being finite) implies that the corresponding (piece of a) flow line $\ell:\mathbb R\to\Delta^n$ is nontrivial.  It follows that we have the desired injectivity.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
We have shown above that each individual $\mathcal A(\sigma,p,q)^{\geq\mathfrak s}$ is an implicit atlas on $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{\leq\mathfrak s}$.  The required compatibility isomorphisms between these implicit atlases follow directly from the definition.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Suppose a stable Floer trajectory $(C,\ell,u)$ is $S^1$-invariant.  Then each of the components $\{C_1^\circ,\ldots,C_k^\circ\}$ must be smooth (no nodes) and hence isomorphic to $S^1\times\mathbb R$.  Using the holomorphic projection $M\times S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R\to S^1\times\coprod_{i=1}^k\mathbb R$, we get holomorphic identifications $C_i^\circ=S^1\times\mathbb R$.  Since $(C,\ell,u)$ is $S^1$-invariant, the function $u:C_i^\circ\to M$ must be independent of the $S^1$-coordinate, and hence (examining the $\bar\partial$-equation) is simply a Morse flow line of $H$.  Running this argument in reverse, we also see that every stable Morse trajectory gives rise to a stable Floer trajectory which is $S^1$-invariant.
\end{proof}

We will also need the following deeper fact (and henceforth we assume that $H$ is defined as in Lemma \ref{morseistransverse}):

\begin{proof}
Salamon--Zehnder \cite[p1342, Theorem 7.3(1)]{salamonzehnder} show that $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma^0,p,q)^{S^1}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma^0,p,q)^\mathrm{reg}$ for the $0$-simplex $\sigma^0$ and sufficiently small $\epsilon>0$.  Since we only consider constant families of $J$ and $H$, the linearized operators for the higher $\sigma^i$ can be written in terms of the linearized operators for $\sigma^0$, and it follows that $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{S^1}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^\mathrm{reg}$ for all $\sigma$.

Now we have $\overline{\mathcal M}_\mathrm{morse}(\sigma,p,q)=\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^{S^1}\subseteq\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^\mathrm{reg}$.  It remains to show that this is an open inclusion.  This can likely be seen by following closely the gluing argument used to prove Propositions \ref{HFgluingneeded} and \ref{morseassumptionMS}, however we can argue directly as follows.  Both $\overline{\mathcal M}_\mathrm{morse}(\sigma,p,q)$ and $\overline{\mathcal M}(\sigma,p,q)^\mathrm{reg}$ are topological manifolds with corners, and the stratification for the former is the pullback of the stratification for the latter.  Their dimensions are dictated by the Morse index and the Conley--Zehnder index respectively, which in this case coincide.  Hence we are done by Lemma \ref{cornersinvarianceofdomain} below.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
