Pick any $\omega$-compatible almost complex structure $J$ and consider the vertex $(J,H)\inJ\!H_\bullet(M)$.  Over this vertex, pick any complex $F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)$ which calculates $F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)$.  Then $F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)$ is a free $\mathbb Q[[\pi]]$-module of rank:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)\leq\#\operatorname{Fix}\phi_H
\end{equation}
(its rank equals the number of null-homotopic periodic orbits).  By Theorem \ref{HFcalculation}, the homology $F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)$ is free of rank:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)=\dim H_\bullet(M;\mathbb Q)
\end{equation}
Now by definition, there is a $\mathbb Q[[\pi]]$-linear boundary map $d:F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)\to F\mathbb C^{\bullet+1}(M)$ and by definition $\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)=\ker d/\operatorname{im} d$.  Now apply Lemma \ref{homologyhassmallerrank} to conclude that:
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb H^\bullet(M)\leq\operatorname{rk}_{\mathbb Q[[\pi]]}F\mathbb C^\bullet(M)
\end{equation}
Thus we are done.
\end{proof}

