\begin{proof}
It suffices to show the weak vanishing property for $\mathcal F^\bullet$.  Let $\alpha\in H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K)$ be arbitrary with $i<0$.  By Remark \ref{nbhdintersectiongood} and Lemma \ref{anycompactintersectionworks}, the following is a quasi-isomorphism:
\begin{equation}
\varinjlim_{\begin{smallmatrix}Z\subseteq V\cr V\textrm{ open}\end{smallmatrix}}\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap\overline V)\to\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap Z)
\end{equation}
Since $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap Z)=0$ by strong vanishing for $i^\ast\mathcal F^\bullet$, we see that the image of $\alpha$ in $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap\overline V)$ vanishes for some open $V\supseteq Z$.  Now applying \eqref{homotopysheafproperty} to $K=(K\cap\overline V)\cup(K\setminus V)$, we see that the vanishing of the image of $\alpha$ in $H^i\mathcal F^\bullet(K\cap\overline V)$ implies that $\alpha$ ``comes from'' the cohomology of $[\mathcal F^\bullet(K\setminus V)\to\mathcal F^{\bullet-1}(K\cap\overline V\cap(K\setminus V))$].  On the other hand, this latter group vanishes in degrees $i<0$ by strong vanishing for $j^\ast\mathcal F^\bullet$.  Thus $\alpha=0$, so we have even shown strong vanishing for $\mathcal F^\bullet$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Statement (\ref{scacyc}) is obvious.

Statement (\ref{scpatch}) can be deduced from Mayer--Vietoris using a form of the nine lemma as we now explain.  Let us write $U:=X\setminus A$ and $V:=X\setminus B$.  Now consider the following total complex:
\begin{equation}
\begin{CD}
C_\bullet(U\cap V)@>>>C_{\bullet+1}(U)\oplus C_{\bullet+1}(V)@>>>C_{\bullet+2}(U\cup V)\cr
@VVV@VVV@VVV\cr
C_{\bullet+1}(X)@>>>C_{\bullet+2}(X)\oplus C_{\bullet+2}(X)@>>>C_{\bullet+3}(X)\cr
@VVV@VVV@VVV\cr
C_{\bullet+2}(X,U\cap V)@>>>C_{\bullet+3}(X,U)\oplus C_{\bullet+3}(X,V)@>>>C_{\bullet+4}(X,U\cup V)\cr
\end{CD}
\end{equation}
The columns are acyclic (by definition of relative chains), and hence the total complex is acyclic as well.  The first row is acyclic by Mayer--Vietoris, and the second row is obviously acyclic.  Thus the third row is acyclic, as needed.

Statement (\ref{sccont}) is true because the map is in fact an isomorphism on the chain level.  It is clearly surjective; to show injectivity we must show that a singular chain on $X$ which is disjoint from $K$ is in fact disjoint from $K_\alpha$ for some $\alpha\in A$.  This follows because the standard $n$-simplex is compact.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Let $X^+$ be the one-point compactification of $X$.  Define a complex of $\mathcal K$-presheaves $\mathcal F^\bullet$ on $X^+$:
\begin{equation}\label{purehomotopyKsheafinPD}
\mathcal F^\bullet(K):=[C_{n-1-\bullet}(N,N\setminus K)\to C_{n-\bullet}(M,M\setminus K)]
\end{equation}
(where on the right hand side by $K$ we really mean $K\cap X$).  Applying Lemmas \ref{singularchainshomotopyKsheaf} and \ref{associatedgradedhomotopysheaf}, we see that $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.

We claim that $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.  Certainly the homology of $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is bounded below, because the singular chain complex of a topological manifold has homology bounded above and $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is built out of these.  Now, it is easy to calculate:
\begin{equation}\label{stalkhomologyofPDpure}
H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet_p=\begin{cases}\mathbb Z&p\in M^\circ\cup N^\circ\cr 0&p\notin M^\circ\cup N^\circ\end{cases}
\end{equation}
concentrated in degree zero (see \cite[p231 \S3.3]{hatcher} for the special case $N=\partial M=\varnothing$).  Hence $\mathcal F^\bullet$ is a pure homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf.  In fact, it is not hard to see (using the adjunction $j_!\dashv j^\ast$) that \eqref{stalkhomologyofPDpure} lifts to an isomorphism of sheaves $f_!i^\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_M\to H^0\mathcal F^\bullet$ where $f:X\hookrightarrow X^+$.

Now we conclude:
\begin{equation*}
H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet(X^+)\overset{\text{Prop \ref{cechcohomologypurehomotopysheaf}}}=\check H^\bullet(X^+;f_!i^\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_M)\overset{\text{Lem \ref{cptcechembeddingproperty}}}=\check H^\bullet_c(X;i^\ast j_!j^\ast\mathfrak o_M)
\end{equation*}
Now observe that $H^\bullet\mathcal F^\bullet(X^+)$ is the left hand side of \eqref{poincaredualitygoal}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Why homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves instead of homotopy sheaves?]
The naive modification of \eqref{purehomotopyKsheafinPD} substituting open $U\subseteq X$ in place of compact $K\subseteq X$ does not yield a homotopy sheaf.  To get a homotopy sheaf, one could apply the proposed functor $R\alpha_\ast:\operatorname{hShv}_\mathcal K X\to\operatorname{hShv} X$ from Remark \ref{homotopyKnotsheavesequivalencermk} to the homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaf \eqref{purehomotopyKsheafinPD}.  It is somewhat easier, though, to just work directly in the setting of homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves (which has some advantages, for example stalks of $\mathcal K$-presheaves are easier to define/understand).  It is for this reason that throughout this paper we work with homotopy $\mathcal K$-sheaves instead of homotopy sheaves.
\end{remark}

\begin{proof}
We filter $\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{\mathfrak s\preceq\mathfrak t\in\mathcal S}A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak t}$ by the number of $\mathfrak s_0,\ldots,\mathfrak s_p$ which are not equal to $\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}$.  The zeroth associated graded piece is the subcomplex $A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top},\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}$, so it suffices to show that all the other associated graded pieces are acyclic.  Each of these is a direct sum of mapping cones $[A^{\bullet+1}_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s^\mathrm{top}}\to A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak t}]$, which are acyclic by assumption.
