\begin{definition}[Cofibrant replacement functor $Q$]\label{cofibrantreplacements}
Let $\mathcal W_\bullet$ be an $\mathsf{F}$-module complex.  Suppose that $\mathcal W_\bullet$ satisfies the following properties:
\begin{rlist}
\item Each $\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q)$ is cofibrant.
\item Each $\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)$ is finite.
\end{rlist}
In this case, we define (functorially) an $\mathsf{F}$-module complex $Q\mathcal W_\bullet$ (called the \emph{cofibrant replacement}) with the following properties:
\begin{rlist}
\item$Q\mathcal W_\bullet$ is cofibrant.
\item$\operatorname{supp} Q\mathcal W_\bullet=\operatorname{supp}\mathcal W_\bullet$.
\item There is a (functorial) surjective quasi-isomorphism $Q\mathcal W_\bullet\overset\sim\twoheadrightarrow\mathcal W_\bullet$ (compatible with product/face maps).
\end{rlist}
We define $Q\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q)$ and the product/face maps with target $(\sigma,p,q)$ by induction on the set of triples $(\sigma,p,q)$, equipped with the partial order $\preceq_\mathcal W$ in which $(\sigma',p',q')\preceq_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)$ iff $(\sigma',p',q')$ appears as a ``factor'' of some element of $\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)$.  This partial order is \emph{well-founded} (i.e.\ there is no strictly decreasing infinite sequence) since each $\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)$ is finite, and thus it is valid for induction.  The inductive step for $(\sigma,p,q)$ works as follows.  By the induction hypothesis, we have defined $Q\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)$ for all $\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)$.  Hence it suffices to construct (functorially) $Q\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q)$ fitting into the following commutative diagram:
\begin{equation}\label{cofibrantdefdiagram}
\begin{tikzcd}[column sep = huge]
