Now we define $Q\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q)$ to be the mapping cylinder of the diagonal composition.  Since the domain and codomain of this map are both cofibrant (for the domain, use Lemma \ref{cofibranthaslotsofcofibrations}, which applies since $\mathcal S_\mathcal W(\sigma,p,q)$ has been assumed to be finite), it follows that the top map is a cofibration and that $Q\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q)$ is cofibrant.  The product/face maps with target $Q\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q)$ are defined using the top horizontal map; they are compatible since by construction they factor through the colimit in the upper left corner.

Certainly $\operatorname{supp} Q\mathcal W_\bullet\supseteq\operatorname{supp}\mathcal W_\bullet$.  Conversely, suppose $Q\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q)$ is nonzero.  Then either $\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q)\ne 0$ (so $(\sigma,p,q)\in\operatorname{supp}\mathcal W_\bullet$), or $Q\mathcal W_\bullet(\sigma,p,q,\mathfrak s)\ne 0$ for some $\mathfrak s\in\partial\mathcal S(\sigma,p,q)$ (so by induction, the triples comprising $\mathfrak s$ are in $\operatorname{supp}\mathcal W_\bullet$, and hence so is $(\sigma,p,q)$).
