\begin{definition}[Pullback on $\check H^\bullet$ and $\check H^\bullet_c$]\label{pullbackdefn}
Let $f:X\to Y$ be a map of spaces.  An open cover of $Y$ pulls back to give an open cover of $X$, and this gives an identification of the \v Cech complex for the cover of $Y$ with coefficients in $f_\ast\mathcal F$ with the \v Cech complex for the cover of $X$ with coefficients in $\mathcal F$.  Hence we get natural maps:
\begin{rlist}
\item$f^\ast:\check H^\bullet(Y;f_\ast\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet(X;\mathcal F)$ for $\mathcal F\in\operatorname{Shv} X$.
\item$f^\ast:\check H^\bullet_c(Y;f_\ast\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet_c(X;\mathcal F)$ for $\mathcal F\in\operatorname{Shv} X$ (if $f$ is proper).
\end{rlist}
\end{definition}

\begin{proof}
For $K\subseteq X$, there are natural maps:
\begin{equation}\label{trivialKequivalence}
\begin{tikzcd}
\check H^\bullet_K(Y;f_!\mathcal F)\ar[yshift=0.5ex]{r}{f^\ast}&\ar[yshift=-0.5ex]{l}{f_!}\check H^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)
\end{tikzcd}
\end{equation}
(for $f^\ast$: pull back the open cover) (for $f_!$: add $Y\setminus K$ to the open cover and extend by zero).  It is easy to see that $f_!$ and $f^\ast$ are inverses.  The desired map $f_!:\check H^\bullet_c(X;\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet_c(Y;f_!\mathcal F)$ is defined as the composition:
\begin{equation}
\varinjlim_{K\subseteq X}\check H^\bullet_K(X;\mathcal F)\overset{\eqref{trivialKequivalence}}=\varinjlim_{K\subseteq X}\check H^\bullet_K(Y;f_!\mathcal F)\to\varinjlim_{K\subseteq Y}\check H^\bullet_K(Y;f_!\mathcal F)
\end{equation}
We must show that the second map is an isomorphism; to see this, it suffices to show that the following is an isomorphism for all $K\subseteq Y$:
\begin{equation}\label{supportcompactinXorY}
\varinjlim_{K'\subseteq X\cap K}\check H^\bullet_{K'}(Y;f_!\mathcal F)\to\check H^\bullet_K(Y;f_!\mathcal F)
\end{equation}
We claim that for any \v Cech cochain $\beta$ for $f_!\mathcal F$ with supports in $K$ subordinate to an open cover of $Y$, there is a refinement on which the restriction of $\beta$ has supports in some $K'\subseteq X\cap K$.  It follows from the claim (using a cofinality argument) that \eqref{supportcompactinXorY} is an isomorphism.  To prove the claim, argue as follows.

First, choose a refinement for which only finitely many open sets $U_1,\ldots,U_n$ intersect $K$ and for which the remaining open sets cover $Y\setminus K$.  Pick open sets $V_i\subseteq U_i$ which cover $K$ and for which $\overline{V_i}$ is compact and $\overline{V_i}\subseteq U_i$ (this is always possible).

Now $\beta$ is a finite collection $\{\beta_I\in(f_!\mathcal F)(\bigcap_{i\in I}U_i)\}_{\varnothing\ne I\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}}$.  We have:
\begin{equation}\label{restrictionsupport}
\operatorname{supp}\Bigl(\beta_I|\bigcap_{i\in I}V_i\Bigr)\subseteq\operatorname{supp}\beta_I\cap\bigcap_{i\in I}\overline{V_i}
\end{equation}
Since $\operatorname{supp}\beta_I\subseteq\bigcap_{i\in I}U_i$ is (relatively) closed and $\bigcap_{i\in I}\overline{V_i}\subseteq\bigcap_{i\in I}U_i$ is compact, it follows that the right hand side is compact.  Also, we have $\operatorname{supp}\beta_I\subseteq X$ (by definition of $f_!\mathcal F$).  Hence the right hand side of \eqref{restrictionsupport} is a compact subset of $X$.  It follows that the restriction of $\beta$ to the refinement obtained by replacing $U_i$ with $V_i$ for $i=1,\ldots,n$ has support in a compact subset of $X$.
\end{proof}

