\begin{definition}[Tensor product of homotopy diagrams]\label{simplicialproduct}
Let $\mathcal S$ and $\mathcal T$ be two finite posets, and let $\{A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s'}\}$ and $\{B^\bullet_{\mathfrak t,\mathfrak t'}\}$ be homotopy diagrams over $\mathcal S$ and $\mathcal T$ respectively.  Their tensor product $\{A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s'}\otimes B^\bullet_{\mathfrak t,\mathfrak t'}\}$ is naturally a homotopy diagram over $\mathcal S\times\mathcal T$.  Now there is a natural morphism:
\begin{equation}\label{tensorproductdirectlimitmorphism}
\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{\mathfrak s\preceq\mathfrak s'\in\mathcal S}A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s'}\otimes\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{\mathfrak t\preceq\mathfrak t'\in\mathcal T}B^\bullet_{\mathfrak t,\mathfrak t'}\to\operatornamewithlimits{hocolim}_{\mathfrak s\times\mathfrak t\preceq\mathfrak s'\times\mathfrak t'\in\mathcal S\times\mathcal T}A^\bullet_{\mathfrak s,\mathfrak s'}\otimes B^\bullet_{\mathfrak t,\mathfrak t'}
\end{equation}
To define this morphism, we simply observe that the nerve of $\mathcal S\times\mathcal T$ is the standard simplicial subdivision of the product of the nerves of $\mathcal S$ and $\mathcal T$, and this is covered by a morphism of coefficient systems.\footnote{In fact, from this perspective one easily sees that \eqref{tensorproductdirectlimitmorphism} is always a quasi-isomorphism.}
\end{definition}

